\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar holds ground after U.S. unemployment rises. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 824 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2003\par 23:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 2 (Reuters) - A bleak U.S. jobs report turned anti-climactic for the U.S. dollar on Friday as investors who sold the dollar leading up to the report were proven correct, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  little changed on the day. \par \par \uc2  U.S. unemployment rose for the fourth straight month, but overall job losses were less than expected. However, the manufacturing sector shed jobs for the 33rd straight month, putting the U.S. economy and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under a dark cloud. \par \par \uc2  "The market was so bearish on the dollar and discounting a horrible jobs report so that anything short of a calamity was bound to cause a correction. And you could say the same for bonds and stocks," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics in Essex, Connecticut. \par \par \uc2  "It's all about positioning. Once the dust clears, the market will resume selling dollars," he said. \par \par \uc2  Next week, investors will contend with a wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings in an environment of continued weak global economic growth. No interest rate cuts are expected next week, however meetings in June could prove otherwise. \par \par \uc2  In the latest Reuters poll, only 2 of 22 primary bond dealers on Wall Street expect the U.S. Federal Reserve to cut four-decade-low interest rates. However, 8 of those same 22 dealers expect a rate cut from 1.25 percent in June. \par \par \uc2  The dollar found some support against the yen after a report from Jiji Press said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will call for yen weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of an expected package due next week containing proposals to help stimulate the economy. \par \par \uc2  The proposal would codify the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Japanese authorities have admitted to already this year. \par \par \uc2  Analysts however said the reality of proposals emanating from Tokyo in the past has proven hollow. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe this will be the magic moment for Japan, but historically these packages come with an initial wave of optimism followed by a tsunami of disappointment," said Rebecca Patterson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar spiked to a session high of 119.35 yen as the Jiji story spread through the market, but the lift proved short-lived and the dollar was whittled back to 118.93 yen, a gain of 0.31 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  The euro lost ground on the dollar after the employment report but eventually made back nearly all of its lost ground to trade at $1.1226, down slightly on the day. After reaching a four-year high of $1.1287 on Thursday in anticipation of a weak jobs report, the euro dropped as low as $1.1191 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's partial recovery was more a function that the week is coming to a close and that the euro has made a spectacular rise in a short period of time," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital Markets LLC in New York. \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell to $1.6044, off 0.41 percent. \par \par \uc2  BANKS AND ECONOMICS \par \par \uc2  In the wake of the employment report showing the jobless rate up to 6.0 percent in April from 5.8 percent in March, investors shrugged off the good news for the factory sector which reported orders rose a bigger than expected 2.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the bad jobs report did not shake economists into thinking the Federal Reserve would trim rates further, as the Reuters poll showed, at its meeting on May 6. \par \par \uc2  Likewise, poor economic forecasts for Europe are not likely at this point to push the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trim its interest rates on May 8 from 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  However, the head of the influential German Ifo institute said the ECB should stop worrying about inflation and instead cut interest rates to revive the stalled European economy. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England holds a two-day policy meeting on May 6 and May 7. The Reuters survey showed 24 of 32 economists predict the bank will trim the benchmark repo rate of 3.75 percent by 25 basis points. But the economists assigned only a 55 percent chance the Monetary Policy Committee would move. \par \par \uc2  "If you did get an easing in the United States and Europe, we think more than affecting euro/dollar or sterling, it would simply widen the differentials with Australia and Canada and extend those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the core majors," said J.P. Morgan Chase's Patterson. \par \par \uc2  The greenback pulled up slightly from Thursday's five-year low of C$1.4158 against the Canadian dollar, trading at $1.4188, a slight loss on the day. The Australian dollar made a fresh three-year high of $0.6343 before being trimmed back to $0.6310. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark overnight interest rate of 3.25 percent is expected to go higher, while Australia's benchmark rate of 4.75 percent offers a hefty premium over U.S. rates. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030502dz52013oa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
