\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Gets Brief Respite Vs Majors Pre-Holiday \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2 887 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2003\par 08:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Downward pressure on the dollar let up temporarily ahead of Japan's three-day weekend, helping it remain within established trading ranges against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Given all the excitement in New York overnight, Tokyo players were at a loss as to how to play the market today, especially with the weekend approaching," said Minoru Shioiri, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Mitsubishi Securities. Tokyo markets will be closed Monday for a national holiday. \par \par \uc2  While most market players see room for more dollar weakness down the line, Friday's slight pause in dollar selling kept the euro trading quietly below $1.1250 for the Asian session, preventing a repeat performance of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's overnight leap to fresh four-year highs above $1.1280. \par \par \uc2  The dollar managed to regain some lost ground against the yen on profit taking and on U.S. President George W. Bush's speech officially declaring that "major combat \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Iraq have ended." \par \par \uc2  But the reaction on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to the speech was extremely small and overall dollar/yen movements remained sluggish given the market's general lack of interest in buying the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's not immune from the current weak-dollar trend, but there's no reason to actively buy the yen either," said Mitsubishi's Shioiri, citing rock-bottom Japanese interest rates, dismal Japanese economic fundamentals and fears that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry will quietly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yen from rising too far. \par \par \uc2  Rather, Japanese investors are shifting their focus to higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Australian and Canadian dollar, which have continued to post strong gains against the yen, as well as the greenback, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Funds are flowing to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with high yields as investors' risk appetites are recovering," said Satoshi Tate, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at UFJ Bank. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar jumped some 30 sen against the yen Friday morning to a peak of Y75.32, its highest level against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in three and a half years. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely continue posting gains given that it's likely the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep interest rates steady and that the Australian economy is likely to outperform the sluggish world economy. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar also continued to rack up gains against the yen, rising to Y83.72, its strongest level in just over a year. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y118.78, above Y118.55 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at Y118.75 late Thursday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1238, little changed from $1.1239 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.1196 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y133.48, up from Y133.24 in New York late Thursday. The euro was at Y132.95 in Tokyo late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is likely to remain under pressure against its major rivals, especially the euro, as "the consensus view remains that any post war bounce in the U.S. economy will be insufficient to attract enough foreign capital to fund the U.S. current account deficit at present exchange rates," said UBS Warburg in a weekly research note. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's downside risks against the yen will likely be limited due to concerns over North Korea's nuclear program and the spread of SARS, said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "The market still underestimates" the threat to the yen from North Korea and SARS related woes, he said, pointing out that Japan's geographical proximity to North Korea makes it vulnerable to Pyongyang's ballistic missiles. \par \par \uc2  SARS is also taking its toll on China, which is Japan's second largest export partner. A protracted SARS outbreak "could lead to a slowdown of Japan's industrial production and shrink the nation's trade surplus," Umemoto said. \par \par \uc2  Another reason not to buy yen is Japan's Finance Ministry, which reminded markets again on Friday that it will act against excessive strengthening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If there are large moves or they are too excessive, we will take appropriate action," Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi said. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan data released earlier this week showed the Finance Ministry didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets during April - the first month this year that it hasn't sold yen. \par \par \uc2  But traders believe that if the dollar continues to head south, the government won't hesitate to resume its stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many in the market expect the ministry to step in below Y117.50. \par \par \uc2  In late Tokyo Friday, sterling was at $1.6096, down from $1.6100 in New York but higher than $1.6027 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Bennett Richardson\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates that accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture that is updated\par hourly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Subscribers can find this table:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030502dz52000d1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
