\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slumps on data, euro hits new 4-year high. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 802 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 2003\par 18:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 1 (Reuters) - The dollar slumped, hitting fresh four-year troughs against the euro and new session lows against some other major rivals on Thursday, after a major U.S. manufacturing report deepened the sense of gloom surrounding the world's biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  The Institute for Supply Management said its purchasing management index fell more than expected in April, as the contraction in manufacturing continued. \par \par \uc2  In a swift reaction to the report the euro shot well above $1.1200, reaching $1.1287 , its highest levels against the dollar since February 1999. \par \par \uc2  "The ISM number wasn't all that bad, but it continues a string of disappointments on the U.S. economy and everyone is very bearish the dollar," said John Beerling, chief dealer at Wells Fargo in Minneapolis. \par \par \uc2  Some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said the euro's break of $1.1200 and subsequently of $1.1250 took out a series of options positions at these levels, paving the way for the euro prospectively to reach above $1.1300. \par \par \uc2  SPECULATION ABOUT POOR JOBS DATA \par \par \uc2  "What we are seeing is speculative trading ahead of the expected poor April U.S. employment report tomorrow. Because the report is expected to show continued weakness in the U.S. economy, this is going to be a dollar negative," said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with The Bank of New York. \par \par \uc2  Economists' consensus forecasts are for the jobless rate to rise to 5.9 percent in April from 5.8 percent in March. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is definitely on the ropes again," said Jes Black, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with MG Financial Group in New York. \par \par \uc2  U.S. weekly initial jobless claims and productivity data released this morning dealt the greenback a muted blow. Claims fell to 448,000 in the week ending April 26, but were higher than expected. U.S. productivity rose 1.6 percent in the first quarter, slower than the expected 2.1 percent. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Around noon Thursday in New York, the dollar was still down but off\par session lows. The dollar was trading at 118.49 yen , down 0.28 percent.\par Against the Swiss franc  the dollar was at 1.3422, down 0.90 percent.\par Against the single European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar was down 0.55 percent,\par with the euro        at $1.1243.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Broadly, the dollar is just getting beat up," said Michael Cairns,\par trading manager with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Solutions in Ridgewood, New Jersey. With the\par Fed funds rate scraping a four-decade low of 1.25 percent, real rates\par of return in the U.S. are negative once inflation is taken into account\par and that is driving a persistent, powerful flow out of the dollar and\par into higher-yielding fixed income elsewhere, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DOUBTS ABOUT U.S. ASSETS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Doubts over the relative appeal of U.S. assets were also fueled by\par Federal Reserve chief Alan Greenspan in testimony to Congress on\par Wednesday, when he signaled a note of caution over economic recovery.\par He warned that further substantial disinflation would be an unwelcome\par development and reiterated there was clearly room to cut interest rates\par again if needed. The Fed's policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee\par next meets on May 6.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Aside from the euro, the higher-yielding "commodity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " were\par among the chief beneficiaries of shifts out of dollars Thursday,\par traders said. These include the Canadian, Australian and New Zealand\par dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar rose to multiyear highs against its U.S.\par counterpart, also helped by the Bank of Canada's signal it is prepared\par to continue raising interest rates and by the lifting of the World\par Health Organization's travel advisory restrictions on Canada because of\par Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Around noon Thursday in New York, the U.S. dollar was buying C$1.4181,\par down 1.0 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Japan, where short-term interest rates are effectively at zero, the\par Bank of Japan said Wednesday it would push more cash into the financial\par system and broaden the range of assets accepted as collateral.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent gains against the dollar have raised\par nervousness that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again to stem the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par export-damaging strength.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Thursday that\par authorities were watching the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market carefully and\par would take action if needed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Japan - which acts on behalf of Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in the event of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - did not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in April, according to official data. But\par as the dollar has slipped back well below 119.0 yen, some traders are\par starting to prepare for the possibility the MOF may engage again in\par yen-weakening efforts to protect the country's exporters, as it did\par earlier this year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030501dz5100thb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
