\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar succumbs to empowered euro, hits 4-year low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 763 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 2003\par 13:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 1 (Reuters) - The ailing dollar staggered to a new four-year low on the euro and set a one-month low versus the yen on Thursday as a deteriorating chart outlook and upcoming U.S. data releases gave investors new reasons for concern. \par \par \uc2  The greenback slid within a whisker of $1.12 to the euro and below 118.50 yen in early trade after a dismal regional U.S. manufacturing report on Wednesday raised worries that a nationwide survey later in the day would also be disappointing. \par \par \uc2  Selling pressure intensified after the break of the key 97 level in the dollar index in Asia, but the greenback stabilised in the European session, with most European financial centres closed for the May Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  "The market will be relatively quiet ahead of the data and it looks like euro/dollar is facing quite a lot of resistance around $1.12," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London. \par \par \uc2  "But liquidity is fairly thin because of the holiday in most of Europe so we could see exaggerated movements after the data and that could trigger a break above $1.12," he said. \par \par \uc2  Investors hungry for clues on when the world's biggest economy may pick up were eagerly awaiting the Institute for Supply Management's report on factory business conditions at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Analysts recently polled by Reuters expected the ISM index to have ticked up to 47.3 in April from 46.2 in March. However, these forecasts were made before Wednesday's unexpected decline in a report from Chicago-area purchasing managers. \par \par \uc2  A number below 50 reflects a shrinking manufacturing sector. \par \par \uc2  Latest U.S. Jobless claims data and first-quarter non-farm productivity is due at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  DEEP WOUNDS \par \par \uc2  The dollar has shed six cents against the euro in the past four weeks, with many analysts blaming a shift out of low-yielding U.S. bonds into higher-yielding euro-denominated assets and a flurry of weak economic data from the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "Investors continue to seek higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the dollar is a low yielder," said Osman Wahid, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase. "The ISM will be interesting and the risk is on the downside." \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro hovered around half a percent below Wednesday's four-year high of 133.33 yen. Against the Swiss franc, the euro rose for a fifth consecutive session to hit 1.5174, its highest since September 10, 2001. \par \par \uc2  While the yen has weakened against the euro, its recent gains against the dollar have raised nervousness that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-damaging strength. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Thursday that authorities were watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully and would take action if needed. \par \par \uc2  FALLING RATES \par \par \uc2  Doubts over the relative appeal of U.S. assets were also fuelled by Federal Reserve chief Alan Greenspan who, in a testimony to Congress on Wednesday, signalled a note of caution over economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan said the U.S. economy seemed poised for a rebound with the end of the Iraq war. But he cautioned on business confidence, warning that further substantial disinflation would be an unwelcome development and reiterated there was clearly room to cut interest rates again if needed. \par \par \uc2  It was the cautious elements of his comments which raised expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might soon start cutting rates again, if not at its policy meeting next week. \par \par \uc2  A further fall in U.S. rates is likely to be damaging for the dollar in a market where investors reward \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with higher yields. It could also raise dollar selling pressure since lower interest rates cheapen the cost of hedging. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, a Reuters poll of 32 economists showed 24 of them expecting the Bank of England to trim a quarter point off interest rates at its May 7-8 meeting. \par \par \uc2  Eight analysts said the BoE would hold borrowing costs at 3.50 percent despite a weak global outlook and slowing domestic demand in the UK. \par \par \uc2  British retail sales did show signs of improvement in April, growing at the fastest pace in five months thanks to warm weather and the late timing of Easter this year, data showed. \par \par \uc2  In reaction to the numbers, the British pound edged up against the euro and the dollar, setting a seven-week high versus the greenback and coming off four-year lows on the strong single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030501dz5100k7y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
