\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro hits 4-year peak vs dollar after US data, Fed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 2003\par 10:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 1 (Reuters) - The euro extended its meteoric ascent to a fresh four-year peak on the dollar on Thursday, with the U.S. unit undermined by poor regional manufacturing data the previous day and caution before national business indicators later. \par \par \uc2  The Chicago purchasing management index fell on Wednesday, confounding expectations of a bounce. Federal Reserve chief Alan Greenspan, in his testimony to Congress on Wednesday, signalled a greater chance for future rate cuts, all of which fanned doubts whether investing in the U.S. offers better returns. \par \par \uc2  Markets are now focused on the U.S. Institute for Supply Management's report on factory business conditions due later, forecast at 47.3 in April compared with 46.2 in March. \par \par \uc2  "Greenspan's testimony didn't allay fears about the U.S. economy which have built up recently and the Chicago PMI didn't help the dollar either. Investors continue to seek higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the dollar is a low yielder," said Osman Wahid, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase. \par \par \uc2  "The ISM will be interesting and the risk is on the downside. The view is still mixed and there are a number of economic data coming up before the judgement on the economy is made." \par \par \uc2  By 0755 GMT, the euro had risen to $1.1198, its strongest since February 1999. The euro also rose to a four-year high against sterling just short of 70 pence. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro stayed close to Wednesday's four-year high of 133.35 yen. Against the Swiss franc, the euro rose for a fifth consecutive session to hit 1.5174, its highest since September 10, 2001. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also fell to a one-month low of 118.59 yen. \par \par \uc2  Most European markets were shut for the May Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  EASING BIAS? \par \par \uc2  Greenspan said the U.S. economy seemed poised for a rebound with the end of the Iraq war. But he cautioned on business confidence, warning that further substantial disinflation would be an unwelcome development and reiterated there was clearly room to cut interest rates again if needed. \par \par \uc2  It was the cautious elements of his comments which raised expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might soon start cutting rates again, if not at its policy meeting next week. \par \par \uc2  "There's a growing possibility that the Fed will ease monetary policy given Greenspan's comments, in which he expressed caution on the economic outlook," saod Kouki Muroi, deputy manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Aozora Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  A further fall in U.S. rates is likely to be damaging for the dollar in a market where investors reward \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with higher yields. It could also raise dollar selling pressure since lower interest rates cheapen the cost of hedging. \par \par \uc2  "People feel the economy needs more so there are expectations for a rate cut in the future," Wahid said. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN THREAT HELPS \par \par \uc2  The dollar struggled below 119 yen with some caution of possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Thursday that authorities were watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully and would take action if needed. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the dollar would fall sharply against the yen because there is no clear reason to buy the yen actively now and also the market is nervous about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities," said Takashi Toyahara, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  section manager at Nomura Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030501dz5100e7a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
