\par\par \b \uc2  Options Market Points To Continued Euro Strength \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2003\par 21:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The euro's break of strong resistance under $1.11 has shaken up volatilities in the options market, signaling continued strength for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Having tried for several days to shatter long-standing resistance above $1.1084, the euro made a decisive break Wednesday and is continuing to trade well above $1.11 for the first time in four years. \par \par \uc2  The move helped volatilities spike in euro options versus the dollar, with one-month and three-month risk reversals indicating more investors desiring to buy the euro rather than sell it. \par \par \uc2  "There is a risk-reversal favor for euro calls, more so now than a couple of days ago," said Robert Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with BNP Paribas in New York. "We view that as a coincidental indicator that the market sees the euro going higher and feeds into expectation that it will." \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money euro/dollar option volatilities had climbed to 9.90%-10.20%, up from 9.65%-9.95% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen option volatilities held relatively steady, at 8.40%-8.70% as opposed to 8.45%-8.65% in Tokyo, with quiet trade in Japan as the nation is in the midst of the annual string of holidays known as Golden Week. The May 1 holiday that will keep markets closed in Hong Kong, Singapore and much of Europe also helped to damp activity, traders said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over\par the period of the contract. Volatilities on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options gauge the\par anticipated moves in the underlying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with a higher volatility\par implying a wider trading range.\par    In afternoon trading, the triggering of stop-loss orders   pushed\par the dollar down as far as Y118.66, its lowest level in about a month.\par However, the greenback made a quick partial recovery back to around\par Y119.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If the dollar/yen rate remained depressed, verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would become likely, analysts said. The MOF\par and the Bank of Japan have a long history of combatting what the\par Japanese government considers to be excessive yen strength that would\par endanger the competitiveness of the country's exports in the world\par market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Risk reversals, which measure the premium investors are willing to pay\par for the right to buy over the right to sell, are now slated heavily in\par favor of the euro both in the near- and long-term, with euro calls at\par 0.60%-0.90% today in New York marking a sharp upswing from 0.50%-0.70%\par yesterday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We have a bias on euro calls all the way out the curve," said Jim\par Kampenhoefner, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options strategist with Bank of America\par Securities in San Francisco. "The curve is basically at a flat level,\par which means volatilities are not much different at the back or front"\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Strategists said that although the euro seems to have settled above\par the $1.1100 range,  significant barriers exist at the $1.1200 and\par $1.1300 levels. That they don't expect the single European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par fall significantly backwards, though, could spell trouble for the\par dollar, which faces potentially negative jobless numbers later in the\par week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday afternoon, the dollar was trading at Y118.95, down from\par Y119.83 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.1178, up\par sharply from $1.1067 late Tuesday, but off its high from earlier in the\par global day around $1.1186. The dollar was trading at CHF1.3547, down\par from CHF1.3622 late Tuesday, while sterling was at $1.6014, up compared\par with $1.5941 Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Michael Deibert, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2248;\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par michael.deibert@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030430dz4u0019e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
