\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar recovers tentatively on mixed data signals. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2003\par 00:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, April 17 (Reuters) - The dollar turned a retreat into a modest advance on Thursday, recovering from initial losses after a regional U.S. manufacturing report did not prove as dismal as expected. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar's gains, eked out in thin holiday season trade ahead of the closure of many global financial markets Friday, were very tentative, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Many of the world's financial markets will be shut on Friday for the Easter holiday. U.S. markets resume trading on Monday, but other centers, including London, will be closed on Monday as well. \par \par \uc2  "We are rangebound, but the tone is weaker for the dollar, because the bias appears to be toward U.S. economic weakness," said Thomas Sowanick, chief global fixed-income strategist with Merrill Lynch in New York. \par \par \uc2  Early in the New York session, the greenback came under downward pressure from a clear indication of labor market weakness contained in a volatile weekly report. \par \par \uc2  Jobless claims data showed first-time claims jumped 30,000 to a seasonally adjusted 442,000 for the week ended April 12, the ninth straight week above the 400,000 level, which economists regard as unhealthy for the economy. Economists had expected new claims of 411,000. \par \par \uc2  Until its modest rally on Thursday, the dollar had fallen against the euro every day this week as traders shrugged off upbeat earnings from some of America's top companies and instead worried about the country's long-term economic prospects. \par \par \uc2  But Thursday the greenback took heart from the Philadelphia Federal Reserve's report that its manufacturing index fell to a stronger-than-expected -8.8 in April from -8 in March. \par \par \uc2  "Some of the whispers were for a number as low as -25, so there was a bias for a worse number than -8.8 and therefore it helped the dollar a bit," said Grant Wilson, vice president at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh. \par \par \uc2  An equity market rally also helped underpin the dollar somewhat. The Dow Jones industrial average rose 1.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday afternoon in New York, the dollar was trading at 119.60 yen, a touch higher than 119.54 yen late Wednesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar rose 0.8 percent to 1.3816 francs. The euro slipped below the $1.0900 mark, buying $1.0873, down 0.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  That left the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovering at a key area between $1.0880 and $1.0890. Should the euro lose touch with that zone, the next technically important level down would be around $1.0830, just below which there are some big stop-loss orders, said Thomas Rogers, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst for IFR \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Watch, Thomson Financial in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar will probably trade in a near term range between 121.0 yen and 119.0 yen through Tuesday or Wednesday of next week, but if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should slip below 117.50 yen, that would increase the chances of intensified rhetoric from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at weakening the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Andrew Chaveriat, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  technical analyst with BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  Japan's government is aiming to nurture any signs of export-led growth in the world's second biggest economy by helping Japanese companies sell goods competitively abroad. \par \par \uc2  In the event of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  acts on behalf of the MOF, as occurred earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  Investment flow data showed why both the euro and, to a lesser degree, the dollar have been supported versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  According to Finance Ministry data, Japanese investors bought a net 754.9 billion yen ($6.31 billion) of foreign bonds during the April 7-11 week after selling a net 49.3 billion yen the previous week. \par \par \uc2  In March, they made their biggest purchases of foreign bonds in three months, buying a net 1.6479 trillion yen after a net purchase of 1.1654 trillion yen in February. This demand for higher-yielding foreign bonds has helped fuel the rally of the euro against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro tested four-year highs against the yen around 130.80 briefly in Asian trade on Wednesday. Late Thursday in New York, it was trading at 130.09 yen, down 0.27 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030417dz4h015o1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
