\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Ex-MOF Kuroda: \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Could Ease Deflation \b0\par\par\uc2 211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2003\par 04:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could ease deflation, but forcing the yen lower is difficult due to the likelihood of a backlash from the nation's trading partners, a former senior Finance Ministry official said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "In principle, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at ensuring stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," Haruhiko Kuroda, former vice finance minister for international affairs, said at a seminar on deflation. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry has admitted to selling about Y2.4 trillion this year to cap yen strength, but denies it is trying to drive the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda also said he would like to see further monetary easing, which might lead to a weaker yen, thus helping to soothe the deflation that has seen prices in Japan fall for more than three years. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said the Bank of Japan needs to expand the range of assets it buys in order to stem deflation. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently considering buying asset-backed commercial paper backed by company receivables to help smaller firms gain access to finance. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda left the Finance Ministry earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Alastair Gale and Kirsty Mackenzie \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030416dz4g00042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picsca