\par\par \b \uc2  IMF-Japan needs agressive steps to beat deflation. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anna Willard \par \uc2 590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2003\par 17:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, April 9 (Reuters) - Japan must take urgent and aggressive steps to fight deflation, restructure its banking and corporate sectors and get its economy back on track, the International Monetary Fund said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The fund said economic growth in Japan is likely to remain weak, with gross domestic product seen rising just 0.8 percent this year, and 1.0 percent in 2004. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's entrenched deflation and weak economic prospects underline the urgency of taking aggressive action to reestablish positive inflation," the IMF said in its semi-annual World Economic Outlook report. \par \par \uc2  In a gloomy assessment of the Japanese economy, the IMF found recent measures by the Bank of Japan were not sufficient to turn the economy around, and urged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to show it is ready to do whatever is necessary to buck the deflationary trend. \par \par \uc2  The fund said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should also signal it will target a sufficiently high inflation rate over the medium-term to minimize the risk of deflation. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the Bank of Japan rejected government calls for a loosening of its existing monetary policy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its Policy Board decided to leave unchanged the volume target for its "quantitative easing" policy. \par \par \uc2  Prices of goods and services in Japan have fallen for four consecutive years and the IMF said there is evidence deflationary expectations are getting worse. \par \par \uc2  The fund also said a sharp drop in the yen would be manageable for the region. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to try and weaken the yen against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and make its exports more competitive. \par \par \uc2  RISKS TO THE DOWNSIDE \par \par \uc2  The IMF said there are downside risks to the already weak outlook for Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  A protracted war in Iraq - a scenario that looked unlikely on Wednesday as Saddam Hussein's rule crumbled in Baghdad - could hurt growth through higher oil prices, falling equities and weaker consumer and investor confidence. Japan is especially vulnerable because of its fragile economy and weak financial institutions, it said. \par \par \uc2  The biggest risk is any potential worsening of banking problems, the IMF said. This could be prompted by further falls in equity prices or mismanaged efforts to strengthen banks, which are struggling under a mountain of bad loans. \par \par \uc2  "Another risk would be a credit crunch, as some banks may curtail lending in attempts to improve their capital adequacy ratios," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  BANKS NEED WORK \par \par \uc2  The IMF welcomed the government's plan, announced in October 2002, to strengthen banks but also said: "However, more needs to be done to resolve banking problems." \par \par \uc2  Banks should provision better against bad loans, the government should amend laws to facilitate injecting public funds into weak banks, and smaller and regional banks should be covered in the government's plan to strengthen banks, it said. \par \par \uc2  Sorting out the banking sector is essential for speeding up restructuring in the corporate sector, the IMF said, but warned against too much government involvement in corporate restructuring. \par \par \uc2  The authorities should also undertake reforms in the labor market, which may push up unemployment in the short term so a social safety net is also needed as a cushion against job losses, the fund said. \par \par \uc2  Unless the government embarks on ambitious restructuring, Japan should take on gradual fiscal consolidation to reign in the country's high and growing public debt, the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  Measures are also needed to strengthen the tax base such as lowering the income tax threshold and reducing deductions. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030409dz4900vmy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
