\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Gov Fukui to debut at G7 in harsh spotlight. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 812 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2003\par 09:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 8 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui faces his first international test at a Group of Seven meeting this week, with financial authorities keen to hear how he will tackle deflation in the world's second-largest economy. \par \par \uc2  Fukui, a former deputy chief of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is no stranger to the world stage but as the new BOJ chief, his every word will be closely watched. \par \par \uc2  "The biggest concern among other nations is Japan's deflation problem and there are high expectations of the new BOJ chief," said Takatoshi Kato, a former vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  "I think Governor Fukui strongly feels a need to open a dialogue with the G7." \par \par \uc2  Finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the G7 - the United States, Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy and Japan - meet on Friday and Saturday in Washington ahead of semiannual meetings of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank. \par \par \uc2  HIT THE GROUND RUNNING \par \par \uc2  The diminutive, white-haired governor has been portrayed as a conservative but has hit the ground running. \par \par \uc2  He held an emergency policy-setting meeting shortly after his appointment last month where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to boost the amount of stocks it buys directly from commercial banks, and consider improving the efficacy of its policy framework. \par \par \uc2  At its latest meeting on Tuesday, the BOJ's Policy Board decided to examine the possibility of temporarily buying asset-backed securities, mainly from small and midsize firms, to get money flowing beyond the paralysed banking system. \par \par \uc2  Talks with the United States will likely focus on ways to resolve the persistent price deflation that plagues Japan's sluggish economy and the struggle to deal with massive non-performing loans. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury Department's undersecretary for international affairs, John Taylor, told Reuters last week that he wanted to focus on resolving price falls and the problem of the banks' non-performing loans. \par \par \uc2  "So our focus has been more on monetary policy, banking policy, than on particular discussions with them on exchange rates," Taylor said. \par \par \uc2  STEALTH \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Such comments indicate that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to get any flak about its recent forays into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the dollar sagged against the yen on concerns over the war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo has spent some 2.4 trillion yen ($20 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year in a bid to preempt the yen's rise, which is a negative for the nation's exporters. \par \par \uc2  Taylor said he did not dispute the Japanese explanation for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They have indicated their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to do with keeping the markets orderly and they've expressed exactly the same thing to us in private," he said. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, a former top financial diplomat dubbed "Mr Yen" for his sway over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said: "The United States will allow \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yen rising but not to drive the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when there is a sudden fall in the dollar at the 115-120 yen level is acceptable but they are likely to be negative about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 120 yen," Sakakibara added. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Tuesday that he did not expect \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or oil prices to be major topics of discussion, given that markets were relatively calm. \par \par \uc2  A senior Finance Ministry official said the G7 would likely reiterate its intention to continue to closely monitor the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and cooperate as appropriate. \par \par \uc2  PAYING FOR IRAQ RECONSTRUCTION \par \par \uc2  One impending hot topic will be funding for Iraqi reconstruction. \par \par \uc2  Japan was a major donor in the last Gulf War, when it pitched in some $11 billion. \par \par \uc2  But government officials have indicated that Japan, after a decade of economic stagnation and stuck with a ballooning fiscal deficit, may now find it difficult to provide funding on that scale. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa has said he would like to focus on humanitarian aid and indicated a cautious stance on other areas such as assistance for neighbouring states, reconstructing Iraq itself and aid through international financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  "Japan should play an active role for that (humanitarian aid) but as for the other issues, we have to watch developments in the war and see the framework for post-war reconstruction," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japan has already decided to provide some $110 million in aid for refugees and for some neighbouring countries such as Jordan. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said last week he expected the G7 finance ministers to spend considerable time talking about how Iraqi reconstruction might be directed and how costs might be shared among them. \par \par \uc2  But the topic may be fraught with controversy as U.S. officials have ruled out a key political mission for the United Nations while Britain, other European nations and Japan want the Security Council to endorse the post-war process. ($1=119.86 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030408dz4800eda\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
