\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slips against yen as Iraq optimism wanes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yonggi Kang \par \uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2003\par 08:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 3 (Reuters) - The dollar fell slightly in Asia on Thursday as initial optimism over the advance by U.S.-led forces to the outskirts of Baghdad waned in a market constantly looking over its shoulder for the next development in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had soared against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday when the fast pace of the march toward the Iraqi capital suggested to some players that the war could end soon. \par \par \uc2  U.S. forces thrust towards Baghdad, smashing two of Iraq's elite Republican Guard divisions, and the Pentagon said the U.S.-led coalition was now "threatening the core of the regime" of President Saddam Hussein. \par \par \uc2  But market players were concerned that Iraq may use chemical weapons now that U.S. troops were inside the so-called "red line", the area where the U.S. military says Iraqi forces might be most likely to launch a poison-gas attack. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen traded lower from a morning high of 119.13 to 118.63 as the rumour circulated in the market that Iraq could use chemical weapons," Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley, said in a note to clients. \par \par \uc2  As of 0604 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 118.68/74 yen down from 118.91/99 yen in late U.S. trade. But it was still well above the low of 117.60 hit on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro meanwhile pared earlier losses against the dollar, leaving it at $1.0766/71 from the day's low at 1.0734 and barely changed from $1.0772/77 in late U.S. trade. On Wednesday, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had climbed to a peak $1.0960. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was trading at 127.78/83 yen versus 128.16 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR SEESAWS \par \par \uc2  The dollar has seesawed since the start of the war on March 20 as the market remained preoccupied with headlines reporting the progress of the battle in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  But the uncertainties swirling around the duration of the war have kept the greenback locked in a range between 117 to 121 yen. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of defence and Middle East experts published on Wednesday showed they expected U.S.-led forces to gain control of Baghdad within four to eight weeks. \par \par \uc2  "The market clearly lacks direction," said a trader at a major Japanese bank. On the downside, the dollar was aided against the yen by wariness about further yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some traders suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  from late Tuesday to Wednesday to stem the yen's gains, which could hinder the nation's exports. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Wednesday that Japanese authorities were ready to back up verbal warnings against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations with action. \par \par \uc2  Some traders were also worried about recent signs of a decline in the already struggling U.S. economy, even though economic fundamentals have taken a back seat in the market because of the war. \par \par \uc2  Thursday will see the release of U.S. data including weekly jobless claims and the Institute for Supply Management's (ISM) non-manufacturing index for March, a gauge of the health of the U.S. service sector. \par \par \uc2  Economists surveyed by Reuters expect that jobless claims rose to 410,000 last week from 402,000 a week earlier. \par \par \uc2  The ISM's non-manufacturing index for March is expected to slip to 52.3 from 53.9 in February. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold a policy meeting later in the day, though most economists expect the bank to leave rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030403dz4300ba7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
