\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar supported, focus on war developments. \b0\par\par\uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2003\par 01:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 3 (Reuters) - The dollar was marginally higher in early Asian trade on Thursday with all attention on the war front in Iraq after U.S forces closed in on Baghdad. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had risen against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday when the advance of U.S.-led forces towards Baghdad suggested to some that the war could end soon, but views on the outlook for the war are changing all the time and many players remain wary of betting on a quick U.S. victory. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was thus likely to be range-bound, unless there were fresh significant developments in Iraq, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  As of 2315 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 119.02/07 yen , slightly above 118.91/99 yen in late U.S. level but below Wednesday's high of 119.25. \par \par \uc2  "I think the dollar is unlikely to break above Wednesday's high, unless there is a new development in Iraq," said a dealer at another Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  The euro eased to $1.0754/59 , around Wednesday's low of $1.0756 and a shade below $1.0772/77 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  It was changing hands at at 127.98/10 yen  versus 128.16 yen in late U.S. trade. The dollar was aided against the yen by concern over further yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said the Japanese authorities were ready to back up their verbal warnings against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations with action. \par \par \uc2  With the focus on Iraq, the dollar was unperturbed by a weak U.S. factory orders report on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Factory orders tumbled 1.5 percent in February, more than the 0.6 percent fall expected by economists. January's rise in orders was revised down to 1.7 percent from 2.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's U.S. data include weekly jobless claims and the Institute for Supply Management's (ISM) non-manufacturing index for March, a gauge of the health of the U.S. service sector. \par \par \uc2  Jobless claims are expected to have risen to 410,000 last week from 402,000 a week earlier. \par \par \uc2  The ISM's non-manufacturing index is expected to slip to 52.3 in March from 53.9 in February. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is holding a policy meeting, though most economists expect the bank to leave rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030402dz42018gd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
