\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar soars as U.S. forces move in on Baghdad. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2003\par 00:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, April 2 (Reuters) - The dollar soared on Wednesday as the advance of U.S.-led forces toward Baghdad raised speculation the war against Iraq was starting to progress more rapidly. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose about one and a third percent against the euro and almost 2 percent against the safe-haven Swiss franc, more than erasing the losses suffered at the start of this week when the concern was that the war might drag on. \par \par \uc2  With views on the outlook for the nearly two-week old war changing by the day, and sometimes by the minute, dealers said they were wary of calling the day's sharp move a change in direction for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The thinking is that all of a sudden the U.S. is winning the war again. And if tomorrow something bad happens, it will be losing the war, and the dollar will give up its gains," said Grant Wilson, vice president at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh. \par \par \uc2  But for Wednesday at least, the signs that U.S. forces were close to Baghdad bolstered the dollar and stocks while pushing down gold and oil prices. \par \par \uc2  "It's a big day for everybody, both the dollar and equities and U.S. forces especially," said Andrew Weiss, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at AIG Trading in Connecticut. Blue chip stocks rallied 2.7 percent while tech stocks were up even more. \par \par \uc2  But Weiss said the stiff resistance U.S.-led forces have encountered in other parts of Iraq meant that success for the U.S. in Baghdad was far from assured. \par \par \uc2  "We'll have to be cautiously optimistic they can rack up gains similar to what we've seen in the last 24 hours, but it would be premature to jump to the conclusion that Baghdad will fall imminently," he said. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of defense and Middle East experts published on Wednesday showed they expected U.S. troops to take four to eight weeks to gain control of Baghdad. \par \par \uc2  SWISS RATE CUT GIVES DOLLAR ADDED BOOST \par \par \uc2  In late New York trading, the dollar was at $1.0764 per euro , having flown through key technical levels at $1.0800 and $1.0780. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was up 1.88 percent at 1.3792 Swiss francs , helped by the Swiss National Bank's decision to cut its repo rate 2 basis points to 0.11 percent. \par \par \uc2  With interest rates near zero, Swiss authorities have pledged to dampen the ascent of the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has risen as a result of safe haven flows. \par \par \uc2  The franc also lost ground against the euro, slipping to one-year lows at 1.4854 francs per euro . \par \par \uc2  "The Swiss rate cut helped push the Swiss to its weakest level against the euro in quite some time. That also helped ... drag the dollar up (against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Barings Capital Markets in New York. "It was the coming together of several events that helped produce the dollar rally." \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose 0.77 percent to 118.92 yen . \par \par \uc2  The dollar was aided against the yen by concerns over further yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Japanese authorities were ready to back up their verbal warnings against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations with action. \par \par \uc2  MARKET UNBOTHERED BY DATA \par \par \uc2  With the focus on Iraq, the dollar was unperturbed by a weak U.S. factory orders report. \par \par \uc2  Factory orders tumbled 1.5 percent in February, more than the 0.6 percent fall expected by economists. January's rise in orders was revised down to 1.7 percent from the 2.1 percent originally reported. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's U.S. data include weekly jobless claims and the Institute for Supply Management's non-manufacturing index for March, a gauge of the health of the U.S. service sector. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the highlight is a meeting of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though most economists expect the bank to stand pat on rates. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said economic developments would continue to take a backseat to news on Iraq, for now. \par \par \uc2  "If the war does end well and there does seem to be a renewed bit of confidence in the U.S., the dollar might be in good stead. But eventually we will have to go back to the old dynamics of capital flows and the U.S. current account deficit," said Mellon Bank's Wilson. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030402dz42016ai\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
