\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Ends Lower as Market Braces for Extended War \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Deibert \par \uc2 846 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2003\par 00:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar fell against its major rivals Monday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market braced for an extended war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  With coalition bombers pounding Republican Guard positions outside Baghdad and heavy fighting continuing in the south and central regions of the country, expectations for a quick and clean for the U.S.-led coalition have all but disappeared, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There's a lot of uncertainty right now and the market is pricing in that risk," said Robert Lynch, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  The intensified bombing was accompanied by vows by  coalition leaders not to turn back from their drive to oust Iraqi leader Saddam Hussein. While virtually no one doubts the ability of the U.S. and United Kingdom to defeat Saddam's troops in open battle, the coalition's campaign isn't looking like the cakewalk which some of the most optimistic scenarios had predicted. \par \par \uc2  Instead, the war may still be in an early phase, setting the stage for the battle for Baghdad to begin in earnest in the coming days. Concern is also mounting that the war may prove more costly to the coalition than anticipated, both in terms of combat casualties and long-term harm to U.S. relations with countries that opposed the attack on Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "When we get some more clarity about the conclusion of the war, that will signal the next move for the dollar," said Grant Wilson, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh. "The phase we're in now is watching how its going to end and when its going to end, but the markets are not looking kindly on events we can't quantify." \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, the dollar was at 117.92 yen, down sharply from 119.71 yen late Friday in New York and falling below 118 yen for the first time in three weeks. The euro was at $1.0926, its high for the global session and up from $1.0779 late Friday. Against the Swiss franc - a classic refuge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in times of military conflict - the dollar was at 1.3501 francs, down from 1.3696 francs. Sterling was at $1.5813, up from $1.5752 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  In tandem with the dollar's fall, other key global asset markets reflected a marked rise in risk aversion. Gold rose almost $6 to $335.90 per ounce and crude-oil futures rose nearly a dollar on the session to $31.20 a barrel, an additional reflection of worries about supply. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 153.64 points, or 1.9%, to 7992.13, as 28 of its 30 components lost ground. The Standard & Poor's 500 Index shed 15.32, or 1.8%, to 848.18, part of its 31.70, or 3.6%, first-quarter decline. The Nasdaq Composite Index dropped 28.43, or 2.1%, to 1341.17, leaving it up just 5.27 points, or 0.4%, for the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  More discouraging news for the dollar came from the  Purchasing Management Association of Chicago's index of regional business activity, which fell to 48.4 in March from 54.9 in February. Index readings above 50 indicate an expansion in manufacturing, while readings below 50 show retrenchment. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, William Poole, president of the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, said in a written comment that the recent decline seen in the dollar relative to other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflects a slowing U.S. economy and is therefore not a major worry. \par \par \uc2  In the bank's April issue of its Regional Economist publication, Mr. Poole wrote that "if the dollar's recent decline can be attributed to the slowdown in the U.S. economy, along with corporate governance and geopolitical uncertainties, which I suspect it can, then recent weakness in the dollar is not a matter for serious concern." \par \par \uc2  "As the economy rebounds, I would expect foreign investment to make a comeback, and the dollar with it," Mr. Poole stated. \par \par \uc2  Between Jan. 2, 2002 and March 7 of this year, the dollar is down 20% against the euro and 13% against the yen, Mr. Poole noted in his remarks. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Japan's fiscal year ended Monday amid news that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  had confirmed that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Feb. 26 totaled roughly 1.13 trillion yen, almost as much as the combined totals for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January and February combined. On instructions from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to slow the yen's appreciation against the dollar, as a too-strong yen is seen by the Japanese government as a threat to the country's chances for an export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Michael Deibert; Dow Jones Newswires\par    michael.deibert@dowjones.com; 201-938-2248\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Michael Derby contributed to this report.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All Rights Reserved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 03-31-03 1738ET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030331dz3v008hl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppic