\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar mostly negative as jumpy traders sell. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kyle Peterson \par \uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 19:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates prices, adds comments, changes byline, dateline, previous NEW YORK) \par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, March 28 (Reuters) - The dollar remained on the defensive on Friday after extending losses in early New York trade amid persistent signs that the U.S.-led war in Iraq will drag on a while. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hovered near its lows after a sudden drop on news that a New York bridge was closed while police investigated suspicious activity. The incident raised fears of an attempted terror attack, but it later turned out to be harmless. \par \par \uc2  The incident served as a reminder of how on-edge markets remain with the United States at war. The dollar has been shifting lower all week on indications that the war with Iraq would not progress as quickly as initially hoped. \par \par \uc2  "We've had clear indications from the ground in Iraq that victory will not come swiftly to the U.S.-led coalition," said Alex Beuzelin, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Ruesch International. \par \par \uc2  "This war could become more of a drawn-out affair," he said. "That's dollar-negative because it's going to prolong the global uncertainty. We've seen the fog of war have adverse consequences for the U.S. economy." \par \par \uc2  Even before the reports about the bridge, the greenback slipped as traders continued to dump dollars on views the war in Iraq would be longer than thought in the first days of the conflict last week. \par \par \uc2  BAGHDAD POUNDED \par \par \uc2  In Iraq, warplanes and cruise missiles pounded Baghdad, while in the south U.S. brigades battled about 1,500 Iraqis overnight near Najaf, 100 miles (160 km) south of the capital. \par \par \uc2  Traders have been on guard for the possibility of retaliatory attacks on the United States in response to the U.S.-led war, so reports of a disturbance on the bridge in New York sparked an immediate fall in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Police said three men were arrested after they were seen climbing the Williamsburg Bridge that connects Manhattan to Brooklyn. Officers soon determined that the men were only "fooling around." \par \par \uc2  The dollar slid to session lows of $1.0777 per euro and 1.3688 Swiss francs. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like that news might have helped the market run some stops at the $1.0745-50 area. Especially with liquidity so low this week, we've seen some exaggerated moves," said Andrew Chaveriat, analyst at BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  Markets paid almost no attention to a mixed batch of U.S. economic data released on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Consumer spending was unchanged in a storm-battered February but incomes posted a solid 0.3 percent advance. Consumption also had been flat in January, while incomes rose 0.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  The University of Michigan's final March consumer sentiment index came in better than expected at 77.6, though it fell from February's reading of 79.9. Economists had expected a final reading of 75.3 for March. \par \par \uc2  IRAQ, ALL THE TIME \par \par \uc2  "No one is even looking at the data. It's all Iraq, all the time," said Tim Mazanec, director at Investor's Bank and Trust Company. "And any time there is a little scare, it's not helping the dollar." \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, market sources said Japanese monetary authorities had conducted dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during London and New York trading hours in the past few days, continuing this year's pattern of covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They have been doing so to ensure dollar/yen stays above 120 yen at the year-end," a source said. Japan's fiscal year-end is March 31. \par \par \uc2  Robert Sinche, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank, said Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  balance sheet data showed there had been some stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and he suspected it would continue beyond the fiscal year-end. \par \par \uc2  "We think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  favors the yen in a range of 120 to 130, and we are barely there. So I think they will try to resist any downside (in the dollar)," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, repeated on Friday that it was important for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to remain stable and that Japan was ready to act when needed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 120.06 yen, up 0.07 percent in the session. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030328dz3s00yqi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
