\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar mired near lows, drained by long war nerves. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 821 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 14:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 28 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Friday towards its lowest levels against the euro since the war in Iraq began as markets braced for a longer conflict than expected, but talk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  held it steady on the yen. \par \par \uc2  Market sources said the Japanese monetary authorities had conducted dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past few days, which had helped support the greenback in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback slid against the euro and Swiss franc as the U.S.-led conflict in Iraq went into its ninth day and U.S. and British officials said their efforts to oust Iraqi President Saddam Hussein would take longer than expected. \par \par \uc2  "Events in Iraq are still drawing most of the attention but it's difficult to make head or tail of which stories are accurate and which are not," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London. \par \par \uc2  "There's still quite a bit of concern that perhaps this is going to drag on longer than some have been anticipating." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was down a third of a percent on the day against the euro at $1.0724 by 1308 GMT, after testing the week's low of $1.0744 set on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The yen came under early pressure amid speculation that Japan wanted to make sure the dollar is above 120 yen on March 31, when companies close their books for the end of Japan's business year. \par \par \uc2  It fought back to stand just slightly lower on the day against the dollar around 120.17 but remained nearly half a percent weaker at 128.85 per euro. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also half a percent weaker against the Swiss franc, but gained on sterling, which was depressed by interest rate cut expectations in addition to war jitters. \par \par \uc2  WARFARE \par \par \uc2  While fierce bombing of Baghdad continued on Friday and U.S. and UK ground forces advanced on the Iraqi capital, some analysts said persistent uncertainty about the war would keep the dollar in tight ranges with investors wary of taking large bets. \par \par \uc2  Others expected concerns about the length of war, its impact on U.S. and UK budgets and on consumer confidence would make markets volatile. \par \par \uc2  "We're going to have huge lurches in sentiment which will track the progress and pitfalls made in Iraq and until the situation is resolved we're going to have quite a lot of volatility," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Halifax Bank of Scotland Treasury Services. \par \par \uc2  Data due on Friday does not yet reflect consumer reactions to the outbreak of war. \par \par \uc2  U.S. personal spending, at 1330 GMT, is for February but is forecast to show a fall of 0.1 percent after a 0.1 percent decline in January. The final March reading for the University of Michigan's consumer sentiment index is expected to be 75.3 versus 79.9 for February. \par \par \uc2  In Europe however, GfK research group data showed German consumers grew more pessimistic in March and even before the conflict started they did not expect any improvement in April as war and rising joblessness at home added to fears of recession. \par \par \uc2  Engineering orders in the euro zone's largest economy fell one percent in February as firms put off investing in new equipment ahead of the expected war. \par \par \uc2  After a mildly weaker close on Wall Street on Thursday, U.S. stock futures were indicating a weak start there later. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan is due to speak on community development at the Federal Reserve System Community Affairs Research Conference at 1800 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed no reaction to a report that European Union president Greece was proposing European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Wim Duisenberg stay an extra six months in the post pending a court verdict involving designated successor Jean-Claude Trichet. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  TALK \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, market sources said Japanese monetary authorities had conducted dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during London and New York trading hours in the past few days, continuing this year's pattern of covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They have been doing so to ensure dollar/yen stays above 120 yen at the year-end," a source said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry has said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January and February. Dealers believe action continued in March, with authorities thought to be concerned about keeping the dollar above 120 yen for March 31, the end of the fiscal year and book-closing day for many big Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, repeated that it was important for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to remain stable and that Japan was ready to act when needed. \par \par \uc2  Data out of Japan on Friday showed a fall in the jobless rate, which analysts attributed to unemployed people giving up the search for work, and wilting consumer spending, leaving the economy even more dependent on exports. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030328dz3s00pt4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
