\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar stands in place as war in Iraq progresses. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 22:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 27 (Reuters) - Investors played it close to the chest on Thursday, leaving the dollar nearly unchanged as the war in Iraq progressed but the realization that a quick conclusion was not at hand had the greenback under pressure. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been creeping lower since Monday, when it became clear that the war in Iraq was not going to be over as quickly as some had hoped, losing about two cents to the euro. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said economic data, good or bad, were not swaying investors and instead it was an environment of positioning for headlines and major turns in the war. \par \par \uc2  "There is a quest in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to find the safest and most neutral position where investors can better confront the next movement or market storm," said Ashraf Laidi, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at MG Financial Group in New York. \par \par \uc2  "For example, if there is a safe entry into Baghdad by U.S.-led forces, those short-dollar positions will want to reverse. Conversely, should the path to Baghdad become more treacherous, that would reward short-dollar positions," he said. \par \par \uc2  As the war entered its eighth day on Thursday, bombs continued to shake Baghdad. Iraq's defense minister said on Thursday he expected U.S.-led forces would manage to encircle Baghdad within five to 10 days and eventually have to fight Iraqis in the streets. \par \par \uc2  In a joint news briefing with British Prime Minister Tony Blair, U.S. President George W. Bush said the Iraq war would last "however long it takes to win." \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the session, the dollar came under pressure after the Washington Post quoted unidentified senior U.S. defense officials as saying the war was likely to last for months and would require more combat power than in Iraq now. \par \par \uc2  "It's the same trade we've been seeing all week since people began to think that the war in Iraq would be longer than originally thought," said Paul Podolsky, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Fleet Global Rate Markets in Boston. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose slightly to $1.0694, but was off the day's highs of $1.0744, a level that put it at its highest point in 10 days. \par \par \uc2  One longer-term negative for the euro came from Germany's warning that its budget deficit in 2003 would likely be above the 3 percent of gross domestic product limit set by the European Union's Stability and Growth Pact. The pact helps underpin the euro's value. In 2002, Germany's budget deficit was 3.6 percent of GDP. \par \par \uc2  German Finance Minister Hans Eichel cited on Thursday the weak economy's toll on tax revenues for missing the target, but said he was not concerned about the pact because it allows for some maneuverability due to exceptional circumstances, such as war in Iraq. Germany is the largest economy in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was nearly unchanged against the Swiss franc at 1.3818 francs. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped 0.08 percent to 119.93 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might engage in some stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday to keep the yen on the soft side ahead of the fiscal year-end on March 31. \par \par \uc2  U.S. GDP DATA MEETS EXPECTATIONS \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Commerce Department on Thursday offered its final take on fourth-quarter gross domestic product, leaving its estimate at a 1.4 percent annual growth rate. The figure matched economists' expectations and had little effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices. \par \par \uc2  Softer consumer spending and a drop in exports pushed growth down from the 4.0 percent pace of the third quarter. For the year as a whole, the economy grew a modest 2.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  New U.S. weekly jobless claims came in better than forecast, falling to 402,000 in the week ended March 22 from an upwardly revised 427,000 the prior week. Economists had expected 419,000 new claims. \par \par \uc2  "The jobless claims number was better than expected, but the market is not reacting to economic news these days," said Ron Simpson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at MMS International. \par \par \uc2  STERLING FALLS \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell versus the euro as renewed concerns over the progress of the war coincided with speculation about another interest rate cut from the Bank of England. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with Reuters, Monetary Policy Committee member Marian Bell said the Bank could cut rates again and that higher inflation in the short term was no barrier to action. \par \par \uc2  Britain's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stunned markets in February by chopping interest rates to a 48-year low of 3.75 percent because of the prospects for weaker demand at home and abroad. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose 0.68 percent against the pound at 68.31 pence. Against the dollar, sterling was off a 0.62 percent at $1.5646. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030327dz3r0157g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
