\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Uncertainty plagues dollar as Iraq war goes on. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 763 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 18:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 27 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped on Thursday in a continuation of the week's slow grind lower as traders fretted over the possibility of a lengthy Iraq war. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody was so certain last week that the war would be short, and that has been replaced by uncertainty," said Jason Bonanca, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York. \par \par \uc2  "That doesn't mean we know where the war is going, but uncertainty is always bad for the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been creeping lower since Monday, when it became clear that the war in Iraq was not going to be over as quickly as some had hoped, losing about two cents to the euro. \par \par \uc2  As the war entered its eighth day on Thursday, bombs continued to shake Baghdad and about 1,000 U.S. troops parachuted into Kurdish-held northern Iraq. \par \par \uc2  In a joint news briefing with British Prime Minister Tony Blair, U.S. President George W. Bush said the Iraq war would last "however long it takes to win." \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the session, the dollar came under pressure after the Washington Post quoted unidentified senior U.S. defense officials as saying the war was likely to last for months and would require more combat power than in Iraq now. \par \par \uc2  "It's the same trade we've been seeing all week since people began to think that the war in Iraq would be longer than originally thought," said Paul Podolsky, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Fleet Global Rate Markets in Boston. \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to trade within narrow ranges, the potential for volatility was there, he said. \par \par \uc2  "It's still an easy environment to lose money. If you step away from your desk, you could be hurting fast, because there are so many factors that could come in and move the market," he said. \par \par \uc2  By midday, the euro had gained about a third of a percent to $1.0717 , but was off the day's highs of $1.0744, a level that put it at its highest point in 10 days. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was off 0.16 percent at 1.3794 francs . \par \par \uc2  The dollar's losses to the Swiss franc were muted by comments by Swiss National Bank Vice Chairman Bruno Gehrig, who late on Wednesday said the bank would resort to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the franc were to spike higher. \par \par \uc2  His remarks depressed the franc against the euro, and it fell to a one-year low of 1.4811 francs per euro . By midday, it was at 1.4783 francs per euro. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped 0.32 percent to 119.64 yen . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might engage in some stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday to keep the yen on the soft side ahead of the fiscal year-end on March 31. \par \par \uc2  U.S. GDP DATA MEETS EXPECTATIONS \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Commerce Department on Thursday offered its final take on fourth-quarter gross domestic product, leaving its estimate at a 1.4 percent annual growth rate. \par \par \uc2  The figure matched economists' expectations and had little effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices. \par \par \uc2  Softer consumer spending and a drop in exports pushed growth down from the 4.0 percent pace of the third quarter. For the year as a whole, the economy grew a modest 2.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  New U.S. weekly jobless claims came in better than forecast, falling to 402,000 in the week ended March 22 from an upwardly revised 427,000 the prior week. Economists had expected 419,000 new claims. \par \par \uc2  "The jobless claims number was better than expected, but the market is not reacting to economic news these days," said Ron Simpson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at MMS International. \par \par \uc2  STERLING FALLS \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell about a half a percent against the euro as renewed concerns over the progress of the war coincided with speculation about another interest rate cut from the Bank of England. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with Reuters, Monetary Policy Committee member Marian Bell said the Bank could cut rates again and that higher inflation in the short term was not a barrier to action. \par \par \uc2  Britain's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stunned markets in February by chopping interest rates to a 48-year low of 3.75 percent because of the prospects for weaker demand at home and abroad. \par \par \uc2  By midday in New York, the euro was up 0.56 percent against the pound at 68.23 pence . Against the dollar, sterling was off a quarter percent at $1.5706 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030327dz3r00ykh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
