\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar recoils as U.S.-UK bombings of Iraq continue. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 14:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 27 (Reuters) - The dollar fell broadly on Thursday, hitting its lowest level against the euro since the start of the Iraq war, as cautious comments from U.S. officials raised concern the conflict could be prolonged. \par \par \uc2  President George W. Bush cautioned on Wednesday against any expectation of a quick victory in Iraq, changing his prepared remarks at the last minute to drop an assessment that the campaign was "ahead of schedule". \par \par \uc2  Also on Wednesday, the Washington Post quoted unnamed senior U.S. defence officials as saying the war was likely to last for months and would require more combat power than on hand now. \par \par \uc2  Britain, Washington's main ally in war, followed the talk of more power needed with Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown raising the UK war budget to three billion pounds ($4.72 billion) from 1.75 billion sterling. \par \par \uc2  "The market is at the mercy of news headlines," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Mellon Financial Corporation in London. "It is fickle and will continue to trade in this volatile fashion." \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a 10-day low of $1.0744 per euro in the European midsession, triggering automatic sell orders on the break of $1.0730. It was down 0.18 percent at 119.78 yen. \par \par \uc2  Throughout Thursday, which marked the start of the war's second week, U.S.-UK troops continued to bomb Baghdad, while U.S. paratroopers were opening a new front in northern Iraq in addition to the ongoing offensive from Kuwait in the south. \par \par \uc2  NEWS OVERLOAD \par \par \uc2  The dollar gyrated after UK defence chief Admiral Michael Boyce said the army had found chemical weapons suits, arguing they showed Iraqi intent on weapons of mass destruction. \par \par \uc2  The dollar moved up briefly after the news, but market analysts said it was not clear whether this was actually supportive of the greenback, underlining market confusion in recent sessions about the proper reaction to individual pieces of news from the front. \par \par \uc2  Washington says the war is needed to disarm Iraq of banned weapons, which Iraq denies having. \par \par \uc2  Clear evidence that Iraq did possess them could rally more world support for the otherwise pretty isolated Washington, supporting the dollar. But it would also raise fears that Baghdad could use the arms. \par \par \uc2  "It is possible the dollar moved up on this because it makes the war more palatable to the rest of the world," said Gunner. \par \par \uc2  But a European bank trader said the such news should only depress the dollar and not buoy it. \par \par \uc2  Markets are also watching the 1330 GMT release of final U.S. fourth quarter gross domestic product (GDP) data and weekly jobless claims for the week ended on March 22. \par \par \uc2  STERLING FALLS \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell more than half a percent against the euro, before edging up slighty, as renewed concerns over the progress of the war coincided with speculation about another interest rate cut from the Bank of England. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with Reuters, Monetary Policy Committee member Marian Bell said the Bank could cut rates again and that higher inflation in the short-term was not a barrier to action. \par \par \uc2  Britain's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stunned markets in February by chopping interest rates to a 48-year low of 3.75 percent because of the prospects for weaker demand both at home and abroad. \par \par \uc2  Concern a long war will undermine an already soggy global economy by hurting consumer confidence and delaying business investment pushed European stocks lower on Thursday. U.S. stock futures were also pointing to heavy losses at the open. \par \par \uc2  The euro powered to a one-year high above 1.48 Swiss francs on Thursday after the Swiss National Bank again said it might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quell a possible rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  SNB Vice-Chairman Bruno Gehrig said on Wednesday the SNB would use \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary since its traditional interest rate tools were all but exhausted. \par \par \uc2  Gehrig added that with consumer price inflation and inflation forecasts at record lows, the bank could afford to maintain its expansive policy for some time. \par \par \uc2  "The SNB have continued verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the appreciation of the franc and it is easy to see the euro rising to 1.50 or 1.55 francs in the near future," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One. \par \par \uc2  The market was also nervous that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem unwanted appreciation in the yen, particularly ahead of the financial year end on March 31. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030327dz3r00qe6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
