\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar loses ground as fears of prolonged war grow. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 12:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 27 (Reuters) - The dollar fell broadly on Thursday, hitting its lowest level against the euro since the start of the Iraq war, as cautious comments from U.S. officials raised concern that the conflict could be prolonged. \par \par \uc2  President George W. Bush cautioned on Wednesday against any expectation of a quick victory in Iraq, changing his prepared remarks at the last minute to drop an assessment that the campaign was "ahead of schedule". \par \par \uc2  Also on Wednesday, the Washington Post quoted unnamed senior U.S. defence officials as saying the war was likely to last for months and would require more combat power than currently on hand. \par \par \uc2  "The war is proving more difficult than initially envisaged and that is weighing on the dollar," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. is calling for more troops which suggests things are going to be a little more tricky." \par \par \uc2  U.S. troops parachuted into Iraqi mountains in Kurdish controlled northern Iraq after the United States said it would fly more than 30,000 troops to the Gulf to reinforce those already on the ground. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a 10-day low of $1.0744 per euro in the European midsession, triggering automatic sell orders on the break of $1.0730. It was down a quarter-percent at 119.78 yen. \par \par \uc2  STERLING FALLS \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell more than half a percent against the euro as renewed concerns over the progress of the war coincided with speculation about another interest rate cut from the Bank of England. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with Reuters, Monetary Policy Committee member Marian Bell said the Bank could cut rates again if needed and that higher inflation in the short-term was not a barrier to action. \par \par \uc2  Britain's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stunned markets in February by chopping interest rates to a 48-year low of 3.75 percent because of the prospects for weaker demand both at home and abroad. \par \par \uc2  Concern that a long war will undermine an already soggy global economy by hurting consumer confidence and delaying business investment pushed European stocks lower on Thursday. U.S. stock futures were also pointing to heavy losses at the open. \par \par \uc2  A final figure for U.S. fourth-quarter gross domestic product is due at 1330 GMT, along with weekly U.S. jobless claims. \par \par \uc2  EURO GAINS VS SWISSIE \par \par \uc2  The euro powered to a one-year high above 1.48 Swiss francs on Thursday after the Swiss National Bank again said it might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quell a possible rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In an address in Hamburg on Wednesday, SNB Vice-Chairman Bruno Gehrig said the SNB would use \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary since its traditional interest rate tools were all-but exhausted. \par \par \uc2  Gehrig added that with consumer price inflation and inflation forecasts at record lows, the bank could afford to maintain its expansive policy for some time. \par \par \uc2  "The SNB have continued verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the appreciation of the franc and it is easy to see the euro rising to 1.50 or 1.55 francs in the near future," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One. \par \par \uc2  The market was also nervous that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem unwanted appreciation in the yen, particularly ahead of the financial year end on March 31. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, again said on Thursday that the Finance Ministry was ready to act to prevent large swings in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030327dz3r00lx3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
