\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Investor focus on war causes caution on dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 22:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 26 (Reuters) - The dollar weakened slightly on Wednesday, with trading light as investors paid scant attention to a flurry of gloomy U.S. economic news and focused instead on headlines about the war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  As the war moved into its seventh day, U.S. President George W. Bush said the conflict was "far from over" and that the path ahead for U.S.-led forces would not be easy and may be long. \par \par \uc2  "People are watching and hanging off of each last piece of information ... and you see that reflected in price action," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  "But it has been an extremely quiet day and a large part of it has to do with people having already shored up their positions and are now waiting to see what happens," he said. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to $1.0686 , up 0.35 percent from Tuesday's New York close, but below the session high at $1.0702. The dollar was down for the third straight day against the euro after last week's rally following the start of the war. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to 1.3815 Swiss francs , a loss of 0.11 percent on the day. The euro rose to 1.4765 francs , up 0.22 percent. \par \par \uc2  Swiss National Bank Vice-Chairman Bruno Gehrig explicitly warned investors the bank would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try and quell any spike higher in the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Since the SNB slashed interest rates to near zero earlier this month, Gehrig said traditional monetary policy was exhausted and the bank would have little choice but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the franc rise in value and further threaten still-weak Swiss economic growth. \par \par \uc2  Sterling rose to $1.5743 , up 0.11 percent. \par \par \uc2  Joe Francomano, vice president at Erste Bank in New York, said the market was still anticipating an eventual U.S.-led victory in Iraq. But the prospect of a longer conflict "has introduced some uncertainty, and uncertainty is not good for the dollar, and that is why it has weakened this week." \par \par \uc2  Repeated air raids struck the southern outskirts of Baghdad on Wednesday, and up to 15 people died when an apparent U.S. air raid devastated a residential and commercial street in the city. \par \par \uc2  U.S. and British spokesmen said they had no immediate information on the blast, but Britain's defense minister Geoff Hoon said the risk of civilian casualties was growing. \par \par \uc2  Later on Wednesday, British Prime Minister Tony Blair was to meet with Bush in the United States for a war summit. \par \par \uc2  A column of elite Iraqi Republican Guard units streamed out of Baghdad on Wednesday, using a sandstorm for cover and headed toward U.S. forces massed near the southern Iraqi city of Najaf, CNN Television reported. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC GLOOM \par \par \uc2  Economic news took secondary importance to the war in Iraq, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed little reaction to news western Germany's Ifo business climate index unexpectedly fell in March, to 88.1 from 88.9 in February. The majority of the 7,000 participants were surveyed before the U.S.-led attack on Iraq began. \par \par \uc2  "This was a very bad index but the euro was completely unaffected, which highlights the fact that the market is not looking at the fundamentals," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at WestLB in London. \par \par \uc2  The market also paid little mind to a weak U.S. durable goods report for February. Orders fell 1.2 percent, the largest drop since November 2002, while January orders were revised to a 1.9 percent rise from a 2.9 percent gain. \par \par \uc2  Dealers took more notice of comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  member Ernst Welteke, who said bluntly that a rate move from the ECB next week was unlikely if the global situation remained unclear. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed to try to increase the money supply. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the comments had little effect on the yen. They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained constrained on the upside by fears of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on the downside by yen purchases by the nation's exporters. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar traded at 120.02 yen , down 0.10 percent on the session. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030326dz3q015tq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
