\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar steady as traders turn cautious. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 19:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 26 (Reuters) - The dollar was little changed on Wednesday as traders settled in for a protracted war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "After the rollercoaster ride of the last weeks before the war began and in its early days, the market is starting to settle down as traders get used to the idea that the war would be longer than was initially expected," said Joe Francomano, vice president at Erste Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  As the war moved into its seventh day, U.S. President George W. Bush said the war was "far from over" and that the path ahead for U.S.-led forces would not be easy and may be long. \par \par \uc2  Francomano said the market was still anticipating a U.S. victory eventually. But the prospect of a longer conflict "has introduced some uncertainty, and uncertainty is not good for the dollar, and that is why it has weakened this week." \par \par \uc2  By midday on Wednesday, the dollar was off about a tenth of a percent against the euro at $1.0665 per euro, the third straight day of losses for the dollar after last week's rally following the start of the war. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was slightly higher at 1.3841 francs. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said liquidity was very thin, and that traders were less willing to react to every headline about developments in the war, preferring to wait for a major change before taking on big positions. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone is watching the war but people are not as willing to trade on individual snippets of news. It has to be something very specific, a big event, in order to break the ranges in the dollar," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAGlobal in New York. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, repeated air raids struck the southern outskirts of Baghdad, and up to 15 people died when an apparent U.S. air raid devastated a residential and commercial street in the city. \par \par \uc2  U.S. and British spokesmen said they had no immediate information on the blasts, but Britain's defense minister Geoff Hoon said the risk of civilian casualties was growing. \par \par \uc2  Later on Wednesday, British Prime Minister Tony Blair will meet with Bush in the United States for a war summit. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC GLOOM \par \par \uc2  Economic news remained of secondary importance to the war in Iraq, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed little reaction to news western Germany's Ifo business climate index unexpectedly fell in March, to 88.1 from 88.9 in February. \par \par \uc2  The majority of the 7,000 participants were surveyed before the U.S.-led attack on Iraq began. \par \par \uc2  "This was a very bad index but the euro was completely unaffected, which highlights the fact that the market is not looking at the fundamentals," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at WestLB in London. \par \par \uc2  The market also paid little mind to a weak U.S. durable goods report for February. Orders fell 1.2 percent, the largest drop since November 2002, while January orders were revised to a 1.9 percent rise from a 2.9 percent gain. \par \par \uc2  Dealers took more notice of comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  member Ernst Welteke, who said bluntly that a rate move from the ECB next week was unlikely if the global situation remained unclear. \par \par \uc2  "We got a little uptick on the euro from Welteke's comments," said IDEAGlobal's Callow. Most economists also are betting on no rate cut next week. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed to try to increase the money supply. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the comments had little effect on the yen. They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained constrained on the upside by fears of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on the downside by yen purchases by the nation's exporters. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 120.02 yen, down 0.10 percent on the session. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030326dz3q00zyl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
