\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar steadies, market sifts through Iraq news. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 11:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 26 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied in the middle of recent ranges against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, awaiting direction from developments in Iraq for a better view of how long the conflict will last. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was lifted off one-week lows on Tuesday by reports of a popular rebellion in Iraq's second city, Basra, which might give U.S.-led forces a boost if confirmed, but lack of confirmation meant the market was unsure how to react. \par \par \uc2  "The story of an uprising in Basra helped the dollar in the U.S. afternoon but it's not really progressed. If there was anything to it you'd think there'd be more eye witness reports," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Mellon Financial Corporation. \par \par \uc2  "The basic framework is that the market will try to keep in mind the possibility of a dollar rally if the conflict comes to a close. But from time to time it's going to be hostage to all the headlines coming through." \par \par \uc2  Markets were waiting for the battle of Baghdad to begin in earnest on day seven of the war, as repeated air raids struck the southern outskirts of the city on Wednesday and another hit an area housing the television centre. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters witness saw 15 corpses on a Baghdad street, apparently after a missile strike. \par \par \uc2  By 1030 GMT the dollar was steady on the day at $1.0648 per euro  after falling beyond $1.0720 on Tuesday to its lowest levels since the start of the Iraq war. The dollar hit a two-month high beyond $1.05 per euro on Friday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was also steady on the safe-haven Swiss franc at 1.3835  but\par slightly up on the yen at 120.31       .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar hit a three-month high of 121.87 on Friday, but fell as far\par as 119.35 yen on Tuesday. Analysts said the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  retains some\par support from worries over possible dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the\par Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , especially during the sensitive period leading up to the\par end of the Japanese fiscal year on March 31.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BASRA REBELLION?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The British military said it believed citizens of Basra were rising up\par against forces loyal to President Saddam Hussein, but an Iraqi minister\par denied a revolt was under way there and Al-Jazeera television said the\par city was calm.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Reports about an uprising had injected optimism into the market that\par the United States was gaining support from Iraqi people, which could\par make it easier for U.S.-led forces to occupy the capital, Baghdad.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There was an initial optimism that coalition troops would be welcomed\par with open arms, only to be followed by worries over a prolonged\par guerrilla conflict," said Rob Hayward, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at\par ABN Amro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "(A popular uprising) would suggest the worst fears of continued\par guerrilla warfare would perhaps not be borne out."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With traders preoccupied with developments in the Middle East, the\par market reacted little to news that North Korea was pulling out of\par regular military liaison talks with the United States at the\par Demilitarised Zone frontier because of annual U.S. military exercises\par in the South.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For a snapshot of the latest developments in the Iraq crisis, please\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par British Prime Minister Tony Blair answers Prime Minister's Questions in\par the lower house of parliament at 1200 GMT and then leaves for\par Washington where he will meet U.S. President George W. Bush for a war\par summit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WORSENING SENTIMENT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economic news remains of secondary importance to the war in Iraq,\par analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro showed little reaction to news western Germany's Ifo business\par climate index unexpectedly fell in March, to 88.1 from 88.9 in\par February.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The index is one of the first sentiment indicators released since the\par start of the Iraq war last Thursday, but the majority of the 7,000\par participants were surveyed on March 5-20, before the U.S.-led attack on\par Iraq began.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This was a very bad index but the euro was completely unaffected,\par which highlights the fact that the market is not looking at the\par fundamentals," said Michael Klawitter, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at\par WestLB.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Net direct and portfolio investment outflows from the euro zone\par increased in January, according to European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There were net outflows of 16.5 billion euros, compared with outflows\par of 4.3 billion in December.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts have blamed strong capital outflows from the euro zone for\par much of the euro's weakness against the dollar in the past and believe\par the reversal of this trend last year contributed to the single\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ECB Governing Council member and Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke\par holds a news conference at 1200 GMT to present the Bundesbank's 2002\par report.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. durable goods orders at 1330 GMT are forecast to have fallen by\par 1.2 percent in February, after rising by 2.9 percent in January.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030326dz3q00lx0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
