\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Makes Round Trip on Shifting War Outlook \b0\par\par\uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 23:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par John Parry\par Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -- The dollar slipped Tuesday on fears of a longer, more\par convoluted-than-expected war with Iraq, but pared most of its losses in\par late trade on hopes sparked by reports of an uprising against Iraqi\par President Saddam Hussein in the city of Basra.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday in New York the dollar was trading at 120.14 yen, down from 120.66 yen late Monday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.0654, moderately up from $1.0637 late Monday. Against the Swiss franc -- a classic refuge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in times of military conflict -- the dollar was at 1.3827 francs, down from 1.3846 francs late Monday. Sterling was at $1.5724, little changed from $1.5730. \par \par \uc2  "If the uprising does have teeth, it could be significant," says Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Any broader insurrection against the Iraqi leadership, especially if it spread to Baghdad, "could support the theory it would be a short war, and if that does happen, expect the dollar and equities to rally," Mr. Mazanec added. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to reporters Tuesday on the sidelines of an economic conference in Washington, Treasury Secretary John Snow acknowledged the tie between the war and the economy, including the dollar. \par \par \uc2  According to Mr. Snow, the recent drop in oil prices and the rise of the dollar reflects the "positive" effects of the war with Iraq, although he warned the overall economy remains weak and needs the stimulus of tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  "The buildup to the war had the uncertainty associated with it, had a negative effect -- higher oil prices, higher energy prices acted like a tax on the American economy and slowed down the performance very markedly," Mr. Snow told reporters after a speech to the National Association for Business Economics. \par \par \uc2  But those effects, he said, have been at least partly reversed. The start of war, he said, had produced a "very nice" drop in oil prices and a "favorable" reaction in financial markets. \par \par \uc2  "The overall response of the equity markets, the bond markets, the debt markets has been positive," he said. "The dollar has risen, so overall the response has been favorable." \par \par \uc2  The dollar has rebounded some 4% from its prewar trough against the euro, when the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peaked at $1.1087 on March 11. However, this week the dollar has retreated from multimonths highs versus the euro and yen reached last Friday on optimism over a quick and clean war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday, the greenback also took some heart from a stock-market rally, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average rising 0.8%. \par \par \uc2  Stiffer resistance by Iraqi forces than some had anticipated ahead of the confrontation expected between U.S.-led troops and Republican Guard units around Baghdad was the prevailing force dragging the dollar somewhat lower, said Hugh Walsh, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Fortis USA Financial Markets in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that the process is slowing down is really what's hurting the dollar," Mr. Walsh added. Although U.S. forces are now reportedly within about 60 miles of the Iraqi capital, sandstorms slowed down their push Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the market's close focus on the military campaign, some U.S. economic data reports did capture traders' attention. \par \par \uc2  U.S. consumer confidence dipped further in March, following its large decline in February, as ongoing softness in the economy and the U.S.-led war in Iraq continued to weigh on the minds of Americans. The Conference Board's consumer confidence index fell to 62.5 from 64.8 the prior month, which added to factors weighing on the dollar. Consumers account for some two thirds of U.S. economic activity. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have the sense that "there is the economy before Baghdad falls and the economy after Baghdad falls," said Alan Ruskin, research director of 4Cast in New York. But the longer the war drags on, the more sluggish the world's biggest economy may become, some analysts worry. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, events in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have put \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders on alert for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  designed to weaken the yen by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of Japan. For much of the session, the dollar traded below the psychologically key 120 yen mark, hitting session lows around 119.46 yen. \par \par \uc2  A primary catalyst for the yen's rise was the "distinctly undramatic" result of an emergency meeting the BOJ held early in the Asian session, at which traders had expected the newly appointed governor Toshihiko Fukui to announce some fairly dramatic monetary policy easing moves, wrote \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists with Brown Brothers Harriman in a daily research note. \par \par \uc2  -- Joseph Rebello in Washington and Michael Deibert in New York contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  03-25-03 1753ET \p