\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Worries over war's duration hurt dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 14:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 25 (Reuters) - The dollar on Tuesday hit its lowest levels against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the war against Iraq began six days ago, as markets worried that fighting could take longer than they had initially expected. The greenback's slide was propelled also by the Bank of Japan's decision early in the session to keep monetary policy on hold, disappointing some yen bears who were looking for more radical moves to stem deflation and weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S.-led forces were progressing in their bid to defeat Iraq, opening up the southern Iraqi port of Umm Qasr and forcing their way across the Euphrates River at a key town of Nassiriya after days of fighting fierce Iraqi resistance. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, focused tightly on war news, were extremely jittery and investor opinion changed at a moment's notice as news from the war bombarded public opinion, making it hard to make investment decisions. \par \par \uc2  "We are in a period when investors are taking risk off the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said Michael Metcalfe, senior strategist at State Street in London. "The news flow from Iraq is creating a lot of short-term volatility. Investors are staying on the sidelines." \par \par \uc2  "The conflict is still the biggest short-term variable and investor opinion seems to be going back and forth so it is hard to make medium-term decisions," he said. \par \par \uc2  By 1242 GMT the dollar had retraced some of its earlier losses but was still down almost half a percent on the day against the euro, trading at $1.0683. \par \par \uc2  The yen gained 0.70 percent on the dollar, trading at 119.92, and a quarter of a percent on the euro, standing at 128.13. \par \par \uc2  MARKET AT A LOSS \par \par \uc2  For a snapshot of the latest developments in the Iraq crisis, please click on. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said markets, mesmerised by the blow-by-blow and sometimes grisly media coverage of the war, were disappointed with its progress even though U.S. and UK officials have argued the fighting is going in line with plans despite casualties. \par \par \uc2  "The market is now a pendulum, swaying between views of a long war and a short war in Iraq, but I think it's leaning towards a longer war," said a trader at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  A poll of 11 fund managers made by Reuters as the conflict got underway last week showed an average forecast that the war would last four weeks. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said volumes seemed to have fallen back as clients hesitated before taking a view. \par \par \uc2  "They've all gone quiet. People are confused, the war is being drawn out. People are trying to take stock how long it's going to be and how much it will cost," one said. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC WORRIES \par \par \uc2  Any signs of a long combat could play on the market's biggest dollar fear: that the U.S. will be saddled with huge war-related costs in an environment where international investors are wary of sending savings abroad which could help finance its debts and its huge trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President George W. Bush told lawmakers on Monday he urgently needed $75 billion to pay for the war campaign and to reward key allies for supporting the United States' war effort. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, confidence data from the United States is expected to show consumers were cautious before the outbreak of war with the Conference Board's consumer confidence index seen at 62.4 in March, versus 64 in February, when the index fell sharply. \par \par \uc2  The survey period ended before the start of war. \par \par \uc2  National Association of Realtors' report, also due at 1500 GMT, is expected to show that existing home sales fell to a 5.8 million unit rate in February from 6.09 in January. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF JAPAN SITS PAT \par \par \uc2  An emergency meeting of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Policy Board provided no surprises. The BOJ, holding its first policy-setting gathering under its new governor, Toshihiko Fukui, decided to leave monetary policy unchanged but to provide as much liquidity to the market as needed for the time being. \par \par \uc2  Before the meeting, the yen had faced pressure on speculation that the BOJ would adopt more aggressive easing measures, like buying bonds denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Once the outcome was clear the dollar started losing ground, dropping to a session low below 119.50 yen. But the market was nervous about selling the dollar too heavily on worries that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030325dz3p00qe8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
