\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar suffers across board, short-war hopes ebb. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 824 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 12:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 25 (Reuters) - The dollar on Tuesday hit its lowest levels against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the war against Iraq began six days ago, as markets worried that fighting could take longer than they had initially expected. The greenback's slide was propelled also by the Bank of Japan's decision early in the session to keep monetary policy on hold, disappointing some yen bears who were looking for more radical moves to stem deflation and weaken the yen. The U.S.-led forces were progressing in their bid to defeat Iraq, opening up a southern Iraqi port of Umm Qasr and forcing their way across the Euphrates River at a key town of Nassiriya after days of fighting fierce Iraqi resistance. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, focused tightly on war news, were extremely jittery and movement depended on how the war was perceived to be going. \par \par \uc2  "There is repricing of war optimism going on," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London. "Right now the balance is tipped against the dollar." \par \par \uc2  "But there could be a break for the U.S. forces and the dollar could be trading higher. There is a lot of uncertainty and things could change quickly...because the geopolitical situation is hard to predict," he said. \par \par \uc2  By 1113 GMT the dollar shed two thirds of a percent against the euro since late New York trade, but was off lows set earlier at $1.0722. \par \par \uc2  The yen gained 0.90 percent on the dollar, trading at 119.68, and a third of a percent on the euro, standing at 128.12. \par \par \uc2  GRISLY WAR NEWS \par \par \uc2  In a further blow to dollar sentiment, U.S. stock indices clocked up their biggest losses this year, with the high-tech Nasdaq, Dow Jones industrial average and the broad Standard & Poor's index all closing more than 3.5 percent down on Monday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock futures were so far pointing to a mixed start on Wall Street later. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said markets, mesmerised by the blow-by-blow and sometimes grisly media coverage of the war, were disappointed with its progress even though U.S. and UK officials have argued the fighting is going in line with plans despite casualties. \par \par \uc2  "The market is now a pendulum, swaying between views of a long war and a short war in Iraq, but I think it's leaning towards a longer war," said a trader at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  A poll of 11 fund managers made by Reuters as the conflict got underway last week showed an average forecast that the war would last four weeks. \par \par \uc2  Any signs of a long combat could play on the market's biggest dollar fear: that the U.S. will be saddled with huge war-related costs in an environment where international investors are wary of sending savings abroad which could help finance its debts and its huge trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President George W. Bush told lawmakers on Monday he urgently needed $75 billion to pay for the war campaign and to reward key allies for supporting the United States' war effort. \par \par \uc2  If accepted this could spell trouble for the U.S. budget deficit and the dollar longer term, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF JAPAN SITS PAT \par \par \uc2  An emergency meeting of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Policy Board provided no surprises. The BOJ, holding its first policy-setting gathering under its new governor, Toshihiko Fukui, decided to leave monetary policy unchanged but to provide as much liquidity to the market as needed for the time being. \par \par \uc2  The board decided to increase the BOJ's purchases of shares held by banks to three trillion yen ($24.85 billion) from the current two trillion yen, expanding a programme begun last year aimed at reducing banks' exposure to stock volatility. \par \par \uc2  Before the meeting, the yen had faced pressure on speculation that the BOJ would adopt more aggressive easing measures, like buying bonds denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Once the outcome was clear the dollar started losing ground, dropping to a session low below 119.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  "During the recent long (dollar) liquidation triggered by pessimism over the war, the dollar/yen rate had been left out of active selling ahead of the BOJ meeting," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of interbank sales at Trust and Custody Services Bank. \par \par \uc2  "But after confirming that the BOJ had refrained from taking extraordinary measures such as foreign bond buying, long holders unwound their positions. The market must have been holding on to longs pretty heavily, having seen the dollar's sharp falls." But the market was nervous about selling the dollar too heavily amid wariness that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa reiterated his caution over speculative moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, although he said he did not see any such moves at present. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030325dz3p00mzy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
