\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls to Iraq-war lows, BOJ no help. \b0\par\par\uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 10:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  By Charlotte Cooper LONDON, March 25 (Reuters) - The dollar on Tuesday fell to its lowest level on the euro and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the start of the war in Iraq, with markets rattled by slower than expected progress by U.S.-led forces. \par \par \uc2  The greenback slid nearly one percent against the yen, below 120 yen , after yen bears were disappointed the Bank of Japan took no radical steps to tackle deflation and weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at an emergency meeting on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  On the sixth day of the war in Iraq, U.S. Marines were fighting a fierce battle with Iraqi forces in the southern city of Nassiriya, while other coalition forces began an assault on defences approaching Baghdad. \par \par \uc2  "While progress is still being made it's quite clear there is significant Iraqi resistance and the government in Iraq is not going to collapse as quickly as initially hoped. That's pressuring the dollar across the board," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg in London. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By 0820 GMT, the dollar was down more than half a percent on the day against\par the euro and the Swiss franc at $1.0700 per euro  and 1.3764 francs       .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It was 0.9 percent down at 119.65 yen and had given up a third of a percent\par to the British pound .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BANK OF JAPAN DISAPPOINTS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An emergency meeting of the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Policy Board provided no\par surprises. The BOJ, holding its first policy-setting gathering under its new\par governor, Toshihiko Fukui, decided to leave monetary policy unchanged but to\par provide as much liquidity to the market as needed for the time being.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The board decided to increase the BOJ's purchases of shares held by banks to\par three trillion yen ($24.85 billion) from the current two trillion yen,\par expanding a programme begun last year aimed at reducing banks' exposure to\par stock volatility.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Before the meeting, the yen had faced pressure on speculation that the BOJ\par would adopt more aggressive easing measures, like buying bonds denominated\par in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Once the outcome was clear the dollar started losing ground, dropping to a\par session low below 119.50 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "During the recent long (dollar) liquidation triggered by pessimism over the\par war, the dollar/yen rate had been left out of active selling ahead of the\par BOJ meeting," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of interbank sales at Trust and\par Custody Services Bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "But after confirming that the BOJ had refrained from taking extraordinary\par measures such as foreign bond buying, long holders unwound their positions.\par The market must have been holding on to longs pretty heavily, having seen\par the dollar's sharp falls." But the market was nervous about selling the\par dollar too heavily amid wariness that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary authorities may\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa reiterated his caution over speculative\par moves in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, although he said he did not see any\par such moves at present.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BLOW-BY-BLOW\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In a further blow to dollar sentiment, U.S. stock indices clocked up their\par biggest losses this year, with the high-tech Nasdaq , Dow Jones industrial\par average  and the broad Standard & Poor's index        all closing more than\par 3.5 percent down on Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. stock futures were so far pointing to a mixed start on Wall Street\par later.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders and analysts were watching blow-by-blow media coverage of the war in\par Iraq, saying the market was less optimistic than when conflict began last\par week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market is now a pendulum, swaying between views of a long war and a\par short war in Iraq, but I think it's leaning towards a longer war," said a\par trader at a major Japanese bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A poll of 11 fund managers made by Reuters as the conflict got underway last\par week showed an average forecast that the war would last four weeks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Any signs of a long war could play on the market's biggest dollar fears -\par that the United States will be saddled with huge conflict-related costs in\par an environment where international investors are wary of sending savings\par abroad that could help finance its debts and its huge trade deficit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. President George W. Bush told lawmakers on Monday he urgently needed\par $75 billion to pay for the military campaign and to reward key allies for\par supporting the United States' war effort.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If accepted this could spell trouble for the U.S. budget deficit and the\par dollar longer term, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030325dz3p00id5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
