\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar hits Iraq war lows, gets no help from BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 08:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 25 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to its lowest levels against the yen and the euro on Tuesday since the start of the Iraq war. \par \par \uc2  Trade in Asia carried over a dollar-selling trend sparked by views that there will be no quick end to the fighting, although no fresh developments in the war appeared to be influencing the market. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose about 0.6 percent to a one-week high above $1.0700 driven up by an overall mood favouring dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  The dollar received no help from policy meetings by the Bank of Japan, which offered little reason to sell yen. \par \par \uc2  It started losing ground after an emergency meeting of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Policy Board provided no surprises, with yen bulls relieved that it did not adopt additional easing measures, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, holding its first policy-setting gathering under its new governor, Toshihiko Fukui, decided to leave monetary policy unchanged but to provide as much liquidity to the market as needed for the time being. \par \par \uc2  The market also showed little reaction to the board's decision to increase the BOJ's purchases of shares held by banks to three trillion yen ($24.85 billion) from the current two trillion yen, expanding a programme begun last year aimed at reducing banks' exposure to stock volatility. \par \par \uc2  Before the meeting, the yen had faced pressure on speculation that the BOJ would adopt more aggressive easing measures, such as buying bonds denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Once the outcome was clear the dollar started losing ground, dropping to a session low below 119.50 yen after hitting a series of stop-loss orders from 120.30 yen on. \par \par \uc2  "During the recent long (dollar) liquidation triggered by pessimism over the war, the dollar/yen rate had been left out of active selling ahead of the BOJ meeting," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of interbank sales at Trust and Custody Services Bank. \par \par \uc2  "But after confirming that the BOJ had refrained from taking extraordinary measures such as foreign bond buying, long holders unwound their positions. The market must been holding on to longs pretty heavily, having seen the dollar's sharp falls." \par \par \uc2  At 0620 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 119.50/55 yen compared with 120.65/68 yen late on Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0694/98 against $1.0634/40 in late New York. \par \par \uc2  WAR AND \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  But the market was nervous about selling the dollar too heavily amid wariness that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa reiterated his caution over speculative moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, although he said he did not see any such moves at present. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa made his comments in early trade when the dollar was standing well above 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's uptrend has been rather quick and I think the Japanese authorities must be very sensitive now," a senior Japanese trust bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "But it seems the dollar is currently very vulnerable amid the war," the dealer said. "The dollar could come under further pressure in overseas trading." \par \par \uc2  Dollar sentiment was hurt by its falls against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight, mirroring hefty falls in U.S. stock prices after U.S.-led forces seeking to oust Iraqi President Saddam Hussein faced stiffer resistance. \par \par \uc2  "The market is now a pendulum, swaying between views of a long war and a short war in Iraq, but I think it's leaning towards a longer war," said a trader at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  "It's now a difficult time to buy dollars," he added. \par \par \uc2  Any signs of a long war could play on the market's biggest dollar fears - that the United States will be saddled with huge conflict-related costs in an environment where international investors are wary of sending savings abroad that could help finance its debts and its huge trade deficit. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030325dz3p00eim\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
