\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar lower as market mulls longer Iraq war. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier David \par \uc2 732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 18:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 20 (Reuters) - The opening salvo of war against Iraq dented the dollar's momentum on Thursday, with dealers selling the dollar against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they mulled the potential for a drawn-out conflict. \par \par \uc2  Late on Wednesday, U.S. President George W. Bush ordered American and British forces to begin bombing select targets in Baghdad, the first stages of what he termed "a broad and concerted campaign" to topple Iraqi President Saddam Hussein. \par \par \uc2  Although the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately set new two-month highs against the euro and Swiss franc, it pared those gains as concerns grew among dealers that the war could be protracted and may involve heavy casualties. \par \par \uc2  "You had a prewar rally earlier. Now the market is waiting to look for direction," said David Schoenthal, senior managing director at Bear, Stearns in New York. He added that news that Iraqi soldiers had set fire to oil wells in the country further undermined the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Summing up the growing conviction among analysts that the dollar's fortunes hinge on the duration and precision of the conflict, Schoenthal added that if "war goes well and quickly, the dollar will rally, and global equity markets would rally too. The war goes badly, then you see the dollar weaken." \par \par \uc2  Indeed, in his speech from the Oval Office on Wednesday, Bush cautioned that the looming battles "could be longer and more difficult than some predict." The lowered expectations of a swift military campaign made for thin, volatile trading. \par \par \uc2  Europe's common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped from offshore trading highs but was still stronger on the day at $1.0620 , up 0.60 percent from its previous U.S. close and above its lowest level since mid-February at $1.0530. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also stumbled against the Swiss franc , trading near 1.3870 francs and down 0.50 percent on the day. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovered above the two-month high at 1.3810 reached in Asia trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar bought 120.10 yen , down 0.30 percent on the day but supported by fears that Japanese authorities could sell yen in order to keep it from strengthening too rapidly amid the uncertainties of war. \par \par \uc2  "To a large extent the war premium is simply being eroded from the market," said Tommy Molloy, trader at Bank Leumi in New York. "I believe it has further to go, but I don't think it will go as quickly as it has over the last week." \par \par \uc2  War news kept traders from paying much attention to data on the economy. U.S. initial jobless claims, which fell to 421,000 in the latest week from 425,000, had little impact on the market. \par \par \uc2  Additionally, traders largely ignored the release of the Philadelphia Fed Business index, which tumbled to 8.0 in March from 2.3 in February. \par \par \uc2  FIRST STRIKE \par \par \uc2  The United States initiated its military campaign against Iraq with a predawn missile and aircraft strike on Baghdad on Thursday. Reports suggested the strike on the Iraqi capital was limited so far and Britain said it would not be long before military action escalated. \par \par \uc2  A Kuwaiti defense ministry spokesman said an Iraqi Scud missile hit northern Kuwait on Thursday, following two earlier missile attacks that landed in the northern desert. \par \par \uc2  Analysts and traders said markets were looking for further indications as to how long the war might be, and many were reluctant to trade. \par \par \uc2  "For the first day, everyone is going to be glued to their TV screens, watching for every piece of information," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London. "After that, people will look at the bigger picture, and how long the war is going to last." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi said after the attacks on Iraq started that Japanese authorities would take appropriate action to avoid turbulence and excessive moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Japan would work with other G7 major industrialized nations if the war led to major disruptions in the world economic order, although he said Japan had not yet been in contact with other G7 nations. \par \par \uc2  Early in the session, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it stood ready to act if necessary in financial markets to assure "sufficient liquidity provision" during the uncertainty. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030320dz3k00y44\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
