\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar retreats from highs after US strikes begin. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 09:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 20 (Reuters) - The dollar fell around one percent on the euro and the Swiss franc, retreating from earlier two-month highs in nervous trade following news on Thursday U.S.-led forces had started a war on Iraq. \par \par \uc2  After initially spiking up to two-month highs when news of missile attacks in Iraq broke around 0230 GMT, the dollar then turned lower on the day to trade at $1.0630 per euro and 1.3825 Swiss francs by 0830 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has rallied strongly from four-year lows on the euro and franc in the past week as investors closed short dollar positions in preparation for the start of war. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar came under some pressure (at the start of European trade) on news the campaign had actually started," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  "But I don't think anyone's ready to throw in the towel on the dollar's rebound just yet, because they have to make an assessment as to how the bombing campaign is going." \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially neared the previous session's one-month high on the yen at 120.56 yen after news of the military strikes but turned half a percent lower on the day to 119.77. \par \par \uc2  The United States unleashed war on Iraq with dawn missile and aircraft strikes on Baghdad on Thursday. U.S. President George Bush said in a televised address the forces had struck "selected targets of military importance". Reports suggested the strike on the Iraqi capital was limited so far and Britain said it would not be long before military action escalated. \par \par \uc2  For a snapshot of the latest developments in the Iraq crisis, please click on. \par \par \uc2  MARKET JITTERS \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been driven down for months on the prospect of military action but hopes of a swift end to the crisis have lifted it in recent days. \par \par \uc2  It was steady for several minutes after initial reports of bombings and anti-aircraft gunfire in Baghdad before it then hit a two-month high of $1.0535. It subsequently dropped back as European trade got underway. \par \par \uc2  Bush, speaking some two hours after Saddam ignored a U.S. deadline to go into exile, said the war could be longer and more difficult than some had predicted, a comment which helped weigh the greenback down. \par \par \uc2  "There are some concerns the war might go on a little bit longer than expected, and terror alerts are increasing market nervousness," said a London-based trader. \par \par \uc2  "Trading is thin because people are sidelined. But I expect the market to move in lumps." \par \par \uc2  JAPAN WARNINGS \par \par \uc2  Traders in Asian said the market was careful about selling the greenback too aggressively with the lingering possibility of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen providing some support to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are there, but I don't expect the dollar would fall sharply and suddenly like we witnessed on September 11," said Mitsuru Saito, chief economist at UFJ Tsubasa Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "Unless the dollar's falls are unexpectedly large, I don't think other nations would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Saito said. \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi said the Japanese authorities would take appropriate action to avoid turbulence and excessive moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "We will closely watch moves in the market and respond appropriately to avoid excessive moves or turbulence," he told reporters after the attack on Iraq started. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Japan would work with other G7 nations if the war led to major disruptions in the world economic order, although he said Japan had not yet been in contact with other Group of Seven nations. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which holds a regular monetary policy meeting on Thursday, said it was monitoring financial markets in the same way it always did. \par \par \uc2  No interest rate rate decision is expected from Thursday's meeting. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss National Bank, which is concerned that franc strength will damage economic recovery, said it would watch developments in financial markets closely. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030320dz3k00haj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
