\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Iraq Crisis Nears Resolution \b0\par\par\uc2 1,663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 06:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Bush Makes It Clear: One Day Left\par DJIA          7859.71    gain 37.96     up 0.5%\par NASDAQ        1340.33    loss 0.44         0.0%\par S&P 500       833.27     gain 1.37      up 0.2%\par Dow Future    7784.00    loss 92.00     dn 1.2%\par NASDAQ Future 1021.00    loss 16.00     dn 1.5%\par S&P Future    824.00     loss 8.75      dn 1.1%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.70% dn 0.04\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Some momentum from Wall Street's big rally Thursday spilled over to Friday's session, lifting most stocks for a third straight day and helping the market snap a two-week losing streak. But Treasury prices closed higher on later signs of weakness in stocks. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: Investors exhibited some caution, not surprising after Thursday's gains that were the best the market had seen in five months. While investors are more confident that a U.S. war with Iraq would be short and successful, they were wary of making further commitments ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  And for good reason. On Sunday, U.S. President George W. Bush and summit partners from Britain and Spain gave the United Nations a Monday deadline to endorse the use of force to compel Iraq's immediate disarmament. \par \par \uc2  Pentagon officials are saying that the U.S. is at least two days away from war, but they won't speculate on a specific timetable so as not to compromise the tactical surprise of an attack, Cable News Network reported Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Iraqi leader Saddam Hussein warned Sunday that if Iraq is attacked, it will take the war anywhere in the world "wherever there is sky, land or water." \par \par \uc2  BONDS: Prices of Treasurys rebounded Friday as stocks pared earlier gains and safe-haven funds returned to government securities before the weekend and more Iraq developments. \par \par \uc2  Economic data and speculation over whether the Federal Reserve will adjust monetary policy this week had little effect on the market. \par \par \uc2  "The market is mostly keying off the Iraq story, which has brought some...flight-to-quality...back in. There seems to be some room for following through on the sell-off that started Thursday, but it's not going to happen right before a weekend," said Michele Girard, strategist at Prudential Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Japan Shares Fall On Iraq War Likelihood\par USD-Yen             117.85       loss  0.33    dn 0.3%\par AUD-USD             0.6016       gain  0.0036  up 0.6%\par Nikkei 225          7897.09      loss  105.50  dn 1.3%\par Hang Seng           8821.31      loss  134.86  dn 1.5%\par JGB Yield           0.7300% down 0.0150\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks slipped Monday morning as investors\par fretted over the weakening dollar and looming prospects for war in Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Technology shares were targeted by sellers because the weakening of\par the dollar against the yen was bad news for export-dependent industries.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Prices of Japanese and U.S. government bonds gained on war worries.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Shares To Surrender Gains\par Euro-USD            1.0820      gain  0.0050  up 0.5%\par Stlg-USD            1.5890      gain  0.0030  up 0.2%\par USD-Franc           1.3550      loss  0.0075  dn 0.5%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares are expected to start lower, with\par government bonds and the euro support by the likelihood of war in Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: Prospects for an Iraq war should push European shares to\par correct lower Monday after last week's hefty, short-covering-led gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Strategist Anais Faraj at Nomura Securities said Friday's rebound was\par more of a reflection of the market's inherent volatility than any change\par in sentiment. "Nothing has really changed. Hopes that the rally will\par extend much further are premature," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, Josef Ackermann, chief executive of Deutsche Bank AG, said\par the German government should make plans for a bailout amid what senior\par German bankers call the worst business environment in 50 years. "The\par best thing is if we do everything to ensure that none of this will be\par needed," Mr. Ackermann said. "But smart managers and smart government\par leaders must consider how to be prepared for the worst case," which\par would be the failure of a major bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices should emulate U.S. Treasury\par price gains overnight in Asia as the markets brace for an Iraq war.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-zone government bond prices fell Friday on stock market strength,\par with the bond market still hungover after Thursday's sharp sell-off.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Friday's weak U.S. data, while supporting Treasurys, failed to help\par European markets. "That's a sure sign that the bears are in control for\par now," one trader from a U.S. bank said, noting that technical and\par speculative funds remained the biggest sellers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But that may all change this week, at least temporarily until it\par becomes clear whether an Iraq war will be swift.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro certainly should get some support from the probability\par of hostilities in Iraq this week. But the support will be short-lived\par once the uncertainty over Iraq's future lifts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the meantime, wild swings are possible as the week's events unfold.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While twists and turns in the international arena will continue to\par overshadow economic concerns, markets will closely examine the decision\par by the policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee on Tuesday, analysts\par said. A rate cut is unlikely, but a majority of Wall Street banks expect\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will warn of economic weakness.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts cautioned the dollar's fate hinged largely on how broad a\par coalition the U.S. would be able to put together and how any military\par strike - if it occurs - plays out.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If it's a smaller coalition than the U.S. expected, it will be more\par of a burden to (the U.S.), and it puts more of a burden on the dollar,"\par said Bob Kozak, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst with Alaran Trading Corp. in Pompano\par Beach, Fla.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If it's quick, swift, and in-and-out, you could see the dollar take\par off," Kozak said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against this background, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts expect the euro to\par fluctuate in a range between $1.05 and $1.10 and estimated that the\par dollar would likely be trading between Y116.00 and Y120.00. While fears\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen have lessened,\par any movement towards the downside of the range will be viewed with\par suspicion, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, gold and oil prices rose after the Azores summit statement\par on Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Monday, March 17: German Consumer Prices\par ET/GMT\par 0800/1300 U.S.         March NY Fed Manufact