\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Iraq Delay Ups Anxiety \b0\par\par\uc2 1,441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 07:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: U.N. Vote Postponement Prolongs Uncertainty\par DJIA          7524.06    loss 44.12     dn 0.6%\par NASDAQ        1271.47    loss 6.90      dn 0.5%\par S&P 500       800.73     loss 6.75      dn 0.8%\par Dow Future    7538.00    gain 18.00     up 0.2%\par NASDAQ Future 959.50     gain 1.50      up 0.2%\par S&P Future    801.00     gain 1.70      up 0.2%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.58% up 0.02\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Wall Street extended its slump Tuesday after the United States said it would delay a vote on its U.N. resolution on Iraq. The U.S. move raised investors' fears of prolonged tensions with Saddam Hussein. But Treasury investors saw an opportunity in the delay to take some profit. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: "There's a buyer's strike going on, accounting for the low volume in the markets right now," said Jim Russell, director of core equity strategy for Fifth-Third Bank in Cincinnati. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the United States and Britain delayed a vote on giving Saddam an ultimatum to disarm after the countries determined they lacked the minimum nine "yes" votes for passage. \par \par \uc2  They signaled they were willing to compromise to try to win support from Security Council members who oppose a rush to war, but discounted a 45-day delay sought by six swing nations on the Security Council. \par \par \uc2  "The direction for the market overall is going to be sideways until it looks like we come to some resolution, be it war or be it a commitment to long-term inspections and some type of containment in Iraq," said Brian Bush, director of equity research at Stephens Inc. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: After recent heady gains, U.S. Treasury prices came back towards earth Tuesday, posting losses in most issues, although the diplomatic chess game surrounding a possible war with Iraq limited the declines. \par \par \uc2  Reports hinting at weakness in the economy also limited selling. \par \par \uc2  The extremely high price levels made Treasurys look at least temporarily, less attractive to investors. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think many people want to buy at this point," said Michael Trefel, a technical analyst at Lehman Brothers in New York. "The market is so overextended that those people who are long are already nervous and starting to take off some of the positions." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Japan Shares Back From The Brink\par USD-Yen      x      124.04       gain  0.11    up 0.09%\par AUD-USD      x      0.5666       gain  0.0016  up 0.3%\par Nikkei 225          7974.35      gain  111.93  up 1.4%\par Hang Seng           8879.59      gain  19.66   up 0.2%\par JGB Yield           0.7150% down 0.0150\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo shares rebounded Wednesday morning from a\par 20-year low reached a day earlier amid media reports that the government\par was planning new measures to support stock prices. The dollar gained\par against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Local prices gained after the major Tokyo daily, the Asahi Shimbun,\par reported the Finance Ministry and the Financial Services Agency had\par decided to ask the Bank of Japan to increase its purchases of stocks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The newspaper also said authorities decided to request life insurance\par companies stop lending shares to others - for margin trading purposes -\par to prevent market speculation from driving down share prices. The ruling\par political parties are considering similar proposals, the newspaper\par added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Senior bureaucrats from the agencies and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  met\par Wednesday morning reportedly to discuss the steps.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Major automakers Honda, Toyota, and Nissan were all slightly higher,\par while telecommunications giants NTT and NTT DoCoMo also gained.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, traders said market sentiment was weak because of continuing\par concerns about the effect a war in Iraq would have on Japan's fragile\par economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In response to the stock market's recent steep falls, the Bank of\par Japan pumped extra liquidity into the financial system to maintain\par steady money market rates ahead of the fiscal year-end March 31.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In early money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  boosted commercial\par banks' account balances at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by an extra Y700 billion to\par Y22.3 trillion. This brings the account balance to its highest level\par since similar moves a year ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Officials have called for calm and said they are ready to step in to\par support the market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The government may take more potentially aggressive policies toward\par the end of the month," said Brian Rubens, managing director at Nomura\par Securities International in New York. "But the BOJ won't, by themselves,\par be turning things around."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Japan already has slashed interest rates to near zero and\par has been flooding the banking system with cash to encourage lending to\par struggling companies. But most analysts agree that pumping more money\par into the financial sector will do little without fixing commercial\par banks' bad-loan problems, streamlining government regulations to\par encourage new businesses and wiping out waste in government.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Many Japanese shares have been bogged down by a trend of corporate\par investors shedding holdings in their longtime business partners, called\par cross-shareholdings.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Rubens said the sell-off was peaking as banks seek to unload such\par shares before the end of the fiscal year on March 31.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We're reasonably confident that things are going to turn around later\par this week," he said. "We believe it'll get back above 8,000-level by the\par end of the week."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese core machinery orders rose 7.0% in January from December, the\par Cabinet Office said Wednesday, supporting the government's assessment\par that business outlays have stopped declining.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Markets Await Clarity On Iraq Debate\par Euro-USD    x       0.9574      gain  0.0004  up 0.03%\par Stlg-USD    x       1.4912      gain  0.0004  up 0.01%\par USD-Franc   x       1.5363      loss  0.0005  dn 0.05%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares should open slightly higher;\par government bonds and the euro mixed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: Heavily oversold European shares are due for a snapback, if\par only a slight one, on technical grounds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The lull in Iraq developments should allow oversold technicals to take\par hold and encourage some short-covering in especially beleaguered shares\par such as insurance issues.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks fell Tuesday as an attempt by financial stocks to\par rebound from recent severe losses was offset by negative corporate\par updates. Those included profit warnings from Dutch publisher Wolters\par Kluwer and German carmaker Volkswagen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein said in a note that even\par if the current geopolitical uncertainty is resolved, there is unlikely\par to be a strong pickup in economic activity. They argue that the\par continued erosion of corporate profitability hardly augurs well for any\par recovery in hiring or an upswing in investment in 2003.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Indeed the recent behavior of profits helps explain why companies\par continue aggressively to downsize their labor requirements, as the U.S.\par February employment report released last Friday demonstrated so\par graphically," they said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices should open little changed,\par balanced by new supply and Iraq worries.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-zone government bonds fell Tuesday on profit-taking, but ended\par off their session lows.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par More pressure is being felt in anticipation of five-year bond auctions\par from Germany Wednesday and Italy Thursday. The five-year sector of the\par curve underperformed 10-year securities by about two basis points\par Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro remains in a tight range against the dollar while\par uncertainty surrounding the outcome of a United Nations vote on a second\par resolution on Iraq complicates market direction.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ongoing speculation that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be covertly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par to weaken the yen contributed to choppy trade, helping the dollar\par rebound and stay above the key psychological level of Y117.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But an analyst detected little conviction behind the dollar's pause.\par "There are no fundamentals that explain these movements," said Ashraf\par Laidi, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at MG Financial in New York. "It does\par not take away from the general negative tone of the dollar in light of\par the uncertainty in the Security Council," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Wednesday, March 12: France Industrial Production\par ET/GMT\par 0245/0745 France       January Industrial Production\par 0300/0800 Italy        Jan PPI (seen +0.4% MM, +1.9% YY)\par 0300/0800 Italy        Final Feb CPI (seen +2.6% YY)\par 0400/0900 Germany      Annual VDMA press conference\par 0500/1000 E.U.         MEP Ingo Friedrich press conference\par 0700/1200 U.S.         MBA Refinancing Index (last +10.4)\par 0830/1330 U.S.         Jan Trade Deficit (seen $43.3 Bln)\par    N/A   U.S.          ABC/Money Consumer Confidence\par    N/A   France        Bank Of France Monthly Survey\par    N/A   Germany       Bundesbank allocates EUR7 Bln\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1011 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030312dz3c000ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
