\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Asia acts and frets as financial markets swoon. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Wheatley, Asian Economics Correspondent \par \uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 09:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Recasts after Japan, Malaysia steps and South Korean comments) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 11 (Reuters) - Governments across Asia resorted to a mixture of action and promises on Tuesday to soothe market nerves jangled by swooning share prices and deteriorating growth prospects. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumped extra cash into the country's banking system, shortly before Malaysia unveiled a package of measures to bolster its capital markets and so provide some protection from the fallout of any war against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  With leading share indices falling to 20-year lows in Tokyo and a 17-month trough in Seoul, authorities in both capitals stepped up their vigilance but disappointed those investors looking for decisive steps to stabilise the markets. \par \par \uc2  "We will take bold actions if the situation calls for them. We cannot say what exactly, but as policymakers we are always considering various steps," Japan's chief cabinet secretary, Yasuo Fukuda, told reporters in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The drop in Seoul stocks, accompanied by a fall in the won \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its lowest level since October, is proving a baptism of fire for new President Roh Moo-Hyun, who also faces a crisis over North Korea's nuclear and missile programmes. \par \par \uc2  "We have changed our stance to 'a close watch' from 'wait and see' as instability in financial markets deepens," South Korean Vice Finance Minister Kim Gwang-lim said. \par \par \uc2  But Kim's boss, Finance Minister Kim Jin-pyo, said after a meeting with the heads of major South Korean banks that the government was not yet considering market-boosting measures, in part due to fears of restoking a credit boom that brought Asia's fourth-largest economy to the brink of overheating last year. \par \par \uc2  "There are limits to boosting consumption, since there still are threats posed by household loans and high property prices," he said. \par \par \uc2  RESILENT ASIA \par \par \uc2  Asia's economies have shown impressive resilience in the face of subdued global demand. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, data on Tuesday showed Thai consumer confidence rose in February to its highest level since the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis, while Chinese industrial output jumped 19.8 percent from a year earlier - the fastest clip since at least 1996. \par \par \uc2  But investors are nervous that a looming war against Iraq will deal a body blow to a region that is a thirsty oil importer and highly dependent on exports for growth. \par \par \uc2  Investment bank JP Morgan Chase said it had lowered its forecast for growth this year in Asia excluding China and India by half a percentage point to 4.2 percent in anticipation of a marked export slowdown in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  "The downdraft could be exacerbated if the hit to sentiment among the region's consumers and investors is more pronounced than we currently believe. Consequently, domestic demand may suffer a more dramatic pullback than we have incorporated in our forecasts," JP Morgan Chase economists Bill Belchere and David Fernandez wrote in a report. \par \par \uc2  Japan is in especially dire straits because tumbling share prices erode the capital of the country's struggling banks. \par \par \uc2  To keep markets calm, the Bank of Japan pumped an extra one trillion yen ($8.56 billion) into the banking system on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "We supplied additional funds...to prevent uncertainties in the stock market, including those from the Iraq situation, from spilling over to the short-term money market," a senior official from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Japan's other headache is the yen, which is rising because of the country's massive savings surplus, thereby threatening the profitability of export titans like Toyota Motor Corp \par \par \uc2  The Nihon Keizai Shimbun business daily reported that the government and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were looking at \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of up to one trillion yen a day to push down the value of the yen. \par \par \uc2  "As a matter of policy, if there are rapid movements we will move immediately and actively, although we will leave rates to market forces if there aren't rapid movements," said Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, who on Monday blamed stock price falls on a lack of morals among securities firms. \par \par \uc2  MALAYSIAN MOVES \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Malaysia unveiled measures to make its capital markets more investor-friendly. Stamp duty paid on stock market deals will be capped and initial public offerings speeded up. \par \par \uc2  Bankers also expect Malaysia to raise $500 million through a global bond issue to fund an economic package later this month aimed at cushioning its economy from a possible war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, financial markets speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would advance its quarterly rate-setting meeting to March 20. \par \par \uc2  If the Federal Reserve cuts U.S. interest rates at its own policy meeting on March 18 - as a number of investment banks expect - Taiwan would be in a position to quickly follow suit, though local market opinion was mixed as to whether it would actually do so. \par \par \uc2  Not everyone welcomed the policy activism. \par \par \uc2  Either the stock market turbulence is temporary, and will dissipate assuming a war against Iraq is over quickly, or it is symptomatic of deeper-seated problems that should not be tackled by direct support measures, said Dominique Dwor-Frecaut, a regional economist with Barclays Capital in Singapore. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030311dz3b00gtr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
