\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar keeps off lows on  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2  jitters. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 04:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 11 (Reuters) - The dollar kept off recent lows in Asia on Tuesday morning thanks largely to speculation that Japanese authorities may step up their campaign to stem the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  The Nihon Keizai Shimbun business daily reported the government and Bank of Japan were prepared to steady the domestic stock market by introducing measures that could include massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Still, the greenback was plagued by expectations that Washington will soon wage war on Iraq, as well as concerns - sparked by poor U.S. job data last week - that continued U.S. economic growth is far from guaranteed even if a war ends quickly. \par \par \uc2  "We recently revised down our forecasts for the U.S. economy. There are growing worries over the U.S. economy not just because of Iraq," said Minori Takeuchi, vice president of research at J.P. Morgan Chase. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's quite possible the market will test the determination of Japanese authorities," he said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0225 GMT, the dollar was at 116.85/90 yen a tad higher than Monday's late New York levels and a half yen off last week's seven-month low of around 116.36. \par \par \uc2  The euro also eased slightly to $1.1043/48 compared with $1.1055/59 in late U.S. trade but still within a ( cent of last week's four-year high of $1.1069. \par \par \uc2  The euro was flat versus the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 129.09/16 yen \par \par \uc2  MASSIVE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? \par \par \uc2  Dollar bears were held in check by speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has admitted it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  covertly several times in January and February, might step up its effort to curb the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials worry that excessive strength in the yen could hurt exports, seen as the main support for a faltering economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Traders think the government is deeply worried about the health of the Tokyo stock market. The Nikkei share average fell below the 8,000 mark for the first time since 1983 on Monday - just three weeks before the end-March book-closing for most Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  Speculation about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was fuelled further by a Nihon Keizai Shimbun report that the Finance Ministry is considering massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of about 1.0 trillion yen ($8.56 billion) a day. \par \par \uc2  That would would be substantially bigger than \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far. Traders estimate \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent one to two trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. \par \par \uc2  "Theoretically, if the authorities want to stop the yen's appreciation, they can sell yen as much as they want (by printing more yen). There's no limit," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  When asked about the possibility of massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately if it sees undesirable volatility in exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  Whether massive or not, traders think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  any time the dollar hovers below 117 yen, with 115 yen seen as the most important level for Japan to protect. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's pretty clear to anyone who has seen a dollar/yen chart that the dollar could dive once it breaks below 115 yen," said Kimura. \par \par \uc2  NEW DEADLINE? \par \par \uc2  Still, the dollar's health is still tied to developments concerning Iraq, with all eyes on Washington's attempt to enlist the help of other United Nations' members to disarm Iraq, by force if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Diplomats at the U.N. said on Monday the six undecided members of the Security Council had weighed delaying a deadline for Iraqi compliance to April 17, a month later than demanded in a U.S.-British-Spanish draft resolution. \par \par \uc2  If the six were to agree on a compromise plan, this could interfere with Washington's hopes for a vote as early as Tuesday on a draft resolution that would give Baghdad until March 17 to fully cooperate with U.N. disarmament demands or face war. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say that concerns about the economic impact of waging a war with limited financial support from a smaller coalition of nations - as well as the possibility of new terror attacks on U.S. soil - would exacerbate the weakness of the U.S. economy, the stock market and the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It's not just that a war may be about to begin but also that there is growing concern about the U.S. economy even if the war ends swiftly," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030311dz3b0060t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
