\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Iraq Mess Gets More Muddled \b0\par\par\uc2 2,343 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 06:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Iraq Jockeying Leaves Investors At A Loss\par DJIA          7568.18    loss 171.85    dn 2.2%\par NASDAQ        1278.37    loss 26.92     dn 2.1%\par S&P 500       807.48     loss 21.41     dn 2.6%\par Dow Future    7557.00    loss 3.00         0.0%\par NASDAQ Future 965.50     loss 1.00      dn 0.1%\par S&P Future    806.00     loss 1.20      dn 0.2%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.56% dn 0.06\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Stocks tumbled Monday, pulling the Dow Jones industrial average down more than 170 points, after France said it was prepared to vote against a U.N. resolution on Iraq. Treasurys rallied on the uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  President Bush's urgent phone campaign to world leaders, seeking their support for a tough deadline on Iraq, came up short Monday - forcing a delay of the Security Council's vote and opening the doors to a possible compromise to give Saddam Hussein more time. \par \par \uc2  The United States had hoped to present the resolution to the council on Tuesday, setting a March 17 deadline for Iraqi disarmament or war. But the vote was put on hold when it became evident that America and its allies had not yet won the nine votes they needed for a majority. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: For investors, the French announcement raised the prospect of the United States going to war with Iraq without support from its allies. \par \par \uc2  "It is just a very gloomy picture," said Peter Cardillo, president and chief strategist of Global Partner Securities Inc. "Certainly the attitude, the psychology, the mood on Wall Street is one of gloom and doom, and that is continuing." \par \par \uc2  The declines were in keeping with the steep selloffs the market has suffered all year due to uncertainty about war and the impact it would have on the already shaky economy. Trading volume was very light, another sign that investors refuse to make major commitments. \par \par \uc2  "The irrational despair seems to imply that there is no floor to stock prices. The public without question has retreated psychologically and monetarily. ... The risk aversion is quite dominant," said Ned Riley, chief investment strategist at State Street Global Advisors. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: Treasury prices rallied into record territory Monday, with the yield on the benchmark 10-year note closing at its lowest level in 44 years. \par \par \uc2  After a weekend of diplomatic shuffling against the backdrop of the steady drumbeat surrounding war with Iraq, the wave of buying of Treasurys was further driven by North Korea's cruise missile test and slumping U.S. equities. \par \par \uc2  The related uncertainty this is creating for the sluggish U.S. economy also continued to support Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, hints of further rate cuts at the Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee meeting March 18, by non-voting Fed Bank of St. Louis President William Poole, also bolstered the front end of the Treasury curve. However, the overriding theme remained one of safe haven buying ahead of a looming war in Iraq, said traders. \par \par \uc2  "The market will keep attracting a bid in the lead up to war and the downside will remain limited," said David Ging, fixed income strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo Shares Slide Again\par USD-Yen             116.90       gain  0.08    up 0.07%\par AUD-USD             0.6144       loss  0.0003  dn 0.07\par Nikkei 225          7891.15      loss  151.11  dn 1.9%\par Hang Seng           8810.47      loss  39.75   dn 0.4%\par JGB Yield           0.7350% down 0.0200\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks fell again Tuesday on the usual Iraq\par worry but also on a view that the Japanese government has been slow in\par tackling the country's economic problems.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said investor sentiment remains poor in the wake of Monday's\par fall in New York shares after France said it was prepared to vote\par against a U.N. resolution on Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Underscoring the possibility the United States may go to war without\par the backing of the United Nations and key allies, the statement rattled\par investors.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading early Tuesday, the U.S. dollar gained slightly\par after Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Tokyo stands\par ready to step in to the market to curb rapid yen gains against the\par dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If there are sudden rises or falls, we must be ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par immediately," Shiokawa told a regular press conference early Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in recent weeks to\par prevent the yen's rise from snuffing out the nation's fragile economic\par recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Treasury prices were slightly higher in late Tokyo Tuesday due to\par buying of mainly long-end papers on growing hopes for Federal Reserve\par monetary easing, bond traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Markets Reassess Latest Iraq Dramatics\par Euro-USD            1.1045      loss  0.0013  dn 0.1%\par Stlg-USD            1.6035      gain  0.0020  up 0.1%\par USD-Franc           1.3265      gain  0.0016  up 0.1%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European stocks and government bond prices should\par mostly consolidate this session, while the euro also pauses.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares should start slightly lower but then\par consolidate after massive losses of late.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks remained mired at six-year lows Monday as concerns\par over potential global economic recovery and corporate profits were\par heightened by widening cracks in transatlantic relations over the issue\par of war with Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investors steered clear of equities ahead of a U.K.-U.S. March 17\par deadline for Iraq to disarm or face war.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Unless events unfold in a remarkably benign and timely manner, the\par downward pull on equities from the surge in oil prices seems likely to\par be with us for some time yet. Tactical preparation for a 20% to 25%\par short-covering rally is one thing; calling for an end to the bear market\par is quite another," said Anais Faraj strategist at Nomura Securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Attention will shift - at least briefly - to Nokia, which delivers its\par mid-quarter update.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bonds are expected to open mixed to\par slightly higher as the market reassesses how quickly a war in Iraq might\par happen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-zone government bonds rose Monday as stronger Treasurys and\par struggling equities helped the European market extend early gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There is a feeling that war with Iraq is likely regardless of whether\par the U.S. gets U.N. backing or not at the next vote," said one dealer\par with a U.K. bank. "It means that any dips in bond prices will be met\par with buying and investors are steering clear of equities," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Pakistan will abstain from voting in the U.N. Security Council on a\par U.S.-backed resolution approving war with Iraq, a spokesman for the\par ruling party said Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Adopting a two-pronged strategy to avert a U.N. defeat, the Bush\par administration once again portrayed Iraq as in violation of\par international demands it disarm, but signaled a willingness to revise\par its troubled resolution endorsing the use of force.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : It's not that the euro has a lot going for it. It's more that\par just about everything is working against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar fell against most major rivals on Monday amid an\par intensification of negative sentiment surrounding a looming war against\par Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It did manage to pare some losses late in the session on a report that\par the Bank of Japan is planning massive yen sales.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese government and the BOJ are discussing ways to stabilize\par the stock market in the wake of a decline that sent the benchmark Nikkei\par Stock Average below 8,000 for the first time in 20 years, the Nihon\par Keizai Shimbun reported in its Tuesday morning edition.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With the dollar steadily losing ground against the euro and Tokyo\par considering aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow a rise in the yen, euro-yen\par should extend its recent gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Since a mid-August low at Y114.29, the euro-yen pair exhibited\par characteristics of a bull market - a series of higher highs and higher\par lows. To continue with this healthy uptrend the euro must eclipse a Feb.\par 13 high at Y130.77.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Tuesday, March 11: German, UK Trade; Fed Indices\par ET/GMT\par 0200/0700 Germany   Jan Current Account (seen EUR3.7 Bln)\par 0200/0700 Germany   January Trade Balance\par 0245/0745 France    Fourth-Quarter New 