\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL YIELD: Japan Tackles The Ides Of March, Again \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 904 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 23:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Japan's struggling economy is facing the Ides of March once more with its fiscal year end looming, the yen firming and Tokyo stocks sliding to 20-year lows. \par \par \uc2  Often Japan has managed to run the gauntlet of this difficult time of year, when Japanese banks and companies are forced to sell foreign assets and repatriate the proceeds into yen in order to offset their mounting losses. The country has a tendency to somehow muddle through a potentially treacherous financial minefield. \par \par \uc2  But this time Japan may have even more reason to beware, as spreading weakness in the global economy could exacerbate Japan's escalating economic woes more than ever. \par \par \uc2  "They know how to plug the hole so that they forestall the true crisis for another year, but with what's happening in the global financial system, it is going to be tough," to make that approach work, said Jason Bonanca, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Credit Suisse First Boston in New York. \par \par \uc2  Before the added twist of a firming \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in Japan things look particularly bleak, even by the dismal standards of the country's last deflation-dogged decade. The government is desperate to avoid a slide in Tokyo stocks to 20-year lows from crippling bank balance sheets as they struggle to write off nonperforming loans. \par \par \uc2  This need is perhaps even more urgent considering reform efforts to force banks to write off their huge Y236 trillion slew of bad debts have been mostly half-baked. \par \par \uc2  With the global economy paralyzed by war jitters and growth in the euro zone barely chugging along, the U.S. economy is looking increasingly like it may not even recover convincingly later this year. Partly as a result of that softer outlook, the dollar is being punished. \par \par \uc2  For Japanese policy makers and companies, that's having the unwelcome effect of boosting the yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, threatening to snuff out any fragile signs of export-led recovery in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  altogether. \par \par \uc2  Even the threat of massive yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to follow the covert \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  undertook in January and February in an effort to cap the yen's rise, may only have a short-lived effect. \par \par \uc2  "We are poised to actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market (by selling the yen for dollars), once the value of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  begins to run up rapidly," the Nihon Keizai Shimbun's Tuesday evening edition quoted Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa as saying. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Band Aid Approach\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yet such measures designed to soften the yen will soon lose their\par impact and leave Japan's creaking economy under renewed pressure,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists warn.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even in the short term, the potential effects of such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are\par fairly limited, warns Andrew Chaveriat, a technical \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par analyst with BNP Paribas in New York. An \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -driven "rally\par might be good for two or three yen", pushing the dollar up to around\par Y120.0 from its Y117.10 level late Tuesday in New York, Chaveriat said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which acts via the BOJ in such\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market actions, "might be consigned to just slowing the\par dollar's decline."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This leaves Japan desperate to implement other measures that might\par alleviate pain on the stock market and ultimately help dress up bank\par balance sheets, analysts reckon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nikkei 225 stock average closed at 7862 points Tuesday, dropping\par clearly below the critical 8000 points mark oft cited as a pain\par threshold for Japanese banks, which have extensive equity holdings. If\par the Nikkei were to remain at these levels through the end of the month,\par that would blow a hole through banks' balance sheets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Keenly aware of this, the government has decided to ask the Bank of\par Japan to substantially increase the volume of stocks it's buying from\par private banks, the Asahi Shimbun newspaper reported on its Web site\par Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ introduced the stock-buying initiative last year, setting\par aside Y2 trillion for the program, and has so far bought shares worth\par about Y1 trillion from banks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In spite of such last-ditch efforts to forestall a full blown crisis\par in Japan's banking sector, the deterioration  of an already dire outlook\par for Japan's banks has caught  credit ratings agencies attention.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although over the past year or so, Japanese banks have shown "a\par slightly more earnest approach to reducing the problem loans...the\par economic environment doesn't help the banking system," said Fred Puorro,\par a banking analyst with Fitch Ratings in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One reflection of how banks' core lending business has withered away,\par in line with sagging consumer demand, is how banks continue to turn to\par the Japanese government bond market as one of the few ways of generating\par returns. So great is the demand for JGBs, also fired by expectations the\par BOJ might increase its JGB purchases as a quantitative easing measure,\par that the benchmark 10-year JGB's yield declined to record lows around\par 0.74% Tuesday. Bond yields and prices move inversely.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yet here too, dangers lurk. U.S.-based fixed income strategists warn\par of a bubble forming in JGB prices that could burst with dramatic effect\par if inflationary pressures return to the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Takeshi Takeuchi in Washington contributed to this report)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4083 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030311dz3b00d49\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
