\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Officials seen letting US$ weakness run its course. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 16:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 10 (Reuters) - Monetary authorities in some countries are trying to talk down their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to minimise economic damage caused by a weakening dollar, but further softening of the greenback is unlikely to provoke joint action. \par \par \uc2  In fact, analysts say, some central banks may quietly welcome a weaker dollar, hoping dollar weakness may actually lend a helping hand to enfeebled industrialised economies once the short-term shock has worn off. \par \par \uc2  If U.S. manufacturers can take advantage of the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise from persistent doldrums, that could end up bolstering the economic growth outlook throughout the major economies. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone will have to grin and bear a weaker dollar," said Adrian Hughes, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "We live in a world where the U.S. is key economic driver to the world economy and for everyone else to come out of stagnation the weak dollar could help that," he said. \par \par \uc2  In addition, stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the euro, will also reduce the impact of higher oil prices as a result of a likely war with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  And finally, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) may welcome the strengthening euro as a gratifying riposte to all those who taunted the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as lacking credibility while it was falling after its inception. \par \par \uc2  TINKERING ON THE EDGES \par \par \uc2  Switzerland and Norway cut interest rates last week in a bid to stave off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation resulting from declines in the dollar, and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has admitted to covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  But few attributed the ECB's modest 25 basis point rate cut on March 6 to concern about the resurgent euro, and ECB officials have been conspicuously quiet about the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength against the greenback. "The euro's fall (after its introduction) was seen to reflect a lack of credibility and its rise now is welcomed by the ECB," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westdeutsche Landesbank in London. \par \par \uc2  "They can now argue the decline in euro/dollar was related to factors other than a lack of credibility. But I strongly disagree," he said. \par \par \uc2  Without the ECB, any joint effort to stem the dollar's fall could be difficult. Even if it agreed to such action, few thought it could succeed in bolstering the dollar whose strength is seen as sapped not only by the risk of war but also by deteriorating fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "There is not going to be any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to put support under the dollar," said David Brown, chief economist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  "The Bush administration has not actually supported a weak dollar but de facto they have. It can boost an export-led recovery and anything makes a difference... And at this juncture the ECB is not going to get involved because given the fundamentals \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will make little difference." \par \par \uc2  HANDS OFF \par \par \uc2  The latest meeting of the rich-nation G7 club appeared to back expectations that little concerted effort could be expected to counter the dollar's fall. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues were low on the agenda when G7 finance ministers met in Paris in February, in stark contrast to a 1987 meeting when the countries agreed in the "Louvre Accord" to prop up the dollar with \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We are not at the stage when we are going to have coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Louvre Accord," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  "Not until we move towards the levels from the mid-1990s." \par \par \uc2  The dollar is hovering around its lowest levels in four years against the Swiss franc and the euro, at 1.3237 francs and $1.1067 per euro respectively. \par \par \uc2  Synthetic euro/dollar charts show the greenback weakened to around $1.35 per euro in 1995 and, on the same measure, was around $1.45 in 1992, according to Reuters calculations. \par \par \uc2  So far, the dollar is down 19 percent against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the start of 2002. Against the euro alone, the dollar has shed more than five percent so far this year. However, on the U.S. Federal Reserve's broader index, which includes emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it has shed only 3.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  WHAT IF... \par \par \uc2  A host of economic issues, including market concern about the U.S. "twin deficits" - the budget and current account gaps - as well as worries over the cost of war with Iraq, are expected to weigh on the dollar longer term. \par \par \uc2  And if this decline takes the greenback near its rate against the euro when the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was launched, then analysts say the issue of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may resurface again. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar has already retraced more than half of its losses from the launch level of $1.1747 in January 1999 to its lowest ever level of $0.8225 set in 2000, and it is expected by many analysts to march on. \par \par \uc2  In Germany, the euro zone's traditional powerhouse now badly hit by the economic slowdown, exporters are already complaining about lost export competitiveness due to the stronger euro and their cries are likely to grow louder. \par \par \uc2  "If we break through the (euro's) opening level, then it will become a problem," said Commerzbank's Sharma. "Then we could think about concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030310dz3a00w3q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
