\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-INTERVIEW-Japan on "red alert" on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  shifts-Watanabe. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Satomi Noguchi and Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (repeats to fix format of first para, adds more quotes) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 10 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be on "red alert" if there are unprecedented moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a senior official said on Monday, suggesting the ministry was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further to hold the yen down. \par \par \uc2  "If there are unprecedented moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market we will be on 'red alert'," said Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the ministry's international bureau, when asked about possible market volatility after a U.S. attack on Iraq or because of other factors. \par \par \uc2  His comments came after the yen rose to seven-month highs around 116.35 yen  to the dollar last Friday as worries over an imminent war in Iraq pummelled the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Japanese authorities have been concerned that a runaway rise in the yen could derail Japan's export-led economic recovery and have disclosed that they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January and February. \par \par \uc2  Watanabe told Reuters in an interview that he had no preconceived idea about what would happen in the market, but said he was carefully watching moves. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the yen approached Friday's high again despite a drop in the Tokyo stock market's Nikkei average  below the 8,000 mark for the first time in 20 years. \par \par \uc2  "It's abnormal that the yen strengthens while Japanese share prices drop. That will be undesirable for the Japanese economy," Watanabe said. \par \par \uc2  Market players think Japan does not want to let the dollar fall below 117 yen, and certainly not below 115 yen, seen as a crucial level for Japanese exporters' profitability. \par \par \uc2  But Watanabe said the ministry did not have any target level. \par \par \uc2  "We don't have any specific level in mind that is appropriate for the dollar/yen. We will act when the rate moves rapidly and widely." \par \par \uc2  The ministry has not been trying to manipulate exchange rates to boost share prices, he added. \par \par \uc2  "We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...to make market moves more predictable," Watanabe said, explaining that the authorities were trying to combat what they saw as erratic moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Officials have declined to say if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  In a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, the Japanese authorities only disclosed their transactions at the end of each month, departing from their practice in recent years of immediately announcing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030310dz3a00izl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
