\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Watanabe says Japan on 'red alert' on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  shifts. \b0\par\par\uc2 179 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 09:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 10 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be on "red alert" if there are unprecedented moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a senior official said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "If there are unprecedented moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market we will be on 'red alert'," Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the ministry's international bureau, said when asked about possible market volatility after a U.S. attack on Iraq or other factors. \par \par \uc2  He told Reuters in an interview that he had no preconceived idea about what would happen in the market, but said he was carefully watching moves. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in January and February to curb the yen's strength, which officials worry could hurt exports. \par \par \uc2  Officials have declined to say if Japan also acted last week, when the dollar fell to seven-month lows against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Watanabe said: "We don't have any specific level in mind that is appropriate for the dollar/yen. We will act when the rate moves rapidly and widely." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030310dz3a00had\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
