\par\par \b \uc2  War Worries Send Dollar Lower Against Yen and Euro \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,100 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 00:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar fell against most major rivals Monday amid an intensification of negative sentiment surrounding a looming war against Iraq, but managed to pare some losses late in the session on a report that the Bank of Japan is planning massive yen sales. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government and the Bank of Japan are discussing ways to stabilize the stock market in the wake of a decline that sent the benchmark Nikkei Stock Average below 8,000 briefly Monday for the first time in 20 years, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported in its Tuesday morning edition. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan is prepared, if necessary, to provide liquidity well above its current target level as Japan's March 31 fiscal-year close approaches. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , for its part, is mulling the option of massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the tune of one trillion yen a day. \par \par \uc2  That report "seemed to spook the market," allowing the dollar to claw back some of its losses against the yen and recover somewhat against other rivals, said Grant Wilson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh. \par \par \uc2  As Japan's ailing banks approach the country's fiscal year end on March 31, some may need to be rescued, should the Nikkei average fall in sustained fashion below the 8000- point mark, denting their extensive equity portfolios. \par \par \uc2  "We will avoid a financial-system crisis under any circumstances," Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi told reporters Monday. \par \par \uc2  Traders suspect that at current levels, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already covertly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a regular basis in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in an effort to weaken the yen - as it did in January and February - in order to avoid the dollar slipping below the 115-yen mark. That area has oft been cited as a critical pain threshold, below which Japanese exporters' competitiveness would suffer. \par \par \uc2  However, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts would probably come before the dollar slipped that far. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, which acts on the ministry's behalf in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will be very keen on keeping the dollar above 116 yen, a figure which will be very, very keenly watched," said Thomas Molloy, a trader at Bank Leumi in New York. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday in New York, the dollar was at 116.83 yen, off its intraday low of 116.42 yen, but down from 117.06 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.1055, up from $1.1005 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3249 francs, near fresh four-year lows and sharply down from 1.3350 francs late Friday. Sterling was at $1.6012, modestly down from $1.6032, as the pound continued to lose its edge on United Kingdom Prime Minister Tony Blair's slipping popularity and threats of ministerial resignations over his backing for U.S. President George W. Bush in any military action against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Escalating nerves ahead of a critical United Nations' vote expected this week that could pave the way for, or precipitate, military action against Iraq as well as hangover from the dismal U.S. employment report released Friday continued to pressure the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar remains under a lot of pressure and the uncertainty surrounding Iraq is obviously one big factor," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Barings Capital Markets in New York. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders also are turning their attention to the next Federal Reserve policy meeting on March 18, at which some economists are now expecting a rate cut in response to the deteriorating U.S. economic outlook and the seemingly imminent threat of an U.S.-led military campaign. This outlook may send Treasury bond yields even lower and could further detract from the appeal of dollar-denominated fixed-income assets, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 10-year Treasury's yield - which moves inversely to its price - hit a new 44-year low of 3.56% Monday. Treasury yields fell, partly in reaction to comments by Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis President William Poole, who said Monday there is a "strong case" for the Federal Reserve to make an interest rate cut at the Fed's next policy-setting meeting on March 18. \par \par \uc2  One reason Mr. Poole cited - also a cause of the dollar's malaise -- was the markedly weak U.S. employment report Friday showing a decline of 308,000 nonfarm payroll jobs in February, the effects of which continued to reverberate in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Monday. \par \par \uc2  "You had a dismal payrolls report and the backdrop doesn't look too comforting in the equity markets: foreigners still don't want to come back into US stocks," said David Mozina, Head of G10 strategy with Banc of America Securities in New York. Following its retreat last week, the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 2.2% Monday. \par \par \uc2  A vote at the U.N. on the new resolution giving Iraq a deadline of March 17 to disarm completely could come as early as Tuesday. The resolution, sponsored by the U.S., the U.K. and Spain, may well be vetoed, leaving the U.S. relatively isolated if it does lead military actions against Iraq. Yet should such a resolution get passed unexpectedly, that could give the dollar a boost, some analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The U.N. vote is the primary focus for the market, but at this stage it would appear that a good amount of bad news on that front is already priced into the dollar," said Robert Lynch, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  "If there is a vote by a majority on the council authorizing the use of force, the dollar may actually improve," because that would represent a wider base of support for the U.S. in any campaign against Iraq's Saddam Hussein, Lynch added. \par \par \uc2  In any event, the dollar may enjoy a swift reprieve should hostilities start, amid an initial wave of U.S. air attacks on hopes of a swift resolution to the conflict \par \par \uc2  "Once the bombs start going off, maybe the euro could fall three or four cents [against the dollar] in less than three days," said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital in Warren, N.J. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By John Parry, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2096;\par   john.parry@dowjones.com   (Agnes Crane and Michael Deibert\par contributed to this report.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All Rights Reserved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 03-10-03 1809ET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030310dz3a007ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
