\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options:Dlr/Yen Vols Up: Mkts On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Alert \b0\par\par\uc2 502 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 22:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Volatilities implied by one-month dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were modestly higher late Monday afternoon in New York, compared with levels earlier in Tokyo, while euro/dollar options volatilities were hardly changed. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders stood on alert for a possible sharp rise in the dollar should the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the aim of weakening the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar gained some ground against the yen on a report in the Nihon Keizai Shimbun's Tuesday morning edition that the Bank of Japan is prepared, if necessary, to provide liquidity well above its current target level as Japan's March 31 fiscal year close approaches. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , for its part, is mulling the option of massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the tune of Y1 trillion a day, the report said. \par \par \uc2  A Citicorp report also released Monday suggested that the rise in yen has not been, as is customary, caused by Japanese investors repatriating foreign assets before Japan's fiscal year ends on March 31 - and causing a demand for yen - but rather might be due to selling by both speculative investors and non-Japanese longer-term investors. \par \par \uc2  The dollar lost ground against most of its other major rivals amid an intensification of negative sentiment surrounding the apparently looming prospect of an U.S.-led war against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Around 4 p.m. EST (2100 GMT) in New York, the dollar was at Y116.83, well off its Y116.42 intraday low but still modestly down from Y117.06 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.1055, up from $1.1005 late Friday in New York. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.3249, near fresh four-year lows and sharply down from CHF1.3350 late Friday. Sterling was at $1.6012, modestly down from $1.6032. \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options implied volatility of 9.7%-10.0%, modestly higher than 9.6%-9.9% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  One-month euro/dollar option volatilities were at 10.4%-10.7%, little changed from 10.5%-10.75% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. \par \par \uc2  Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favored dollar puts at 0.9%-1.2%, from 0.85%-1.2% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied. \par \par \uc2  Euro calls were favored over the dollar at 0.6%-0.9% in New York. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Deibert and John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires; john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096; michael.deibert@dowjones.com; 201-938-2248 \par \par \uc2  1077 4127 44427 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030310dz3a001ct\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
