\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Countdown To Iraq Showdown \b0\par\par\uc2 2,102 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 06:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Unemployment Rise Hits Stocks\par DJIA          7740.03    gain 66.04     up 0.9%\par NASDAQ        1305.29    gain 2.40      up 0.2%\par S&P 500       828.89     gain 6.79      up 0.8%\par Dow Future    7720.00    loss 15.00     dn 0.2%\par NASDAQ Future 986.00     loss 0.50      dn 0.1%\par S&P Future    827.70     loss 0.90      dn 0.1%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.62% dn 0.03\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Wall Street notched its second weekly decline, despite a day of small gains Friday as investors' hopes that the uncertainty about Iraq might soon end helped overcome a dismal jobs report. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: "The unemployment report was the worst since the Sept. 11 attacks so it was a very big negative," said Todd Clark, head of listed equity trading at Wells Fargo Securities. But "the overriding economic theme is the market wants to see the end of the overhang of uncertainty of Iraq." \par \par \uc2  The Labor Department reported that the nation's unemployment rate rose to 5.8% in February and companies across the economy slashed 308,000 jobs. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: The surprisingly weak February employment report gave Treasurys another big boost Friday. In a volatile trading session, speculation rose that further easing by the Federal Reserve might be on tap. \par \par \uc2  "The bottom line is that the wheels are starting to fall off this economy," said William Strazzullo, market strategist at State Street in Boston. "Despite all the previous rate cuts and tax cuts, the economy remains sluggish and this payroll report must worry the Fed." \par \par \uc2  Traders looking for a relief from recent jumpy trading may be disappointed. A flood of potentially market-moving economic reports are scheduled for this week. \par \par \uc2  Among them, the February retail and food service sales report, to be released Thursday, is expected to show a decline due to weak auto sales. \par \par \uc2  Friday's Producer Price Index for February is expected to show a pronounced increase, largely as a result of surging energy prices. \par \par \uc2  Also Friday, the February Industrial Production Index is expected to show slower growth. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Japan Shares Continue Tailspin; Iraq Cited\par USD-Yen             116.80       loss  0.30    dn 0.3%\par AUD-USD             0.6134       gain  0.0009  up 0.2%\par Nikkei 225          8014.38      loss  129.74  dn 1.6%\par Hang Seng           8837.66      loss  69.42.  dn 0.8%\par JGB Yield           0.7550% up 0.0050\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)-- Tokyo stocks were weaker Monday, with the\par blue-chip index sinking below its new 20-year low reached last Friday\par despite a slight uptick in U.S. stocks. The U.S. dollar continued to\par lose ground against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Depressed technology shares Sony, Advantest, Hitachi and Fujitsu\par pushed the Nikkei down ever closer to the 8,000-point level, which\par traders cite as a crucial psychological barrier.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Uncertainty about what impact a possible U.S. attack on Iraq would\par have on the global economy weighed on Tokyo's markets, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Adding to the tensions, North Korea test-fired a missile into the sea\par off its east coast on Monday, South Korea's Defense Ministry said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Declining shares of banks - which have shed between 20% and 30% of\par their value in the past month - also poisoned the mood. A weekend report\par in the financial newspaper, Nihon Keizai, said that a major Japanese\par life insurance company plans to unload some of its bank stockholdings.\par Major banks UFJ, Sumitomo Mitsui Financial and Mitsubishi Tokyo\par Financial were all lower at midday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Over the weekend, the United States continued to press for an\par ultimatum giving Iraqi leader Saddam Hussein until March 17 to prove he\par has disarmed. Washington is expected to announce Monday that it will\par seek a vote by the U.N. Security Council for Tuesday or Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shares of export-dependent technology firms and automakers are among\par the most vulnerable, given concerns that a conflict with Iraq could\par weigh on the U.S. economy and undermine the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar remained weak against the yen, and\par unconfirmed rumors continued to circulate that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might\par be conducting stealth \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to put pressure on a strengthening\par yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks In For Another Rough Session\par Euro-USD            1.1023      gain  0.0015  up 0.1%\par Stlg-USD            1.5996      loss  0.0024  dn 0.2%\par USD-Franc           1.3333      loss  0.0023  dn 0.2%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares are expected to open weak, with\par government bonds supported by a firm euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares are expected to start a pivotal week under\par pressure, although some strength is expected in defensive issues.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But as the Iraq showdown heats up, investors will likely shy away from\par shares, either in favor of cash or government bonds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Friday, European stock markets tested fresh six-year lows as the\par specter of imminent war with Iraq and bleak U.S. employment data\par triggered a rash of selling.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "U.K. and U.S. economic statistics have turned downwards over the past\par few weeks. European statistics have become markedly more bearish, too.\par If economic data continues to turn down, we are not convinced that\par developed-economy central banks will be able to bail out the economy\par with traditional economic policy measures," NatWest Securities research\par head Jeremy Batstone said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bonds are expected to start the session\par slightly higher in a crunch week for the Iraq crisis.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-zone government bonds rose Friday on Iraq anxieties.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Iraq situation continues to be the driving factor of the market,\par and investors remain very cautious and risk-averse," said a dealer with\par a French bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yields were down across the curve Friday, but the front end\par underperformed slightly following the 25-basis-points rate cut by the\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But with further cuts anticipated in the near future, the flattening\par of the yield curve isn't expected to last, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Overall, the market is certainly overvalued compared to a historical\par average, but global uncertainty should still support bond price for a\par while," said the dealer.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets will continue to struggle with the\par foreboding that any U.S.-led military action against Iraq could begin by\par mid-March. That will leave the dollar still the prisoner of the negative\par sentiment that last week dragged the greenback to fresh four-year lows\par versus the euro and the Swiss franc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This week, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists expect the dollar to trade in a range\par between about Y115.0 and Y118.30, while the euro may fluctuate in a\par range between $1.0860 and $1.1200. But in a nervous and rumor-prone\par market, sudden breakouts from even these wide ranges may occur, traders\par warned.\par    This week could see the final twist of events at the U.N. that would\par set a specific date for war against Iraq.     The U.S., U.K. and Spain\par submitted an amendment Friday that set March 17 as a deadline for Iraq\par to commit itself totally to disarmament.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Such efforts by British and U.S. officials to pursue a new U.N.\par resolution to that effect are likely to prove "dollar positive...even if\par military conflict is pursued in the end," said Ian Gunner, head of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research with Mellon Bank in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Monday, March 10: Italy GDP, U.K. Prices\par ET/GMT\par 0300/0800 Italy        Final Fourth-Quarter GDP\par 0430/0930 U.K.         February Input Producer Price Index\par 0430/0930 U.K.         February Output Producer Price Index\par 0430/0930 U.K.         February Output Core Producer Price\par 0830/1330 Canada       February Housing Starts (last 183K)\par 0900/1400 France       Weekly T-bill Auction results\par 0900/1400 U.S.         St. Louis Fed President Poole speaks\par 0930/1430 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  calls for bids\par 1100/1600 U.S.         February Kansas City Fed Index\par 1850/2350 Japan        Fourth-Quarter Revised GDP\par    N/A    U.K.         British Retail Confederation Retail\par    N/A    Germany      Bundesbank allocates EUR5 Bln\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-21