\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MOF Shiokawa: Japan Stock Moves Are Strange \b0\par\par\uc2 185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 04:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Nikkei/Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said he would ask the head of the Tokyo Stock Exchange to look into falling share prices. \par \par \uc2  "Recent buying and selling movements have been strange," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa spoke shortly after the Nikkei 225 Stock Average briefly dipped below the 8000 mark in intraday trading. The index hasn't closed below that level since 1983. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the index fell 2.7% to close at a 20-year low of 8144.12. \par \par \uc2  "On behalf of citizens, I want to ask the head of the Tokyo Stock Exchange to look into this," Shiokawa said of the stock price falls. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa also said the government would take proper \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps as necessary. \par \par \uc2  "We're watching the (market) conditions. If there are rapid moves, we'll have to take steps," he said. \par \par \uc2  At 0447 GMT, the dollar was trading at about Y116.78, below the Y117 level at which traders believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  covertly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent weeks to stem the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030310dz3a00078\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
