\par\par \b \uc2  ODJ Dollar Slips On War Worries, But BOJ Plans Check Descent \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,122 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 23:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones Commodities Service \par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar fell against most major rivals Monday amid an\par intensification of negative sentiment surrounding a looming war against Iraq,\par but managed to pare some losses late in the session on a report that the Bank\par of Japan is planning massive yen sales.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese government and the BOJ are discussing ways to stabilize the stock\par market in the wake of a decline that sent the benchmark Nikkei Stock Average\par below 8,000 briefly Monday for the first time in 20 years, the Nihon Keizai\par Shimbun reported in its Tuesday morning edition.\par The BOJ is prepared, if necessary, to provide liquidity well above its current\par target level as Japan's March 31 fiscal-year close approaches. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , for its part, is mulling the option of massive yen-selling\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the tune of one trillion yen a day.\par That report "seemed to spook the market", allowing the dollar to claw back\par some of its losses against the yen and recover somewhat against other rivals,\par said Grant Wilson, trader senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader with Mellon Bank,\par Pittsburgh, Penn.\par As Japan's ailing banks approach the country's fiscal year end on March 31,\par some may need to be rescued, should the Nikkei Average fall in sustained\par fashion below the 8000 points mark, denting their extensive equity portfolios.\par "We will avoid a financial system crisis under any circumstances," Japanese\par Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi told reporters Monday.\par Traders suspect that at current levels, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is already\par covertly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a regular basis in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in an\par effort to weaken the yen - as it did in January and February - in order to\par avoid the dollar slipping below the Y115.0 mark. That area has oft been cited\par as a critical pain threshold, below which Japanese exporters' competitiveness\par would suffer.\par However, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders said that any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts would probably\par come before the dollar slipped that far.\par The BOJ, which acts on the ministry's behalf in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "will be very keen on keeping the dollar above Y116.00, a figure\par which will be very, very keenly watched," said Thomas Molloy, a trader at Bank\par Leumi in New York.\par Late Monday in New York, the dollar was at Y116.83, well off its Y116.42\par intraday low but still modestly down from Y117.06 late Friday in New York. The\par euro was at $1.1055, up from $1.1005 late Friday in New York.\par Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.3249, near fresh four-year\par lows and sharply down from CHF1.3350 late Friday. Sterling was at $1.6012,\par modestly down from $1.6032, as the pound continued to lose its edge on U.K.\par Prime Minister Tony Blair's slipping popularity and threats of ministerial\par resignations over his backing for U.S. President George W. Bush in any\par military action against Iraq.\par Escalating nerves ahead of a critical United Nations' vote expected this week\par that could pave the way for, or precipitate, military action against Iraq as\par well as hangover from the dismal U.S. employment report released Friday\par continued to pressure the greenback.\par "The dollar remains under a lot of pressure and the uncertainty surrounding\par Iraq is obviously one big factor," said John McCarthy, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at ING Barings Capital Markets in New York.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders are also turning their attention to the next Federal Reserve\par policy meeting on March 18, at which some economists are now expecting a rate\par cut in response to the deteriorating U.S. economic outlook and the seemingly\par imminent threat of an U.S.-led military campaign. This outlook may send\par Treasury bond yields even lower and could further detract from the appeal of\par dollar-denominated fixed income assets, analysts said.\par The benchmark 10-year Treasury's yield - which moves inversely to its price -\par hit a new 44-year low of 3.56% Monday. Treasury yields fell, partly in\par reaction to striking comments by Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis President\par William Poole who said Monday there is a "strong case" for the Federal Reserve\par to make an interest rate cut at the Fed's next policy-setting meeting on March\par 18.\par One reason Poole cited - also a cause of the dollar's malaise was the markedly\par weak U.S. employment report Friday showing a decline of 308,000 nonfarm\par payroll jobs in February, the effects of which continued to reverberate in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets Monday.\par "You had a dismal payrolls report and the backdrop doesn't look too comforting\par in the equity markets: foreigners still don't want to come back into US\par stocks," said David Mozina, Head of G10 strategy with Banc of America\par Securities in New York. Following its retreat last week, the Dow Jones\par Industrial Average fell 2.2% Monday.\par A vote at the U.N. on the new resolution giving Iraq a deadline of March 17 to\par disarm completely could come as early as Tuesday. The resolution, sponsored by\par the U.S., the U.K. and Spain, may well be vetoed, leaving the U.S. relatively\par isolated if it does lead military actions against Iraq. Yet should such a\par resolution get passed unexpectedly, that could give the dollar a boost, some\par analysts said.\par "The U.N. vote is the primary focus for the market, but at this stage it would\par appear that a good amount of bad news on that front is already priced into the\par dollar," said Robert Lynch, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with BNP Paribas in\par New York.\par "If there is a vote by a majority on the council authorizing the use of force,\par the dollar may actually improve," because that would represent a wider base of\par support for the U.S. in any campaign against Iraq's Saddam Hussein, Lynch\par added.\par In any event, the dollar may enjoy a swift reprieve should hostilities start,\par amid an initial wave of U.S. air attacks on hopes of a swift resolution to the\par conflict\par "Once the bombs start going off, maybe the euro could fall three or four cents\par (against the dollar) in less than three days," said David Leaver, senior\par trader with Gain Capital in Warren, N.J.\par Among global political developments elsewhere, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists were\par keeping one eye on North Korea's nuclear saber rattling. North Korea fired a\par cruise missile into the Sea of Japan early Monday.\par   -By John Parry, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2096;\par   john.parry@dowjones.com (Agnes Crane and Michael Deibert contributed to this\par report)\par Copyright 2003 OsterDowJones Commodity News (ODJ). All rights reserved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 03-10-03 1703ET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djcs000020030310dz3a00u3e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
