\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Is Mixed Against Rivals on Jobs Data, War News \b0\par\par\uc2  By Agnes T. Crane \par \uc2 870 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 01:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar was mixed against its major rivals Friday after a barrage of U.S. data and news on the war and terror fronts sent the greenback on a roller coaster  ride to land higher against the Swiss franc and lower against the euro and yen. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the dollar was at 117.06 yen, up sharply from its global session low of 116.40 yen but down from 117.36 yen in late New York trading Thursday. The euro was at $1.1005, down from its global session high of $1.1066 but  up from $1.0968 late Thursday in New York. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3350 Swiss francs, up from 1.3341 francs late Thursday in New York. Sterling was at $1.6021 compared with $1.6032 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Traders had to grapple with conflicting information on the alleged arrest of one or more of Osama bin Laden's sons in Pakistan. Reports of their capture fueled dollar buying while the White House's refusal to confirm the reports sparked further selling of the greenback against the euro, the Swiss franc and sterling. \par \par \uc2  However, the testimony from United Nations chief weapons  inspector Hans Blix caused barely a ripple in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading Friday as his report provided little new insights into how the global tensions involving Iraq's disarmament will play out. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the greenback staged an eleventh hour comeback and moved back above its key psychological level of  117 yen, prompting speculation among market participants that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  covertly to stymie the  yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  President Bush's press conference late Thursday set the tone for the dollar early in the global session when traders  sold the greenback on hawkish comments that seemed to indicate military action in the Middle East was only a matter days, not weeks, away. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc, considered to be a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in times of war, and the euro both touched multiyear highs during the London session, then retreated moderately before the New York trading began until the U.S. February employment report dropped like a bombshell on the market. \par \par \uc2  The Labor Department reported nonfarm business payrolls plummeted by 308,000 in February, much worse than the consensus forecast of a 5,000 job gain and well below the 185,000 gain reported in January. The unemployment rate rose  0.1 percentage point to 5.8%. \par \par \uc2  The data stunned traders who called the numbers "horrible." The euro shot to a new four-year high on the news, hitting $1.1066, while the dollar sunk to a four-year low against the Swiss franc at 1.3247 francs, and stewed below the 117 yen against the yen. \par \par \uc2  But the day was far from over. \par \par \uc2  Profit taking soon set in to push the greenback off its lows and developments on the terror and war fronts soon fueled further dollar buying, especially against the euro and Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  News reports suggested that the U.S. and the United Kingdom have set March 17 as a deadline for Iraq to comply fully with disarmament, buying the diplomatic process more time to bear fruit before a possible outbreak of war. Both President Bush in his televised press conference Thursday night and Secretary of State Colin Powell during his speech before the U.N. Security Council, however, signaled that time had run out for Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The sense that the U.S. is making efforts to gather broader international support and to reduce the relative isolation of its standoff with Iraq helped give the dollar some relief, strategists said. \par \par \uc2  "If any conflict is under the U.N. sanction, that would be preferable to the U.S. going boldly by itself" into a war, said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with  Mellon Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the yen traded as if it were in a world of its own. \par \par \uc2  "The bigger story is the yen rather than the dollar," said Robert Sinche, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in  New York, who attributed much of the yen's strength to euro  selling against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But, in the waning hours of the New York session, the dollar popped higher still against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite renewed weakness against other rivals, prompting many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and strategists to mull over the possibility that the Bank of Japan was behind the move. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan -- which acts on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf in such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- sold $4.5 billion worth of yen in covert \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February. \par \par \uc2  While Japanese banks were said to be buyers of the greenback against the yen, raising suspicions of yet more stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists noted that trading volume slimmed to a trickle in the last few hours of  the New York session ahead of the weekend, which exacerbated the price action. \par \par \uc2  -- John Parry contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Write to Agnes T. Crane at agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright