\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Weak Vs Majors As Mkt Weighs War Risks \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 06:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar remained on the defensive against its major counterparts in Asia Friday, though its declines were limited as the market awaited a clearer picture of how a looming showdown in Iraq would affect the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  While U.S. President George W. Bush continued to talk tough on Iraq, his speech late Thursday in Washington did little to upset the market given the lack of fresh information regarding the timing of the war, or how long it would last, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "It did reinforce the view that a climax may be imminent," said Minoru Shioiri, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Kokusai Securities. "But what then? Do you really sell dollars? It's not that simple," he said pointing out that "it's no longer the 'go sell dollars, buy euros' mood of a few days ago." \par \par \uc2  The euro, while edging gradually up against the dollar, failed to significantly reclaim $1.10 during the Asian session. The greenback also remained trapped in an extremely tight range against the yen, hovering in the lower Y117s, as fear of Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept volatility low and eliminated fresh incentives to trade. \par \par \uc2  Market players are now looking at the possibility that a war in Iraq could sway the dollar in either direction. The dollar continues to be weighed down by concern over the fiscal and political costs to the U.S. if it pushes ahead with the war despite the diplomatic obstacles. But a quick success by the U.S. in ousting Saddam would likely be positive, they say. \par \par \uc2  "The approaching climax in Iraq offers two-way risk to short dollar positions," Tim Stewart, Morgan Stanley's chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  With regard to the euro, "it is certainly difficult to argue against the recent momentum, and we still favor higher levels later in the year," he said. But "I would keep some powder dry to add on pullbacks to $1.0865," Stewart said. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y117.37, little changed from Y117.36 late Thursday in New York and late Tuesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0994, higher than $1.0972 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.0967 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y129.04, up from Y128.77 in New York late Thursday. The euro was at Y128.71 in Tokyo late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Trade thinned in the afternoon as players retreated to the sidelines to await a crucial report to the U.N. Security Council by U.N. chief weapons inspector Hans Blix as well as U.S. payrolls data later Friday. \par \par \uc2  Trading in the yen was especially dull, as repeated stealth yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry have sapped volatility from the market, a Japanese bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  While the ministry has declined to comment on whether it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in markets this week, as some suspect, it admitted last Friday that it had quietly sold yen several times for dollars and euros in late February. They have also admitted to a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar/yen's no longer in the limelight...(and) the Tokyo market's dead right now," Kokusai's Shioiri said. \par \par \uc2  A senior Finance Ministry official effectively welcomed the slower pace of trading, as he told reporters late Thursday the goal of the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are to curb excessive fluctuations in the dollar/yen exchange rate so as not to harm real-demand-backed trades made by Japanese institutional investors who need to exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for business purposes. \par \par \uc2  "If lack of volatility is driving away those who don't like that lack of volatility because they can't turn a quick profit, then that's fine," the senior ministry official said. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, made clear again Friday that Japan remains vigilant against excessive moves, saying his ministry will watch the market carefully in the aftermath of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest rate cut overnight. \par \par \uc2  "We plan to watch \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate moves today and tomorrow," Shiokawa told reporters, saying also that he welcomed the move by the ECB to coordinate its policies with those of Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Japan needs to glare at both the euro and the dollar," with regard to the yen, Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo traders expect the dollar to trade quietly next week between Y116-Y119. \par \par \uc2  In late Tokyo, sterling was at $1.6015, little changed from $1.6018 in New York but higher than $1.6005 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates that accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture that is updated\par h