\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr See-Saws On Bin Laden Sons Capture Enigma, Blix, UN \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 17:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The beleaguered dollar made a swift partial rebound, then retraced part of its gains midmorning Friday after a report - later denied by U.S. officials - that two of Osama Bin Laden's sons had been captured in Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  The euro slipped nearly half a cent to around $1.1020 against the dollar on the information about Bin Laden's family, announced by Pakistan's provincial home minister. The sense of relief about the reported arrests helped the dollar come off its global session lows plumbed in the wake of a dismal U.S. employment report earlier Friday. \par \par \uc2  "There was definitely initial enthusiasm," as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants pushed the dollar higher on the  sense that the U.S. administration's efforts to curtail global terrorist activities are bearing some fruit, said Tim  Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. \par \par \uc2  However, in Washington, U.S. counterterrorism officials then strongly disputed reports saying bin Laden's sons were captured. They said they had no information that would suggest any of the sons had been detained. That took some of  the wind out of the dollar's sails and it started to retrace some of its gains. \par \par \uc2  In addition, the persistent gloom emanating from a very  weak U.S. employment report, showing a huge 308,000 fall in  nonfarm payroll jobs that had bashed the dollar and sent it  to four-year lows against the euro earlier in the session, haven't been dispelled and are still weighing on the greenback, Mazanec said. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, any such efforts the greenback makes to rally back from its global session troughs may provide an opportunity for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - acting via the  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  - to step in and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with the aim of weakening the yen, traders warned Friday. \par \par \uc2  "This is exactly the kind of environment that the BOJ wants to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in, said Thomas Molloy, trader at Bank Leumi in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also looking for a cue to rally, after getting somewhat oversold, Molloy argued. "The market got ahead of itself" to the downside, aiding its partial snap-back, he said. \par \par \uc2  As chief U.N. weapons inspector Hans Blix finished giving his report to the U.N. on Iraq - which financial market participants had hotly awaited - the dollar continued   hovering in the fairly narrow ranges where it had spent during most of Blix's address. \par \par \uc2  Late morning in New York, the dollar was at Y116.50 against the yen, up from Y116.42 immediately following the U.S. employment report released at 8:30 a.m. EST, and down from Y117.36 late Thursday. The euro was at $1.1027, down from its global session high of $1.1066 after the jobs data,  but up from $1.0972 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.3298, up  from CHF1.3249 following the jobs report but down from CHF1.3341 late Thursday. The pound was at $1.6042, down from   session highs of $1.6081 in the wake of the jobs report and up from $1.6018 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Agnes T. Crane  contributed to this report).\par \f28 