\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar struggles as Bush ratchets up rhetoric. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 04:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 7 (Reuters) - The dollar was stuck not far off multi-year lows against the euro in Asia on Friday as U.S. President George W. Bush maintained a hardline stance on disarming Iraq, reinforcing the view that war may be imminent. \par \par \uc2  Bush, in a keenly awaited news conference at the White House, said that refusing to use force to disarm Iraq posed unacceptable risk. \par \par \uc2  He also said that the United States would spend only days persuading other nations to support a U.N resolution authorising force before bringing the issue to a vote at the Security Council, regardless of whether Washington could count on its passage. \par \par \uc2  Analysts took his remarks as indicating that Washington may go ahead with military action despite opposition from many other United Nations member countries. \par \par \uc2  "If the United States were to start a war without a U.N. resolution, it would most certainly be damaging for the dollar," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  The costs of waging a conflict would have to be borne by the United States, while the already slowing U.S. economy would likely suffer and oil prices likely rise. \par \par \uc2  As of 0220 GMT, the greenback was quoted at 117.40/45 yen almost flat from 117.32/40 yen in late U.S. trade, after retreating slightly from the day's high of 117.48 following Bush's comments. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's been clear to many market players that there will be a war soon and Bush's comments made that all too clear again. So the market didn't show much reaction," said Seiya Nakajima, chief economist at Japanese trading house Itochu Corp said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also little changed at $1.0972/77 levelling with $1.0972/76 in late U.S. trade. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 128.83/92 yen versus 128.79 yen. \par \par \uc2  ECB CUTS RATES \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been under pressure over the last few weeks on worries about the cost of a war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  It had hit four-year lows against the euro, which climbed to $1.1012 on Thursday after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its key interest rate by a modest quarter point. \par \par \uc2  "I expected a 50 basis point cut, so the ECB's move was a bit surprising," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president at UFJ Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing group. \par \par \uc2  "The euro initially edged down, but soon bounced back as the market assessed that the 25-basis point cut could be positive for the economy, thus for the euro." \par \par \uc2  The euro defied gloomy news that France's deficit would hit the equivalent of 3.4 percent of gross domestic product in 2003, above the ceiling for the European Union's Stability and Growth Pact. \par \par \uc2  READY TO ACT \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar was in part helped by lingering wariness about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with dealers saying there was again talk that the Bank of Japan was bidding in the 117.10-20 yen range. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  disclosed last Friday that it had conducted covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times in February, to stem the yen's rise, which Japanese officials fear could hurt exports. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came as the dollar slipped to around 117 yen, raising speculation that 117 yen may be a major defence line for Tokyo, although Japanese officials have said they do not have specific levels to protect. \par \par \uc2  Zembei Mizoguchi, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, reiterated on Friday that Japan would watch the market carefully and act as needed. \par \par \uc2  The market is now waiting on a key report by chief U.N. weapons inspector Hans Blix on Iraqi compliance expected later in the day and if Washington could garner international support for military action. \par \par \uc2  Also attracting attention is key U.S. employment data due at 1330 GMT. Economists polled by Reuters are expecting non-farm payrolls to have increased a meagre 8,000 in February after rising by 143,000 in January. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030307dz37006my\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
