\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's Kuroda named govt policy adviser. \b0\par\par\uc2 175 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 04:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 7 (Reuters) - Japan's former top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, has been appointed as a policy adviser to the cabinet, the government said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda, who retired as vice finance minister for international affairs last month, will specialise in international financial policy, a government spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi has recently stepped up his rhetoric on fixing Japan's economic problems as the nation's continued downturn saps his popularity. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda has been an influential figure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market over the past few years due to his clout in setting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy - notably whether and when to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  Fluent in English, he has written widely on international financial affairs and is a frequent speaker at conferences. \par \par \uc2  His name had been mentioned as a possible candidate for one of the vice governors' position at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Instead the posts went to former Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto and Cabinet Office official Kazumasa Iwata. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030307dz370060q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
