\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Touches a 4-Year High Against Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 1,131 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 00:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -- The euro touched a fresh four-year high just above the\par $1.10 mark Thursday, as weaker-than-expected U.S. data and persistent\par war worries dragged the greenback lower versus the common European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mustered gains against the yen and Swiss franc, and was steady against the pound. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday in New York, the dollar had recovered from its earlier lows to reach &#165;117.36, modestly up from &#165;117.25 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.0972, up from $1.0965 late Wednesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3341 francs, up from 1.3301 francs late Wednesday. Sterling was at $1.6018, little changed from $1.6013. \par \par \uc2  The euro's push to $1.1007 at about 9:10 a.m. EST -- a fresh four-year high -- was the second time in the past two days the euro had made an assault on the $1.10 mark but failed to break through decisively. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's wavering against the euro comes on the eve of what could prove a critical day for the greenback. On Friday, chief United Nations weapons inspector Hans Blix will deliver a pivotal report on inspections in Iraq to the U.N., while the dollar also has to contend with the widely-watched U.S. monthly employment report, which will be released at 8:30 a.m. EST. \par \par \uc2  Cross currents of jumpy and sometimes rumor-driven trade ahead of a press conference President Bush called for Thursday evening also lent an uncertain tone to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  However, growing expectations that a military attack on Iraq might only be days away, probably without the support of another U.N. resolution, continued to lend a negative tone to the dollar. "The more it looks like the U.S. is going to go it alone with the U.K. and Spain [in waging a war against Iraq], the more that is putting the dollar under some pressure," said Hugh Walsh, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Fortis Financial USA in New York. \par \par \uc2  In a reflection of jitters about terrorism and the possibility of war, stocks fell, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average shedding 1.3% and sullying dollar sentiment even further. \par \par \uc2  While persistent suspicions that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be covertly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilized the dollar against the yen, a surprise Swiss National Bank rate cut helped the greenback gain some ground against the Swiss franc. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  trimmed rates by a quarter-percentage point, less than some economists had expected, adding momentum to the euro's gains. The Bank of England stood pat, in line with expectations. \par \par \uc2  Comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials Thursday that they don't believe the euro's strength poses a threat to the competitiveness of euro-zone exporters were one of the catalysts that may have given the euro its latest leg up. \par \par \uc2  ECB President Wim Duisenberg said that the euro's appreciation this week isn't yet hampering the competitiveness of euro-zone companies, but U.S.-based strategists viewed his remarks skeptically. \par \par \uc2  "Exchange-rate developments haven't fundamentally undermined the position of the euro zone relative to the rest of world," Mr. Duisenberg said, helping the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  get another leg up. Mr. Duisenberg's remarks were all the more striking as European companies and policy makers have persistently flagged the $1.10 threshold as a level that would start to hurt European export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, a German government official's warning this week that a euro above $1.10 could become critically harm that country's economy essentially reiterated protests that manufacturers in Europe's biggest economy have made during the past year, as the euro has gained some 22% against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The whispers about the strength of the euro have become louder; they are not whispers any more," said Joseph Quinlan, Global Economist with Johns Hopkins University in Washington. \par \par \uc2  Early in the afternoon, the New York Board of Trade's U.S. Dollar Index -- a trade-weighted geometric average of the dollar against six other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- was at 98.35. The euro's ascent to a key technical threshold is one indication that the dollar index could fall by around 4%, as low as 94.90 by the end of April, Stephen Cox, chief technical analyst with Dow Jones Newswires. That index has declined by about 25% since February of last year, but this is largely due to the gains of the euro. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists also emphasized that a raft of weaker-than-expected U.S. economic data including weekly initial jobless claims released Thursday that rose by 12,000 -- was the root cause of the dollar's slip against major rivals, including the euro. \par \par \uc2  "The unemployment-claims data adds to the mosaic over the past few weeks that suggests that the U.S. [economic] cycle is under some pressure" and in an environment where U.S. monetary policy is loose and U.S. interest rates are at record lows "it's going to be difficult to attract capital," said Robert Sinche, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank in New York. He added that the dollar was coming under pressure across the board, not just against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Amid a continuing rift between France, Germany and Russia who favor continued U.N. weapons inspections in Iraq, and the U.S., the U.K. and Spain who are pressing for a resolution to authorize swift military action against Saddam Hussein, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are intensifying their focus on Mr. Blix ahead of his report. \par \par \uc2  "The more the situation with Iraq is prolonged, the worse it is for the dollar," said Larry Kantor, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at JP Morgan. The uncertainty about when a military conflict may start and the imponderables any such action may cause "is weighing on the markets, the economy," and to a degree is discouraging investment flows into the U.S., Mr. Kantor said. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar has become increasingly battered by risk aversion surrounding the U.S. standoff with Iraq over the past two weeks, the greenback has fallen to levels where it could muster a relief rally during the initial stages of a conflict, some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists say. Still, cautioned Mr. Kantor, there are significant risks to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , should any U.S.-led military campaign get unexpectedly bogged down. \par \par \uc2  -- Agnes T. Crane contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  03-06-03 1845ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030306dz36009kh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
