\par\par \b \uc2  Remarks from Snow Send Dollar Reeling \b0\par\par\uc2  By Agnes T. Crane \par \uc2 938 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 23:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar fell swiftly Wednesday, after off  the cuff remarks from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow sparked a wave of selling that pushed the Swiss franc - and briefly the euro - to fresh four-year highs against the U.S.  \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Speaking to reporters after testifying before the House Ways and Means Committee late Tuesday, Mr. Snow said the dollar is trading within a normal range and that he did not "see anything troubling" about recent declines against other  major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The comments had more resonance as they came on a day that the dollar was in any case weakening. \par \par \uc2  Traders viewed the remarks as tacit approval for a softer greenback, a marked departure from the strong-dollar policy in place since Robert Rubin was Treasury chief during  the Clinton Administration. Mr. Snow's predecessor, Paul O'Neill, also sought to stick to the strong-dollar mantra during his tenure, but only after he caused market jitters by failing to do so and came across as ambivalent. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the global session, the euro shot up on Mr. Snow's comments, briefly flirting with the $1.10 level, an area not seen in four years. The dollar hit a fresh low against the Swiss franc at 1.3262 francs, its weakest level since late 1998. The comments also helped undermine ongoing efforts by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen through covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The dollar hit a global session low of 117.04 yen. \par \par \uc2  A quasi-retraction by Mr. Snow during the New York trading day helped reverse the losses somewhat. At a signing  ceremony to collect his signature for use on new paper money, Mr. Snow repeated that he is in favor of a strong dollar. "Let me reiterate my support for the strong dollar,"  he said, declining to take questions from reporters. \par \par \uc2  In late dealings Wednesday, the dollar was at 117.25 yen, down from 117.95 yen late Tuesday in New York. The euro  was at $1.0965, up from $1.0886 late Tuesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3301 francs, down from 1.3393 francs late Tuesday in New York. Sterling was at $1.6013, compared with $1.5807 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the reaction to Mr. Snow's remarks underscored the current volatile trading conditions. They also argued that Mr. Snow shouldn't make such comments, unless he wants to signal a change in the Bush administration's support for a strong dollar. \par \par \uc2  "If you have nothing new to say, then your best strategy  is to say nothing," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York. "Nobody forced him to say anything about the state of the dollar." \par \par \uc2  To be sure, the weak dollar can't be solely pinned to the new Treasury Secretary. "The market certainly is using him as an excuse," said Thomas Molloy, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Bank Leumi in New York. \par \par \uc2  The initial remarks may have sparked the downturn, but the speed and magnitude of the dollar's fall had more to do with technical factors and broader problems facing the beleaguered greenback. "The dollar has been weakening since January of last year and at each step along the way, a new catalyst is identified" to explain the most recent movement,  said Paul Podolsky, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Fleet Boston Financial in Boston. \par \par \uc2  "The bigger theme is there is just not enough demand for  dollars right now," he said, citing loose monetary policy, low interest rates and the possibility of a U.S.-led war with Iraq as factors inhibiting investment in U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  During Wednesday trading, the steady drumbeat surrounding a potential military conflict in the Middle East  failed to elicit much market response as traders shrugged off declarations from officials on both sides of the debate as more of the same. \par \par \uc2  France, Germany and Russia, who have repeatedly voiced their opposition to a military solution, said in a joint statement that they will vote against a second United Nations resolution on Iraq. The United Kingdom, the U.S. and  Spain have proposed a draft resolution that says Iraqi President Saddam Hussein has missed his final opportunity to disarm. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell rejected  the European bloc's call to give inspectors more time and resources before deciding on war. As long as Saddam is determined to hide his weapons of mass destruction, these efforts won't work, Mr. Powell said. \par \par \uc2  Chief weapons inspector Hans Blix for his part offered little new insight into Iraqi compliance to destroy its weapons of mass destruction, but did say the destruction of the illegal Al Samoud 2 missiles represents "real disarmament." \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's focus was on monetary policy, with both the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of England to meet to decide on short-term interest rates. Strategists expect the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slice  rates by as much as one-half percentage point to 2.25%, while the Bank of England is expected to leave policy unchanged at 3.75%. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the Bank of Canada raised interest rates one-quarter percentage point, while the Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased rates by one-half percentage point earlier Wednesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Agnes Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2248\par    email:agnes.crane@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (John Parry contributed to this report.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All Rights Reserved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 03-05-03 1719ET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030305dz350086h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
