\par\par \b \uc2  Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For March 5 \b0\par\par\uc2 2,099 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 10:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020. \par \par \uc2  MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: The Bank of Japan Wednesday acted to maintain its ultra-easy monetary policy, raising its excess liquidity target in anticipation of a move by Japan's postal corporation to deposit reserves at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BOJ's nine-member policy board voted unanimously to increase its account balance target by Y2 trillion to a range of Y17 trillion to Y22 trillion, a move it claimed was a technical adjustment rather than an intentional effort to loosen credit. The new target takes effect April 1. ("=WRAP: BOJ Ups Acct Balance To Maintain Ultra-Easy Policy" at 0730 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: As expected, Financial Secretary Antony Leung in his budget speech Wednesday announced tax hikes and spending cuts to rein in the government's blowout budget deficit. Leung also laid down a path to a future goods and services tax, and said Hong Kong's gross domestic product is expected to grow 3% in 2003 - improving from 2.3% growth in 2002. The forecast is in line with expectations by the International Monetary Fund as well as economists surveyed by Dow Jones. (Wrap around 1100 GMT to include latest GDP data) \par \par \uc2  MANILA: Philippine financial markets took a beating Wednesday after deadly bombings in a key southern city late Tuesday fueled fears of a backlash in the economy. The peso plunged in early trading to a new 25-month low against the dollar at PHP54.735, the highest level for the greenback since PHP54.790 on Jan. 18, 2001. The Philippine Stock Exchange index ended down 1.6% at 1009.68. ("=WRAP: Philippine Financial Markets Slide On Davao Blasts" at 0603 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING: In a public swansong Wednesday Chinese Premier Zhu Rongji charted a path of reduced economic pump-priming through bond issuances and efforts to increase domestic incomes to reach gross domestic product growth of around 7% in 2003. ("=WRAP:China's Zhu Signals Sunset Of Fiscal Growth Policy" at 0832 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY: Australian economic growth slowed in the final quarter of 2002 - reflecting the impact of the severe drought on rural exports and sluggish global growth - but resilient domestic demand allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep rates steady for the ninth month in a row. ("=WRAP: Australian Economy Slows But No Rate Move By RBA" at 0519 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Japanese corporate sentiment worsened in the first three months of this year, even after profits soared and capital spending showed signs of bottoming out in the October-December period, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  surveys released Wednesday showed. ("=WRAP: Japan Large Co Mood Worsens Even After Pfts Soared" ran at 0438 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's International Bureau chief Watanabe, trying to put the brakes on the dollar after remarks by U.S. Treasury Secretary Snow sent it nosediving early this morning, tells Dow Jones that MOF is closely watching \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, signaling the ministry is prepared to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the yen. ("\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF Watanabe" at 0016 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: "It won't happen overnight, but it will happen," was the advertising slogan purred by leggy New Zealand supermodel Rachel Hunter in the 1990s to promote Pantene Pro-V shampoo, but her comments are also apropos for the Aussie-Kiwi \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cross rate. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: On Aussie-Kiwi Just Listen To Rachel Hunter" ran 0023 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: There's room for the South Korean government to take fiscal stimulus measures to support the sagging economy, a director at the International Monetary Fund said Wednesday. Separately, a report issued by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy Wednesday said economic conditions in South Korea are deteriorating faster than expected as its trade terms weaken amid higher oil prices and a drop in semiconductor prices. (Wrap expected by around 1030 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MANILA: Philippine year-on-year inflation in February stood at one of its highest levels in months, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday the rise in consumer prices was still within target, giving it room to maintain its current neutral policy stance. ("=WRAP: Higher Philippine Feb CPI Won't Spur Rate Hike" at 0508 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =AUSTRALIA WATCH: With the 20th anniversary of the Hawke Labor government's election this month, some Australians are reflecting on the pace and virtue of economic reform since those heady days. If ever there was evidence to support the view the effort was worth it, this week's data has provided it. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: Good Times For Reformed Economy" at 0541 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: The Bank of Korea will almost certainly keep the overnight call rate at its current level of 4.25% this month at its monetary policy meeting scheduled for Thursday, as pressures for a cut or hike are evenly balanced amid global geopolitical uncertainties, say economists. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Korea Call Rate Frozen Yet Again" at 0426 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Singapore's Purchasing Managers' Index fell to 50.4% in February, from 51% in January, indicating that activity in the local manufacturing industry is still increasing although momentum has weakened. ("Singapore Feb PMI 50.4% Vs Jan 51%; Elec Subindex 50.8%" at 1600 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: Stung by U.S. Treasury John Snow's apparent indifference to its fate, the dollar tumbled against its major counterparts in Asia Wednesday, skidding to new four-year lows against the euro. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " moved at 0536 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs around 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  EQUITIES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Struggling supermarket store operator Daiei, a bellwether of the Japanese corporate sector's debt-crushed walking wounded, unveiled the latest details of its revival strategy Wednesday, including a decision to open new food supermarkets - one of the few promising segments of Japan's weak consumer sector. But the latest news won't clear up doubts about the lumbering giant's mainline retail \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Wrap expected by around 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: Cathay Pacific Airways, Hong Kong's largest airline, Wednesday posted a six-fold rise in net profit last year, buoyed by continued cost controls and a surge in passenger and cargo traffic. Cathay's 2002 net profit rose to HK$3.98 billion from HK$657 million in 2001, well above expectations. (Wrap/Interview expected around 1100 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BRISBANE: Australia's Tabcorp Holdings is poised to gain a stranglehold on the Queensland casino market with the launch of a friendly A$1.66 billion cash-and-scrip takeover bid for Jupiters. The bid is Tabcorp's most adventurous expansion since its 1999 acquisition of Sydney's Star City casino, and will reinforce the company's position as Australia's largest gambling concern. ("=WRAP: Australia's Tabcorp Launches A$1.66B Jupiters Bid" at 0640 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING: UTStarcom Inc. said Tuesday it will gain access to new customers and markets outside of China with its deal to buy part of the telecom-equipment business of 3Com Corp. ("=WRAP: UTStarcom Expands Outside China With Acquisition" at 0401 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI: In the third and final day of an after-hours auction of about 1% of Chunghwa Telecom, the government Wednesday sold a 6.39 million shares to raise NT$326.71 million. Wednesday's auction brings the total shares sold in these last three days to 7.42 million out of the 100 million shares it had hoped to unload, as it works toward privatizing Taiwan's largest telecom operator by the end of the year. ("Taiwan/Chunghwa Tel" first take ran at 0801 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK: General Electric will likely offer a big discount to win a bid to supply engines for Malaysian Airline's new Airbus 380-800 passenger planes after losing a potential contract with Thai Airways, which decided to buy used aircraft. The value of the possible deal "could be around $500 million to $700 million," Stuart Dean, president of GE Southeast Asia said. ("=INTERVIEW: GE Bid In Malaysian Plane Deal Up To $700Mln" at 0709 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: South Korean prosecutors summoned SK Group Chairman Son Kil-Seung for questioning Wednesday over his alleged involvement in illegal stock transactions, as well as possible accounting fraud involving the group's key affiliates. ("S Korea Prosecutors Summon SK Group Chmn Son Kil-Seung" at 0353 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: South Korea's Hana Bank, the country's third-largest lender, has signed deals with two Chinese banks to cooperate in various areas of \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a part of its effort to bolster its business in China, the bank said Wednesday. ("S Korea Hana Bank Signs Alliance Deals With 2 China Banks" ran at 0322 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Singapore-based investment holding company BIL International is confident its bid for Thistle Hotels will succeed despite an earlier thumbs-down by the U.K. company, a banking source close to BIL said Wednesday. BIL earlier made an offer of 115 pence a share to buy the 54% of Thistle it doesn't already own, but Thistle's board said the proposal undervalued the U.K. company. ("Singapore BIL/Thistle Bid" first take ran at 0840 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MELBOURNE: Papua New Guinea oil and gas company Oil Search Wednesday reported a sharp jump in 2002 net profit to A$87.2 million from A$23.7 million a year earlier, buoyed by its merger with Orogen Minerals and higher oil production and prices. ("=WRAP: PNG Oil Search Posts Strong Pft Rise, To Buy Shrs" at 0637 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY: Macquarie Airports said Wednesday it made a maiden net profit of A$48.1 million in 2002 but warned that a war in Iraq could crimp its earnings growth. However, the international airport operator is hoping that cost cuts would offset a sharp fall in passenger traffic at its airports in Australia, the U.K. and Italy in the event of a war in the Middle East. ("=WRAP:Macquarie Air Posts A$48M Pft; War May Crimp Growth" at 0730 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE:Intel Corp. and Singapore's telecommunications regulator, the Infocomm Development Authority of Singapore, Wednesday announced a joint initiative to pave the way for seamless wireless broadband access, known as Wi-Fi, roaming throughout Asia. ("Singapore's IDA, Intel To Work On Wi-Fi Wireless Roaming" at 0835 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: TPI Polene PCL, a Thai cement maker under a $950 million debt restructuring, is facing an uphill task to raise at least $180 million worth of new capital through a public share offering to repay its debt. Creditors have petitioned the bankruptcy court to oust the company's Chief Executive Prachai Leophairatana as debt administrator, so the offering has little chance of success, analysts and fund managers say. ("=Asian Stock Focus:Mkt Wary Of Thai TPI Polene Cap Hike" at 0437 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: ING Financial Markets said Wednesday it has downgraded its weighting on Asian utilities to underweight from overweight on expectations that this sector will benefit the least from a relief rally to be triggered by a closure to the Iraq crisis. ("ING Fincl Mkts Downgrades Asian Utilities To Underweight" first take ran at 0512 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: Hong Kong & China Gas posted Wednesday a 2.8% fall in 2002 net profit to HK$3.09 billion, its first decline in full-year net earnings in more than 10 years, as slow demand growth and frozen tariffs finally took their toll on the city's virtual gas monopoly. ("HK & China Gas/Earnings" first take ran at 0637 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Singapore's aviation authority has awarded Japan's Shimizu Corp. a S$965-million contract to build a new airport terminal at Singapore's Changi Airport, an official said Wednesday. ("Singapore Airport/Shimizu" at 0037 GMT) \par \par \uc2  KARACHI: Hub Power Co., Pakistan's biggest independent power producer, Wednesday announced an interim dividend for the current financial year in line with market expectations. But that failed to lift the stock market, which remains depressed due to uncertainty over the Iraq crisis and concerns over domestic security. ("=WRAP: Pakistan's Hubco Continues Steady Dividends" at 0749 GMT) \par \par \uc2  ENERGY/COMMODITIES \par \par \uc2  =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Taxi drivers in China were pleasantly surprised last weekend when the government didn't raise gasoline prices, despite a spike in international oil prices. One reason was because the government wanted to avoid negative public opinion during the sessions of the National People's Congress in early March, government officials and analysts say. ("=CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Parliament, Reforms And Oil Prices" at 0759 GMT) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030305dz35000kv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
