\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Skids Vs Euro, Yen; Snow Hurts Sentiment \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2 776 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 06:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Stung by U.S. Treasury John Snow's apparent indifference to its fate, the dollar tumbled against its major counterparts in Asia Wednesday, skidding to new four-year lows against the euro. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were taken by surprise early in the Asian-Pacific session when Snow, responding to a reporter's questions after testimony before the House Ways and Means Committee late Tuesday in Washington, said; "It's within normal range. I don't see anything troubling about it," referring to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The remarks sent the greenback down to a low of Y117.11 and the euro up past a previous four-year high of $1.0936 to $1.0989, as they "reinforce the market's idea that the so-called 'strong dollar policy' really means 'benign neglect,' said Marshall Gittler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  While the U.S. Treasury was quick to clarify the remarks and to stress that the strong dollar policy remains unchanged, the damage will likely linger, especially given already weak sentiment toward the dollar caused by geopolitical concerns and growing doubts over the U.S. economy, said Minoru Shioiri, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Mitsubishi Securities. \par \par \uc2  "No one really wanted to buy the dollar before, but now even less after Snow," Shioiri said. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, "from the perspective of the euro/dollar, it is certainly more kindling on an existing fire that will likely take us above $1.10," said Tim Stewart at Morgan Stanley in New York. \par \par \uc2  Officials at Japan's Finance Ministry, which has spent the past two months quietly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the dollar from falling, declined direct comment on the remarks, but warned markets about pushing the yen too high. \par \par \uc2  "We are watching the market closely. Our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is unchanged," Hiroshi Watanabe, director-general of the international bureau at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  The comments suggest that despite Snow, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely continue its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it feels necessary, keeping the dollar from falling too far against the yen. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y117.38, below Y117.90 late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was at Y117.61 late Tuesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0972, sharply higher than $1.0878 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at $1.0914 in Tokyo Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y128.79, up from Y128.25 in New York late Tuesday. The euro was at Y128.33 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Given the excitement over Snow's remarks, the dollar was little affected by the Bank of Japan's decision Wednesday to maintain its ultra-easy monetary policy while raising its excess liquidity target in anticipation of a move by Japan's postal corporation to deposit reserves at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nine-member policy board voted unanimously to increase its account balance target by Y2 trillion to a range of Y17 trillion to Y22 trillion, a move it claimed was a technical adjustment rather than an intentional effort to loosen credit. The new target range will take effect from April 1. \par \par \uc2  For the moment, "the focus is more on Iraq and the U.S. economy," rather than Japan issues, Mitsubishi's Shioiri said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is likely to remain weak, although Japan's finance ministry is expected to step in to keep it from falling too far below Y117.00, Tokyo traders say. \par \par \uc2  "It's a given that they'll \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again," said a Japanese trader, adding that it was widely believed the ministry has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermittently this week. \par \par \uc2  But as the ministry's aim is to absorb speculative purchases when the yen is appreciating rapidly, and not to artificially weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar will likely remain locked in a range of Y117-Y120 this coming week, said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  In late Asian trading, sterling was at $1.5896, up from $1.5809 in New York and higher than $1.5819 in Tokyo Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Bennett Richardson\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates that accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture that is updated\par hourly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Subscribers can find this table:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -On Reuters by 