\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Gets Modest Boost As Tsy Snow Reiterates Support \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 18:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The greenback gained some much needed - albeit modest - relief early afternoon Wednesday after Treasury Secretary John Snow reiterated his support for a strong dollar. \par \par \uc2  At a signing ceremony to collect his signature for use on new paper money, Snow repeated that he is in favor of a strong dollar. "Let me reiterate my support for the strong dollar," Snow said. \par \par \uc2  He declined to take questions from reporters. \par \par \uc2  In the wake of Snow's remarks, made shortly after 11:00  a.m. EST, the dollar managed to raise its head a little from earlier global session lows. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains under pressure amid persistent harbingers of a nearing potential war with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The euro slipped to around $1.0975 from $1.0990 in reaction to Snow's remarks. The dollar also came modestly off its earlier global session troughs against the Swiss franc, up to CHF1.3283, but was still near the fresh four-year lows the dollar had plumbed earlier in the New York session. Against the yen, the dollar recovered slightly, to Y117.20 from around Y117.15. \par \par \uc2  The strong dollar policy, espoused by former Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin was perpetuated by successors including former Secretary Paul O'Neill. Snow clearly backed the policy at his confirmation hearings in January. \par \par \uc2  But late Tuesday, comments from Snow indicating he didn't' see "anything troubling" in the dollar's decline, had weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the global day, while fueling a sense that the Bush administration was no longer as committed to a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants viewed Snow's  latest remarks Wednesday as an effort to calm those troubled waters. These remarks "have helped the dollar strengthen just a little bit, but this is a new Treasury Secretary and the market may be even second guessing his comments", so the dollar's reaction has been fairly muted, said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Commenting on the sharp slide in the dollar since the beginning of Wednesday's global session, David Leaver, trader at Gain Capital in Warren, N.J., said that only \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can help the dollar markedly now, because technical factors as well as the deepening international tensions favor dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  In addition to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has covertly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to lift the dollar - and thus weaken the yen - during recent weeks, Leaver is also watching the Swiss National Bank after the dollar hit new four-year low around CHF1.3266 earlier in the New York morning. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Rebecca Christie in Washington and Agnes T. Crane in New York\par contributed to this report).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030305dz35007ka\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
