\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Snow plows dollar to 4-year lows - Iraq no help. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 00:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 5 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow's belated support for the dollar on Wednesday did not convince enough investors to buy it back after the sell-off he sparked a day before when he downplayed its recent weakness. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the dollar fell to four-year lows against the euro and Swiss franc, never recovering from comments Snow made to reporters saying he was "not particularly concerned" about the dollar's decline. Snow moved into damage control mode on Wednesday stating his support for the government's long standing strong-dollar policy. But the damage had been done. \par \par \uc2  In any case, Snow was just a brief distraction from all the talk of war, weapons of mass destruction and diplomacy surrounding Iraq that have weighed heavily on the dollar in the last six months. \par \par \uc2  "The war drums are beating louder and the Treasury is saying it is comfortable with the dollar's recent decline and potential future decline, leaving the door open for the dollar's drop today," said Tod Van Name, senior vice president in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  group at Mizuho Corporate Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Snow's original comments on Tuesday were enough to sow seeds of doubt about the government's commitment to a strong dollar, even though few believed that Snow was trying to send a signal that a weaker dollar was desired. \par \par \uc2  The White House has espoused a strong dollar policy since Robert Rubin headed the Treasury department during the Clinton administration, but what the policy means in practice has always been subject to debate. \par \par \uc2  "Snow is learning that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is filled with people that spend a lot of time parsing any word he says. He put his foot in it, and learned the hard way," said Alan Ruskin, head of research at 4cast Ltd in New York. \par \par \uc2  The market has been left to wonder what constitutes a strong dollar when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen 20 percent against the euro in the past year and as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the dollar higher against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro climbed to a four-year high of $1.0965, up 0.70 percent compared with Tuesday's New York close. At its birth in January 1999, the euro traded at $1.17. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a four-year low of 1.3302 Swiss francs, a loss of 1.30 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a session low of 117.23 yen, down 0.54 percent on the day. However, dealer said the threat of covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped dollar losses. \par \par \uc2  IRAQ STILL THE FOCUS \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Snow's comments were little more than brief distraction from the unfolding situation with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "The Iraq situation is still front and center," said Bob Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell's statement that Iraqi President Saddam Hussein had not decided to disarm as required under U.N. resolution 1441 kept the negative pressure on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Mizuho's Van Name said Powell's comments served to firm investor opinions that the United States will lead an attack to remove weapons of mass destruction from Iraq even without full U.N. support. \par \par \uc2  The White House said on Wednesday it would persist with a U.N. resolution paving the way for war on Iraq even as France, Germany and Russia teamed up to block the move. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  MEETINGS \par \par \uc2  The market showed no reaction to the Bank of Japan's decision to raise its target for the volume of current account deposits held by financial institutions at the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said the move was a technical step to accommodate changes to Japan's postal savings system next month. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar rallied to three-year highs at 61.80 U.S. cents, helped by the earlier Snow comments and by news that the Reserve Bank of Australia had kept its key interest rate unchanged at 4.75 percent, preserving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yield advantage. \par \par \uc2  Norway cut its key rates by 50 basis points and signaled that further easing could be in the cards. The crown firmed modestly after the expected move. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar traded near the upper end of its trading range against the greenback, above 56.60 cents after the Reserve Bank of New Zealand kept its key interest rate unchanged at 5.75 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030306dz3500cd1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
