\par\par \b \uc2  Takenaka: Want BOJ, Govt To Act As One Vs Deflation \b0\par\par\uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 02:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The smooth implementation of this fiscal year's extra budget and the main budget for next fiscal year is the best way for the government to underpin the economy, Japan's economy and banking minister said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Although I am aware that there are already calls for an extra budget for next fiscal year, at this point, the main budget takes into consideration the current state of the economy," Heizo Takenaka told reporters. "We should aim for its speedy enactment." \par \par \uc2  The Lower House of Parliament is expected to approve later Tuesday an Y81.79 trillion budget bill for next fiscal year, ensuring its enactment into law. The budget keeps spending roughly flat from this year but requires record borrowing as the stagnant economy takes a heavy toll on tax revenue. \par \par \uc2  But with deflation firmly entrenched and signs the economic recovery is faltering, politicians have already started to call for more spending through an extra budget for next fiscal year from April. \par \par \uc2  Takenaka added that parliamentary budget deliberations would keep him from attending the Bank of Japan's two-day policy-board meeting that ends Wednesday. But he kept pressure on the BOJ by repeating that he hopes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will act in concert with the government and take steps to combat deflation. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ (board members) are monetary policy specialists. I hope they will use their expertise in analyzing the situation and taking necessary measures," he said. Takenaka has long maintained that deflation remains a monetary phenomenon, which should be countered by monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  When asked about his view over the recent weakness in banking shares, the economy and banking minister said recent stock market movements reflected nervousness over various geopolitical uncertainties and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements. \par \par \uc2  "I think the stock market is looking at various uncertainties. It's also nervously reflecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. It's a nervous market," Takenaka said, but declined to comment specifically on banking shares. \par \par \uc2  Shares in major banks have been sliding recently amid continued concerns about a supply glut, worries about dilution of shareholders' earnings and speculative selling ahead of planned fund-raising moves that include share issuance. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the dollar continues to be pressured lower amid geopolitical uncertainties and concerns over the strength of the U.S. economy, forcing the yen to gain despite repeated attempts by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry to curb the rise through covert \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Alastair Gale and Kirsty Mackenzie \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030304dz340003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
