\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar recovers but still biased toward weakness. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 19:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 4 (Reuters) - The dollar recovered to stand slightly higher on Tuesday after breaching new lows on the Swiss franc earlier, but dealers said a negative bias on the dollar would remain as long as Iraq war worries weighed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading 0.13 percent higher against the Swiss franc at the U.S. midday after hitting its lowest level against the franc since late 1998 and toying with four-year lows against the euro. \par \par \uc2  It was up very slightly against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the turnaround in the dollar's fortunes had more to do with profit-taking after its overnight slump than with any new-found affection for the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "There is a generally accepted range for the euro of between $1.09 and $1.0750. We tested the upside of that range, and, at that point, risk return of being short dollars for euros doesn't pay as much," said John Beerling, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Wells Fargo in Minneapolis. \par \par \uc2  "But it doesn't change the overall outlook, which is one of dollar weakness," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped overnight to $1.0936 per euro , within a hair's breadth of four-year lows of $1.0938 established earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  At midday in New York, the euro was at $1.0888, down 0.07 percent. The dollar was trading at at 1.3387 Swiss francs  after falling as low as 1.3341 overnight. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar was up 0.32 percent at 117.92 yen  as worries over Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to overwhelm negative dollar sentiment. \par \par \uc2  WAR WORRIES WEIGH \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said the dollar remained burdened by worries the United States would go after Iraq with limited international support. \par \par \uc2  "Some people in the market have been talking about easing war fears, but what easing war fears? People in the administration are talking like war is a certainty," said Ron Simpson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at MMS International in New York. \par \par \uc2  "From here forward, sell dollars on anything that looks like a rally, at least against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  Turkey's refusal to allow U.S. forces to use its bases for a possible assault on Iraq was seen by some investors as forcing a delay in any military action, but the White House has made it clear that it could attack Iraq without Turkey's help. \par \par \uc2  The Turkish government on Tuesday said it would consider a second try at winning parliamentary support for U.S. troops. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. also has scoffed at Iraq's destruction of some missiles, saying the move simply reinforced that Iraq was still holding banned weapons in violation of U.N. resolutions. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, France and Germany continued to call on Iraq to disarm voluntarily while opposing any use of force, while Russia on Tuesday warned it might veto any U.N. Security Council resolution paving the way for war on Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the negative sentiment toward the dollar was news on Tuesday that at least 19 people were killed and 144 wounded by a bomb attack at a Philippines airport in the Mindanao region, where U.S. troops are training government forces to fight Muslim rebels. \par \par \uc2  Reports that North Korean fighter jets intercepted an unarmed U.S. spy plane in international air space off the coast of North Korea over the weekend also unnerved markets. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF CANADA RAISES RATES \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada on Tuesday raised its key interest rate by 25 basis points, a move that was neither wholly unexpected nor wholly anticipated. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar surged on the news, rising to C$1.4752  per U.S. dollar, its highest since September 2000. \par \par \uc2  The bank said it made the move because inflation was running above its target of 1 to 3 percent. Year-on-year inflation shot up to an 11-year high in January of 4.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  Canada was the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of the block in a week chock full of bank meetings. Central banks of Japan, the euro zone, Britain, Australia, New Zealand and Norway hold meetings later this week. \par \par \uc2  Markets have fully priced in at least a quarter point rate cut by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030304dz3400zhz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
