\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar resumes descent as US seen isolated on war. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 12:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 4 (Reuters) - The dollar resumed its fall on Tuesday, reaching its lowest since 1998 against the Swiss franc and testing a four-year low on the euro as fears grew the United States could attack Iraq without broad international support. \par \par \uc2  Already reeling after a U.S. manufacturing survey on Monday showed war concerns were harming confidence, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended losses to 1.3341 Swiss francs and $1.0936 to the euro in early European trade. \par \par \uc2  Talk that Japan was buying dollars for yen in a desperate effort to retain export competitiveness meant the greenback's losses against the yen were more limited. \par \par \uc2  "There's still a bit of confusion and uncertainty as to the timing and the length of a war as well as event risks coming up," said Osman Wahid, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase. \par \par \uc2  "Iraq compliance and developments in Turkey are not helping the case for war for the U.S. and UK and the longer the war the heavier it would weigh on the dollar." \par \par \uc2  Baghdad has begun to destroy some missiles since the weekend, meeting a key U.N. deadline, and also offered new concessions relating to chemical and biological weapons. \par \par \uc2  Turkey has refused to allow U.S. troops to use its bases, but Washington said it can win a war without Turkey's help. \par \par \uc2  Chief U.N. arms inspector Hans Blix is expected to make another report to the U.N. Security council on Friday. \par \par \uc2  CENTRAL BANKS WATCH \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell one percent against both the euro and the Swiss franc on Monday after a survey from the Institute of Supply Management showed U.S. manufacturing activity barely grew in February. Investors remain nervous of holding dollars ahead of Friday's key U.S. jobs report. \par \par \uc2  "A very negative dollar story is building at the moment," said Mitul Kotecha, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  "We are already moving towards the worst case scenario for the dollar which is that the U.S. takes action without broad-based U.N. backing and yesterday's weak data highlighted fears that a war would delay U.S. economic recovery." \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, investors will be focused on meetings by key central banks of Japan, Europe, Britain, Australia, New Zealand, Canada and Norway. Markets are fully priced in at least a quarter point rate cut by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say a rate cut would pressure European fixed income and the euro as it narrows the interest rate differential between the eurozone and the United States. \par \par \uc2  But some said investors would actually reward the European economy by selling the euro in the event of a 50 basis point cut, as a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is what the economy needs in period of weak growth. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN IN AGAIN? \par \par \uc2  Dealers detected Japanese authorities buying dollars for yen at around 117.50/55 yen in Asia, continuing their campaign to curb export-damaging yen strength. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed that it had conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February for the second straight month. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless some question whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent policy of covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be enough to stop the dollar heading back to last week's six-month low below 117. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, investors were focused on speeches by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan at 1330 GMT and U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow at 1500 GMT. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030304dz3400mdr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
