\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Ends Sharply Lower Against Euro and Swiss Franc \b0\par\par\uc2  By Agnes T. Crane \par \uc2 911 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 22:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- A combination of worse-than-expected U.S. data and a test of the Bank of Japan's resolve to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak pummeled the dollar into new territory against  its rivals Monday. \par \par \uc2  During afternoon trading in New York, the dollar nosedived against the yen as a breach of technical-support levels ignited heavy selling, despite speculation earlier that the Bank of Japan would defend the dollar against further weakening. The greenback quickly bounced off its lows for the global session, but failed to move back to safer ground above 117.80 yen. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, lackluster growth in the U.S. manufacturing sector weighed heavily on the dollar, forcing it out of what   had been an ironbound range against the euro and pushing it  to four-year lows against the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, the dollar was at 117.58 yen, up from its 117.19-yen intraday low, but down from 118.08 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.0895, up sharply from  $1.0797 late Friday in New York and well above $1.0850 that  had been the pair's top range for weeks. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3382 francs, down sharply from 1.3522 francs late Friday in New York. Sterling was at $1.5782, compared with $1.5730. \par \par \uc2  Softer U.S. equities also contributed to the dollar's losses. The Dow Jones Industrial Averaged closed at 7837.86,   down 53.22. \par \par \uc2  After declining gradually throughout the global session to the 117.80-yen area, the dollar suddenly went into a tailspin when stop-loss orders were triggered amid heavy selling, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists. The greenback dropped roughly 0.6 yen in seconds to 117.19 yen before rebounding back above 117.50 yen, considered to be a key support area. Traders had believed earlier that the Bank of Japan stood ready to bolster the dollar against the yen if it dipped below 117.80 yen. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan - which acts on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - last month undertook covert \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounting to some $4.5 billion to weaken the yen. Traders were therefore nervous that the Bank of Japan would once again defend key levels between 117.60 yen and 117.80 yen. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in Monday's global session, some traders said they suspected covert Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have been once again at work, serving to prop up the dollar against the yen throughout the New York morning. None had actually seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however. Any waning of such supportive efforts could have left the dollar vulnerable against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which helps to partly explain  the swift slide in the New York afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro and the Swiss franc, the dollar broke into a new range as U.S. economic data failed to convince \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders that the world's largest economy has enough   traction to get back on the recovery track. \par \par \uc2  The Institute for Supply Management reported that U.S. manufacturing activity grew by a hair in February with its index falling to 50.5 from 53.9 in the previous month, only 0.5 above the growth mark of 50. Economists had expected a smaller decline to 52.0. \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets didn't react to the data   immediately, traders soon pushed the dollar lower across the board as U.S. equities eventually felt the sting from the data with the Dow industrial average reversing the majority of its earlier gains. \par \par \uc2  "It's an extremely irritating, extremely delayed reaction" to the manufacturing data, said one trader, commenting on the dollar's softness well after the ISM released its survey. \par \par \uc2  After a slow grind higher, the euro soon popped outside the bounds of its recent range around $1.0850, touching 1.0899 before falling back slightly. Against the Swiss franc, the greenback dropped to its worst levels in more than four year. \par \par \uc2  "It goes back to this morning's numbers," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Investors Bank and Trust, citing the less than inspirational ISM data as well as separate numbers that showed a 0.1% contraction in personal spending in January. \par \par \uc2  "Are people throwing in the towel for a recovery in the second half of the year? ... maybe some people are," he said. \par \par \uc2  Thin market conditions during the afternoon session in New York when the bulk of traders in London call it a day, also contributed to the price action, Mr. Mazanec said, adding that weaker stocks didn't help either. "Overall, there's a lack of momentum" in U.S. equities and subsequently in the dollar to move higher. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The possible U.S.-led war with Iraq had weighed on the dollar earlier\par in the session as traders chewed on over-the-weekend developments,\par including Iraq's destruction of its illegal Al Samoud missiles and a\par surprise legislative  vote in Turkey that blocked U.S. efforts to use\par that nation  as a staging ground for its troops in the case of an\par invasion into Iraq.\par    By Agnes T. Crane; Dow Jones Newswires\par    agnes.crane@dowjones.com; 201-938-2248\par    (John Parry contributed to this report.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Corrected March 3, 2003 19:17 ET (00:17 GMT)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All Rights Reserved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 03-03-03 1649ET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030303dz3300795\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } 